window.TEKS_FULL = {"courses":[{"course":"Anatomy & Physiology","hasStaarEOC":false,"completeness":"Aligned to Texas Anatomy & Physiology, 19 TAC §127.423 (CTE / Health Science, Subchapter I, adopted 2021) — 19 knowledge-and-skills statements in subsection (d). Topic-level scope; verify sub-expectations against the official TAC before official use.","strands":[{"code":"127.423(d)(1)","statement":"Professional standards and employability skills for health-science careers","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(2)","statement":"Scientific and laboratory practices: safe investigation, tools, and measurement","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(3)","statement":"Analyzing and interpreting physiological data to identify patterns","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(4)","statement":"Developing evidence-based explanations and communicating findings","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(5)","statement":"Contributions of scientists and careers that use anatomy and physiology","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(6)","statement":"Human body organization: levels of structure, anatomical terminology, and homeostasis","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(7)","statement":"Histology: cell structure and the four primary tissue types","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(8)","statement":"Skeletal system: bone structure, joints, and related disorders","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(9)","statement":"Integumentary system: skin structure, function, and conditions","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(10)","statement":"Muscular system: muscle types, contraction, and movement","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(11)","statement":"Nervous system: structures, functions, and the senses","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(12)","statement":"Endocrine system: glands, hormones, and regulation","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(13)","statement":"Urinary system: kidney structure and urine formation","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(14)","statement":"Cardiovascular system: heart anatomy, blood, and circulation","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(15)","statement":"Lymphatic and immune system: structures and defense mechanisms","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(16)","statement":"Digestive system: structures and digestive processes","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(17)","statement":"Respiratory system: structures and gas exchange","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(18)","statement":"Reproductive system: organs and development","expectations":[]},{"code":"127.423(d)(19)","statement":"Emerging technologies: bionics, stem cells, and gene therapy","expectations":[]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Orientation, Body Organization & Homeostasis","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(1)","127.423(d)(2)","127.423(d)(5)","127.423(d)(6)"],"items":["Course orientation, lab-safety contract, and health-science professional standards (HOSA, ethics, teamwork, confidentiality)","Careers that use anatomy & physiology and the scientists behind key discoveries","Levels of structural organization: chemical, cell, tissue, organ, organ system, organism","Anatomical position, directional terms, body planes, cavities, and regional terminology","Homeostasis with negative and positive feedback loops (body-temperature and blood-glucose models)","Metric measurement, microscope use, and safe handling of lab tools and instruments"]},{"unit":"Cells & Histology: The Four Tissue Types","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(7)","127.423(d)(2)","127.423(d)(3)"],"items":["Cell structure and organelle functions review","Membrane transport: diffusion, osmosis, and active transport (dialysis/egg lab)","Epithelial tissue types, locations, and functions","Connective, muscle, and nervous tissue identification","Microscopy lab: identify unknown tissue slides and record and analyze observation data"]},{"unit":"Integumentary System","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(9)","127.423(d)(4)"],"items":["Skin structure: epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis layers","Skin appendages: hair, nails, sweat and sebaceous glands","Functions of skin: protection, thermoregulation, sensation, and vitamin D synthesis","Skin conditions, burns (rule of nines), and wound healing","Evidence-based case study: diagnose a skin condition and justify the claim with evidence"]},{"unit":"Skeletal System & Joints","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(8)","127.423(d)(3)"],"items":["Bone functions, gross anatomy of a long bone, and bone tissue (compact vs spongy)","Axial skeleton: skull, vertebral column, and thoracic cage","Appendicular skeleton: pectoral and pelvic girdles and the limbs","Ossification, bone growth and remodeling, and joint types with their movements","Skeletal disorders (osteoporosis, fractures, arthritis) and interpreting bone-density data"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["127.423(d)(1)","127.423(d)(2)","127.423(d)(3)"],"items":["Reteach and remediation of tissue, integumentary, and skeletal concepts","Cumulative lab-skills practical and data-analysis checkpoint","Flex day for calendar shifts, make-up labs, and enrichment extensions"]},{"unit":"Muscular System","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(10)","127.423(d)(4)"],"items":["Muscle types (skeletal, smooth, cardiac) and gross muscle anatomy","Skeletal-muscle microanatomy and the sliding-filament theory of contraction","Major muscles, their actions, and origins/insertions; movement and levers","Muscle-fatigue lab: collect data and write a claim-evidence-reasoning lab report on findings"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Exam (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["127.423(d)(3)","127.423(d)(4)"],"items":["Cumulative review of units 1-6 using stations and a practice practical","Semester exam and lab practical","Data-analysis and scientific-communication reflection"]},{"unit":"Nervous System & Special Senses","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(11)","127.423(d)(3)"],"items":["Neuron structure and the nerve impulse (action potential and synapse)","Central nervous system: brain regions and spinal cord","Peripheral and autonomic nervous systems; reflex-arc lab","Special senses: eye and vision","Special senses: ear (hearing and balance), taste, and smell; interpret reaction-time data"]},{"unit":"Endocrine System","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(12)","127.423(d)(5)"],"items":["Endocrine vs nervous control; hormone action and feedback regulation","Major glands and their hormones (pituitary, thyroid, adrenal, pancreas, gonads)","Blood-glucose regulation and diabetes; Banting & Best insulin discovery and endocrine careers","Endocrine-disorder case studies and interpretation of hormone-level data"]},{"unit":"Cardiovascular System & Blood","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(14)","127.423(d)(3)"],"items":["Blood composition, blood types, and hemostasis","Heart anatomy and the cardiac conduction system","Cardiac cycle, blood pressure, and reading an EKG","Blood vessels and the systemic and pulmonary circulation pathways","Graph and interpret blood-pressure and heart-rate data before and after exercise","Cardiovascular disorders and risk factors"]},{"unit":"Lymphatic & Immune System","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(15)","127.423(d)(4)"],"items":["Lymphatic vessels, nodes, and organs; fluid balance","Innate (nonspecific) defenses","Adaptive immunity: B cells, T cells, and antibodies","Vaccines, immune disorders, and allergies; evaluate evidence and communicate findings"]},{"unit":"Respiratory System","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(17)","127.423(d)(3)"],"items":["Respiratory anatomy: upper and lower airways and the lungs","Mechanics of breathing and lung volumes (spirometry lab)","Gas exchange and transport of oxygen and carbon dioxide","Respiratory disorders; interpret spirometry and vital-capacity data"]},{"unit":"Digestive System & Nutrition","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(16)","127.423(d)(4)"],"items":["Alimentary-canal anatomy and accessory organs","Mechanical and chemical digestion and the role of enzymes","Absorption of nutrients and the roles of the liver and pancreas","Nutrition and metabolism; evidence-based analysis of a diet or nutrition label"]},{"unit":"Urinary System","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(13)","127.423(d)(3)"],"items":["Kidney gross and microscopic anatomy; the nephron","Urine formation: filtration, reabsorption, and secretion","Fluid, electrolyte, and acid-base balance","Urinalysis lab: interpret results and diagnose simulated cases"]},{"unit":"Reproductive System & Development","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(18)","127.423(d)(5)"],"items":["Male reproductive anatomy and spermatogenesis","Female reproductive anatomy, oogenesis, and the menstrual cycle","Fertilization, pregnancy, and the stages of development","Reproductive health, heredity basics, and careers/scientists in reproductive medicine"]},{"unit":"Emerging Technologies in Anatomy & Physiology","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.423(d)(19)","127.423(d)(4)","127.423(d)(5)"],"items":["Bionics, prosthetics, and biomedical engineering","Stem cells and regenerative medicine","Gene therapy and genetic engineering with an ethics discussion","Capstone: write and present an evidence-based (CER) argument on an emerging technology and its careers"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Project/Exam (wiggle)","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["127.423(d)(1)","127.423(d)(3)","127.423(d)(4)"],"items":["Cumulative review of all body systems using integrated case studies","Final capstone: trace a stimulus or nutrient through multiple interacting systems","Comprehensive final exam and lab practical","Portfolio reflection, professional-skills self-assessment, and course wrap-up"]}],"tacSection":"19 TAC §127.423"},{"course":"Algebra I","completeness":"COMPLETE — All 12 knowledge-and-skills strands (A.1–A.12) and every lettered student expectation captured verbatim from 19 TAC §111.39 (adopted 2012). STAAR Readiness/Supporting tags applied to all assessed expectations (strands A.2–A.12) from the TEA STAAR Algebra I Assessed Curriculum (Jan 2014); process standards A.1A–A.1G are intentionally untagged (embedded across reporting categories, no separate designation). Pacing from Fort Bend ISD 2025-2026 (district-representative, not EPISD-official). PACING GAP: A.4A and A.4B are not assigned to any pacing unit — teacher should verify these get covered.","hasStaarEOC":true,"strands":[{"code":"111.39(c)(1)","statement":"Mathematical process standards. The student uses mathematical processes to acquire and demonstrate mathematical understanding. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(1)(A)","text":"apply mathematics to problems arising in everyday life, society, and the workplace;"},{"code":"111.39(c)(1)(B)","text":"use a problem-solving model that incorporates analyzing given information, formulating a plan or strategy, determining a solution, justifying the solution, and evaluating the problem-solving process and the reasonableness of the solution;"},{"code":"111.39(c)(1)(C)","text":"select tools, including real objects, manipulatives, paper and pencil, and technology as appropriate, and techniques, including mental math, estimation, and number sense as appropriate, to solve problems;"},{"code":"111.39(c)(1)(D)","text":"communicate mathematical ideas, reasoning, and their implications using multiple representations, including symbols, diagrams, graphs, and language as appropriate;"},{"code":"111.39(c)(1)(E)","text":"create and use representations to organize, record, and communicate mathematical ideas;"},{"code":"111.39(c)(1)(F)","text":"analyze mathematical relationships to connect and communicate mathematical ideas; and"},{"code":"111.39(c)(1)(G)","text":"display, explain, and justify mathematical ideas and arguments using precise mathematical language in written or oral communication."}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(2)","statement":"Linear functions, equations, and inequalities. The student applies the mathematical process standards when using properties of linear functions to write and represent in multiple ways, with and without technology, linear equations, inequalities, and systems of equations. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 3: Writing and Solving Linear Functions, Equations, and Inequalities]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(2)(A)","text":"determine the domain and range of a linear function in mathematical problems; determine reasonable domain and range values for real-world situations, both continuous and discrete; and represent domain and range using inequalities;","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(2)(B)","text":"write linear equations in two variables in various forms, including y = mx + b, Ax + By = C, and y - y1 = m(x - x1), given one point and the slope and given two points;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(2)(C)","text":"write linear equations in two variables given a table of values, a graph, and a verbal description;","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(2)(D)","text":"write and solve equations involving direct variation;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(2)(E)","text":"write the equation of a line that contains a given point and is parallel to a given line;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(2)(F)","text":"write the equation of a line that contains a given point and is perpendicular to a given line;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(2)(G)","text":"write an equation of a line that is parallel or perpendicular to the X or Y axis and determine whether the slope of the line is zero or undefined;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(2)(H)","text":"write linear inequalities in two variables given a table of values, a graph, and a verbal description; and","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(2)(I)","text":"write systems of two linear equations given a table of values, a graph, and a verbal description.","staar":"Readiness"}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(3)","statement":"Linear functions, equations, and inequalities. The student applies the mathematical process standards when using graphs of linear functions, key features, and related transformations to represent in multiple ways and solve, with and without technology, equations, inequalities, and systems of equations. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 2: Describing and Graphing Linear Functions, Equations, and Inequalities]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(3)(A)","text":"determine the slope of a line given a table of values, a graph, two points on the line, and an equation written in various forms, including y = mx + b, Ax + By = C, and y - y1 = m(x - x1);","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(3)(B)","text":"calculate the rate of change of a linear function represented tabularly, graphically, or algebraically in context of mathematical and real-world problems;","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(3)(C)","text":"graph linear functions on the coordinate plane and identify key features, including x-intercept, y-intercept, zeros, and slope, in mathematical and real-world problems;","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(3)(D)","text":"graph the solution set of linear inequalities in two variables on the coordinate plane;","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(3)(E)","text":"determine the effects on the graph of the parent function f(x) = x when f(x) is replaced by af(x), f(x) + d, f(x - c), f(bx) for specific values of a, b, c, and d;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(3)(F)","text":"graph systems of two linear equations in two variables on the coordinate plane and determine the solutions if they exist;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(3)(G)","text":"estimate graphically the solutions to systems of two linear equations with two variables in real-world problems; and","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(3)(H)","text":"graph the solution set of systems of two linear inequalities in two variables on the coordinate plane.","staar":"Supporting"}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(4)","statement":"Linear functions, equations, and inequalities. The student applies the mathematical process standards to formulate statistical relationships and evaluate their reasonableness based on real-world data. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 2: Describing and Graphing Linear Functions, Equations, and Inequalities]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(4)(A)","text":"calculate, using technology, the correlation coefficient between two quantitative variables and interpret this quantity as a measure of the strength of the linear association;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(4)(B)","text":"compare and contrast association and causation in real-world problems; and","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(4)(C)","text":"write, with and without technology, linear functions that provide a reasonable fit to data to estimate solutions and make predictions for real-world problems.","staar":"Supporting"}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(5)","statement":"Linear functions, equations, and inequalities. The student applies the mathematical process standards to solve, with and without technology, linear equations and evaluate the reasonableness of their solutions. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 3: Writing and Solving Linear Functions, Equations, and Inequalities]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(5)(A)","text":"solve linear equations in one variable, including those for which the application of the distributive property is necessary and for which variables are included on both sides;","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(5)(B)","text":"solve linear inequalities in one variable, including those for which the application of the distributive property is necessary and for which variables are included on both sides; and","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(5)(C)","text":"solve systems of two linear equations with two variables for mathematical and real-world problems.","staar":"Readiness"}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(6)","statement":"Quadratic functions and equations. The student applies the mathematical process standards when using properties of quadratic functions to write and represent in multiple ways, with and without technology, quadratic equations. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 4: Quadratic Functions and Equations]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(6)(A)","text":"determine the domain and range of quadratic functions and represent the domain and range using inequalities;","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(6)(B)","text":"write equations of quadratic functions given the vertex and another point on the graph, write the equation in vertex form (f(x) = a(x - h)2 + k), and rewrite the equation from vertex form to standard form (f(x) = ax2 + bx + c); and","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(6)(C)","text":"write quadratic functions when given real solutions and graphs of their related equations.","staar":"Supporting"}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(7)","statement":"Quadratic functions and equations. The student applies the mathematical process standards when using graphs of quadratic functions and their related transformations to represent in multiple ways and determine, with and without technology, the solutions to equations. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 4: Quadratic Functions and Equations]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(7)(A)","text":"graph quadratic functions on the coordinate plane and use the graph to identify key attributes, if possible, including x-intercept, y-intercept, zeros, maximum value, minimum values, vertex, and the equation of the axis of symmetry;","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(7)(B)","text":"describe the relationship between the linear factors of quadratic expressions and the zeros of their associated quadratic functions; and","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(7)(C)","text":"determine the effects on the graph of the parent function f(x) = x2 when f(x) is replaced by af(x), f(x) + d, f(x - c), f(bx) for specific values of a, b, c, and d.","staar":"Readiness"}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(8)","statement":"Quadratic functions and equations. The student applies the mathematical process standards to solve, with and without technology, quadratic equations and evaluate the reasonableness of their solutions. The student formulates statistical relationships and evaluates their reasonableness based on real-world data. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 4: Quadratic Functions and Equations]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(8)(A)","text":"solve quadratic equations having real solutions by factoring, taking square roots, completing the square, and applying the quadratic formula; and","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(8)(B)","text":"write, using technology, quadratic functions that provide a reasonable fit to data to estimate solutions and make predictions for real-world problems.","staar":"Supporting"}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(9)","statement":"Exponential functions and equations. The student applies the mathematical process standards when using properties of exponential functions and their related transformations to write, graph, and represent in multiple ways exponential equations and evaluate, with and without technology, the reasonableness of their solutions. The student formulates statistical relationships and evaluates their reasonableness based on real-world data. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 5: Exponential Functions and Equations]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(9)(A)","text":"determine the domain and range of exponential functions of the form f(x) = abx and represent the domain and range using inequalities;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(9)(B)","text":"interpret the meaning of the values of a and b in exponential functions of the form f(x) = abx in real-world problems;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(9)(C)","text":"write exponential functions in the form f(x) = abx (where b is a rational number) to describe problems arising from mathematical and real-world situations, including growth and decay;","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(9)(D)","text":"graph exponential functions that model growth and decay and identify key features, including y-intercept and asymptote, in mathematical and real-world problems; and","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(9)(E)","text":"write, using technology, exponential functions that provide a reasonable fit to data and make predictions for real-world problems.","staar":"Supporting"}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(10)","statement":"Number and algebraic methods. The student applies the mathematical process standards and algebraic methods to rewrite in equivalent forms and perform operations on polynomial expressions. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 1: Number and Algebraic Methods]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(10)(A)","text":"add and subtract polynomials of degree one and degree two;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(10)(B)","text":"multiply polynomials of degree one and degree two;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(10)(C)","text":"determine the quotient of a polynomial of degree one and polynomial of degree two when divided by a polynomial of degree one and polynomial of degree two when the degree of the divisor does not exceed the degree of the dividend;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(10)(D)","text":"rewrite polynomial expressions of degree one and degree two in equivalent forms using the distributive property;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(10)(E)","text":"factor, if possible, trinomials with real factors in the form ax2 + bx + c, including perfect square trinomials of degree two; and","staar":"Readiness"},{"code":"111.39(c)(10)(F)","text":"decide if a binomial can be written as the difference of two squares and, if possible, use the structure of a difference of two squares to rewrite the binomial.","staar":"Supporting"}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(11)","statement":"Number and algebraic methods. The student applies the mathematical process standards and algebraic methods to rewrite algebraic expressions into equivalent forms. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 1: Number and Algebraic Methods]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(11)(A)","text":"simplify numerical radical expressions involving square roots; and","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(11)(B)","text":"simplify numeric and algebraic expressions using the laws of exponents, including integral and rational exponents.","staar":"Readiness"}]},{"code":"111.39(c)(12)","statement":"Number and algebraic methods. The student applies the mathematical process standards and algebraic methods to write, solve, analyze, and evaluate equations, relations, and functions. The student is expected to: [STAAR Reporting Category 1: Number and Algebraic Methods]","expectations":[{"code":"111.39(c)(12)(A)","text":"decide whether relations represented verbally, tabularly, graphically, and symbolically define a function;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(12)(B)","text":"evaluate functions, expressed in function notation, given one or more elements in their domains;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(12)(C)","text":"identify terms of arithmetic and geometric sequences when the sequences are given in function form using recursive processes;","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(12)(D)","text":"write a formula for the nth term of arithmetic and geometric sequences, given the value of several of their terms; and","staar":"Supporting"},{"code":"111.39(c)(12)(E)","text":"solve mathematic and scientific formulas, and other literal equations, for a specified variable.","staar":"Supporting"}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Foundations, Functions & Mathematical Processes","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(1)","111.39(c)(12)"],"items":["Problem-solving model & mathematical process standards (tools, multiple representations, justifying reasoning)","Real number sets and evaluating algebraic expressions","Relations vs. functions and the vertical line test","Function notation and evaluating f(x)","Domain and range from graphs, tables, and mappings","Deciding whether a relation is a function in real-world contexts"]},{"unit":"Solving Linear Equations & Inequalities","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(5)","111.39(c)(1)","111.39(c)(12)"],"items":["One-step and multi-step linear equations","Variables on both sides and special-case solutions (none / all reals)","Solving literal equations & formulas for a specified variable","Solving and graphing one-variable linear inequalities","Compound inequalities and interval reasoning","Modeling and solving equations/inequalities from word problems (reasonableness check)"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["111.39(c)(1)","111.39(c)(5)"],"items":["Reteach solving equations/inequalities using error analysis","Spiral review: functions, domain/range, and function notation","Unit 1-2 common assessment","Data-driven small-group intervention and enrichment","Calendar catch-up / flex day"]},{"unit":"Graphing Linear Functions & Key Features","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(3)","111.39(c)(1)"],"items":["Rate of change and slope from tables, graphs, and two points","x- and y-intercepts and interpreting key features in context","Graphing from slope-intercept form y = mx + b","Standard form, intercept graphing, and converting between forms","Transformations of the parent linear function f(x) = x","Domain/range and continuous vs. discrete linear situations"]},{"unit":"Writing Linear Equations","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(2)","111.39(c)(12)"],"items":["Writing equations from a slope and a point (point-slope form)","Writing equations from two points and from tables","Parallel and perpendicular lines","Modeling real-world situations with linear equations","Arithmetic sequences as linear functions (nth-term formula)","Direct variation"]},{"unit":"Systems of Linear Equations & Inequalities","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(2)","111.39(c)(3)","111.39(c)(5)"],"items":["Solving systems by graphing and number of solutions","Solving systems by substitution","Solving systems by elimination","Writing and solving systems from real-world problems","Graphing one- and two-variable linear inequalities","Systems of linear inequalities and feasible regions"]},{"unit":"Linear Regression & Bivariate Data","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(4)","111.39(c)(1)"],"items":["Constructing and interpreting scatterplots","Association, correlation, and describing trends","Writing the line of best fit / linear regression with technology","Correlation coefficient and strength of fit","Making predictions (interpolation/extrapolation) and judging reasonableness"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["111.39(c)(2)","111.39(c)(3)","111.39(c)(5)"],"items":["Cumulative review: linear equations, functions, and systems","Mixed spiral practice with STAAR-style items","Semester exam / district benchmark","Error analysis and targeted reteach"]},{"unit":"Exponents & Polynomial Operations","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(10)"],"items":["Laws of exponents (product, quotient, power, zero, negative)","Scientific notation and operations","Adding and subtracting polynomials","Multiplying polynomials (distribution, box, FOIL)","Special products and dividing a polynomial by a monomial"]},{"unit":"Factoring Polynomials","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(10)","111.39(c)(11)"],"items":["Greatest common factor and factoring by grouping","Factoring trinomials x^2 + bx + c","Factoring trinomials ax^2 + bx + c","Factoring differences of squares and perfect-square trinomials","Rewriting expressions in equivalent factored forms"]},{"unit":"Quadratic Functions — Representations & Graphs","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(6)","111.39(c)(7)"],"items":["The parent quadratic f(x) = x^2 and its key features","Transformations: vertex form and the effects of a, h, k","Graphing from standard form; vertex, axis of symmetry, and intercepts","Domain, range, and interpreting key features in context","Writing quadratic functions from graphs, tables, and roots (standard/vertex/factored forms)"]},{"unit":"Solving Quadratic Equations","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(8)","111.39(c)(6)"],"items":["Solving by factoring and the zero-product property","Solving by square roots and by graphing (x-intercepts as solutions)","Completing the square","The quadratic formula and the discriminant","Modeling with quadratics and judging reasonableness of solutions"]},{"unit":"Exponential Functions","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(9)"],"items":["Geometric sequences and multiplicative patterns","Graphing exponential functions and key features (asymptote, domain/range)","Exponential growth models","Exponential decay models","Writing exponential functions from data/points and evaluating reasonableness"]},{"unit":"STAAR EOC Review","weeks":3,"kind":"review","teks":["111.39(c)(2)","111.39(c)(3)","111.39(c)(4)","111.39(c)(5)","111.39(c)(6)","111.39(c)(7)","111.39(c)(8)","111.39(c)(9)","111.39(c)(10)","111.39(c)(11)"],"items":["RC1 review: number & algebraic methods (exponents, polynomials, factoring)","RC2 review: describing & graphing linear functions","RC3 review: writing & solving linear functions, equations, and inequalities","RC4 review: quadratic functions and equations","RC5 review: exponential functions and equations","Full-length practice EOC with item analysis","Targeted small-group reteach by reporting category"]},{"unit":"Post-EOC: Applied Modeling & Function Analysis","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.39(c)(12)","111.39(c)(1)"],"items":["Comparing linear, quadratic, and exponential models","Arithmetic vs. geometric sequences and nth-term formulas","Rearranging scientific/math formulas for a specified variable","Multi-representation modeling project","Real-world data analysis and presentation"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Exam (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"assessment","teks":["111.39(c)(1)","111.39(c)(12)"],"items":["Cumulative course review stations","Final exam review and study strategies","Comprehensive final exam","Reflection and Algebra II readiness preview"]}],"pacingSource":"CK-12","tacSection":"19 TAC §111.39"},{"course":"Geometry","completeness":"COMPLETE — All 13 knowledge-and-skills strands (G.1–G.13) and every lettered student expectation captured verbatim from 19 TAC §111.41 (adopted 2012). No STAAR EOC for Geometry, so no Readiness/Supporting tags exist. Pacing from Fort Bend ISD 2025-2026 (district-representative, not EPISD-official); 10 units run full content through May. Pacing references G.2C in Unit 2 and covers all strands.","hasStaarEOC":false,"strands":[{"code":"111.41(c)(1)","statement":"Mathematical process standards. The student uses mathematical processes to acquire and demonstrate mathematical understanding. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(1)(A)","text":"apply mathematics to problems arising in everyday life, society, and the workplace;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(1)(B)","text":"use a problem-solving model that incorporates analyzing given information, formulating a plan or strategy, determining a solution, justifying the solution, and evaluating the problem-solving process and the reasonableness of the solution;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(1)(C)","text":"select tools, including real objects, manipulatives, paper and pencil, and technology as appropriate, and techniques, including mental math, estimation, and number sense as appropriate, to solve problems;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(1)(D)","text":"communicate mathematical ideas, reasoning, and their implications using multiple representations, including symbols, diagrams, graphs, and language as appropriate;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(1)(E)","text":"create and use representations to organize, record, and communicate mathematical ideas;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(1)(F)","text":"analyze mathematical relationships to connect and communicate mathematical ideas; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(1)(G)","text":"display, explain, and justify mathematical ideas and arguments using precise mathematical language in written or oral communication."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(2)","statement":"Coordinate and transformational geometry. The student uses the process skills to understand the connections between algebra and geometry and uses the one- and two-dimensional coordinate systems to verify geometric conjectures. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(2)(A)","text":"determine the coordinates of a point that is a given fractional distance less than one from one end of a line segment to the other in one- and two-dimensional coordinate systems, including finding the midpoint;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(2)(B)","text":"derive and use the distance, slope, and midpoint formulas to verify geometric relationships, including congruence of segments and parallelism or perpendicularity of pairs of lines; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(2)(C)","text":"determine an equation of a line parallel or perpendicular to a given line that passes through a given point."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(3)","statement":"Coordinate and transformational geometry. The student uses the process skills to generate and describe rigid transformations (translation, reflection, and rotation) and non-rigid transformations (dilations that preserve similarity and reductions and enlargements that do not preserve similarity). The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(3)(A)","text":"describe and perform transformations of figures in a plane using coordinate notation;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(3)(B)","text":"determine the image or pre-image of a given two-dimensional figure under a composition of rigid transformations, a composition of non-rigid transformations, and a composition of both, including dilations where the center can be any point in the plane;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(3)(C)","text":"identify the sequence of transformations that will carry a given pre-image onto an image on and off the coordinate plane; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(3)(D)","text":"identify and distinguish between reflectional and rotational symmetry in a plane figure."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(4)","statement":"Logical argument and constructions. The student uses the process skills with deductive reasoning to understand geometric relationships. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(4)(A)","text":"distinguish between undefined terms, definitions, postulates, conjectures, and theorems;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(4)(B)","text":"identify and determine the validity of the converse, inverse, and contrapositive of a conditional statement and recognize the connection between a biconditional statement and a true conditional statement with a true converse;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(4)(C)","text":"verify that a conjecture is false using a counterexample; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(4)(D)","text":"compare geometric relationships between Euclidean and spherical geometries, including parallel lines and the sum of the angles in a triangle."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(5)","statement":"Logical argument and constructions. The student uses constructions to validate conjectures about geometric figures. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(5)(A)","text":"investigate patterns to make conjectures about geometric relationships, including angles formed by parallel lines cut by a transversal, criteria required for triangle congruence, special segments of triangles, diagonals of quadrilaterals, interior and exterior angles of polygons, and special segments and angles of circles choosing from a variety of tools;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(5)(B)","text":"construct congruent segments, congruent angles, a segment bisector, an angle bisector, perpendicular lines, the perpendicular bisector of a line segment, and a line parallel to a given line through a point not on a line using a compass and a straightedge;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(5)(C)","text":"use the constructions of congruent segments, congruent angles, angle bisectors, and perpendicular bisectors to make conjectures about geometric relationships; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(5)(D)","text":"verify the Triangle Inequality theorem using constructions and apply the theorem to solve problems."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(6)","statement":"Proof and congruence. The student uses the process skills with deductive reasoning to prove and apply theorems by using a variety of methods such as coordinate, transformational, and axiomatic and formats such as two-column, paragraph, and flow chart. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(6)(A)","text":"verify theorems about angles formed by the intersection of lines and line segments, including vertical angles, and angles formed by parallel lines cut by a transversal and prove equidistance between the endpoints of a segment and points on its perpendicular bisector and apply these relationships to solve problems;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(6)(B)","text":"prove two triangles are congruent by applying the Side-Angle-Side, Angle-Side-Angle, Side-Side-Side, Angle-Angle-Side, and Hypotenuse-Leg congruence conditions;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(6)(C)","text":"apply the definition of congruence, in terms of rigid transformations, to identify congruent figures and their corresponding sides and angles;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(6)(D)","text":"verify theorems about the relationships in triangles, including proof of the Pythagorean Theorem, the sum of interior angles, base angles of isosceles triangles, midsegments, and medians, and apply these relationships to solve problems; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(6)(E)","text":"prove a quadrilateral is a parallelogram, rectangle, square, or rhombus using opposite sides, opposite angles, or diagonals and apply these relationships to solve problems."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(7)","statement":"Similarity, proof, and trigonometry. The student uses the process skills in applying similarity to solve problems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(7)(A)","text":"apply the definition of similarity in terms of a dilation to identify similar figures and their proportional sides and the congruent corresponding angles; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(7)(B)","text":"apply the Angle-Angle criterion to verify similar triangles and apply the proportionality of the corresponding sides to solve problems."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(8)","statement":"Similarity, proof, and trigonometry. The student uses the process skills with deductive reasoning to prove and apply theorems by using a variety of methods such as coordinate, transformational, and axiomatic and formats such as two-column, paragraph, and flow chart. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(8)(A)","text":"prove theorems about similar triangles, including the Triangle Proportionality theorem, and apply these theorems to solve problems; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(8)(B)","text":"identify and apply the relationships that exist when an altitude is drawn to the hypotenuse of a right triangle, including the geometric mean, to solve problems."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(9)","statement":"Similarity, proof, and trigonometry. The student uses the process skills to understand and apply relationships in right triangles. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(9)(A)","text":"determine the lengths of sides and measures of angles in a right triangle by applying the trigonometric ratios sine, cosine, and tangent to solve problems; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(9)(B)","text":"apply the relationships in special right triangles 30°-60°-90° and 45°-45°-90° and the Pythagorean theorem, including Pythagorean triples, to solve problems."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(10)","statement":"Two-dimensional and three-dimensional figures. The student uses the process skills to recognize characteristics and dimensional changes of two- and three-dimensional figures. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(10)(A)","text":"identify the shapes of two-dimensional cross-sections of prisms, pyramids, cylinders, cones, and spheres and identify three-dimensional objects generated by rotations of two-dimensional shapes; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(10)(B)","text":"determine and describe how changes in the linear dimensions of a shape affect its perimeter, area, surface area, or volume, including proportional and non-proportional dimensional change."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(11)","statement":"Two-dimensional and three-dimensional figures. The student uses the process skills in the application of formulas to determine measures of two- and three-dimensional figures. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(11)(A)","text":"apply the formula for the area of regular polygons to solve problems using appropriate units of measure;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(11)(B)","text":"determine the area of composite two-dimensional figures comprised of a combination of triangles, parallelograms, trapezoids, kites, regular polygons, or sectors of circles to solve problems using appropriate units of measure;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(11)(C)","text":"apply the formulas for the total and lateral surface area of three-dimensional figures, including prisms, pyramids, cones, cylinders, spheres, and composite figures, to solve problems using appropriate units of measure; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(11)(D)","text":"apply the formulas for the volume of three-dimensional figures, including prisms, pyramids, cones, cylinders, spheres, and composite figures, to solve problems using appropriate units of measure."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(12)","statement":"Circles. The student uses the process skills to understand geometric relationships and apply theorems and equations about circles. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(12)(A)","text":"apply theorems about circles, including relationships among angles, radii, chords, tangents, and secants, to solve non-contextual problems;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(12)(B)","text":"apply the proportional relationship between the measure of an arc length of a circle and the circumference of the circle to solve problems;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(12)(C)","text":"apply the proportional relationship between the measure of the area of a sector of a circle and the area of the circle to solve problems;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(12)(D)","text":"describe radian measure of an angle as the ratio of the length of an arc intercepted by a central angle and the radius of the circle; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(12)(E)","text":"show that the equation of a circle with center at the origin and radius r is x2 + y2 = r2 and determine the equation for the graph of a circle with radius r and center (h, k), (x - h)2 + (y - k)2 = r2."}]},{"code":"111.41(c)(13)","statement":"Probability. The student uses the process skills to understand probability in real-world situations and how to apply independence and dependence of events. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.41(c)(13)(A)","text":"develop strategies to use permutations and combinations to solve contextual problems;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(13)(B)","text":"determine probabilities based on area to solve contextual problems;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(13)(C)","text":"identify whether two events are independent and compute the probability of the two events occurring together with or without replacement;"},{"code":"111.41(c)(13)(D)","text":"apply conditional probability in contextual problems; and"},{"code":"111.41(c)(13)(E)","text":"apply independence in contextual problems."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Foundations & Tools of Geometry","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(1)","111.41(c)(2)"],"items":["Points, lines, planes; defined vs. undefined terms and naming figures","Segment addition postulate; measuring and comparing lengths","Distance and midpoint formulas on the coordinate plane","Angles, angle addition postulate, and classifying angle pairs","Perimeter and area of figures on the coordinate plane","Math process standards: tools, problem-solving, and precise communication"]},{"unit":"Reasoning, Logic & Introduction to Proof","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(1)","111.41(c)(4)"],"items":["Inductive reasoning, conjectures, and counterexamples","Conditional statements: converse, inverse, contrapositive, biconditionals","Deductive reasoning; Law of Detachment and Law of Syllogism","Algebraic proofs and properties of equality and congruence","Two-column proofs of vertical, complementary, and supplementary angles"]},{"unit":"Geometric Constructions","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(5)"],"items":["Copying a segment and copying an angle with compass and straightedge","Constructing segment bisectors and angle bisectors","Constructing perpendicular and parallel lines","Constructing an equilateral triangle and a regular hexagon in a circle","Justifying constructions to validate geometric conjectures"]},{"unit":"Parallel & Perpendicular Lines","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(2)","111.41(c)(4)","111.41(c)(6)"],"items":["Angle pairs formed by parallel lines cut by a transversal","Proving lines parallel using converse theorems","Slope criteria for parallel and perpendicular lines","Writing equations of parallel and perpendicular lines","Perpendicular distance from a point to a line"]},{"unit":"Transformations & Symmetry","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(2)","111.41(c)(3)"],"items":["Translations on the coordinate plane using vectors and rules","Reflections across lines and lines of symmetry","Rotations about a point and rotational symmetry","Compositions of rigid transformations and congruence","Dilations: scale factor and center of dilation","Rigid motions (congruence) vs. non-rigid dilations (similarity)"]},{"unit":"Congruent Triangles & Triangle Proof","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(1)","111.41(c)(6)"],"items":["Triangle angle sum and exterior angle theorems","Congruence criteria: SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, and HL","Two-column and flow-chart proofs of triangle congruence","CPCTC and applications to segments and angles","Coordinate and transformational proofs of congruence","Isosceles and equilateral triangle theorems"]},{"unit":"Relationships within Triangles","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(5)","111.41(c)(6)"],"items":["Perpendicular bisectors and the circumcenter","Angle bisectors and the incenter","Medians and centroid; altitudes and orthocenter","Triangle midsegment theorem","Triangle inequality and side-angle relationships"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester 1 Checkpoint (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["111.41(c)(1)","111.41(c)(6)"],"items":["Cumulative review of proof, congruence, and constructions","Targeted reteach of two-column and coordinate proofs","Mixed spiral practice across Units 1-7","Semester 1 exam and error analysis","Data-driven reteach and enrichment stations"]},{"unit":"Similarity","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(7)","111.41(c)(8)"],"items":["Ratios, proportions, and scale factor","Similar polygons and writing similarity statements","AA, SSS, and SAS similarity criteria and proofs","Triangle Proportionality Theorem and its converse","Proportional segments: midsegments, parallel lines, angle bisectors","Applications: indirect measurement and scale drawings"]},{"unit":"Right Triangles & Trigonometry","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(9)"],"items":["Pythagorean Theorem and its converse","Special right triangles (45-45-90 and 30-60-90)","Geometric mean and altitude-on-hypotenuse relationships","Sine, cosine, and tangent ratios","Solving right triangles with inverse trig ratios","Angles of elevation and depression applications"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["111.41(c)(7)","111.41(c)(9)"],"items":["Flex days to absorb calendar shifts and reteach similarity and trig","Spiral review of proportions and right-triangle solving","Common-error clinics and small-group intervention","Unit assessment with retake opportunities","Enrichment: real-world trigonometry modeling project"]},{"unit":"Quadrilaterals & Polygons","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(6)","111.41(c)(10)"],"items":["Interior and exterior angle sums of polygons","Properties and proofs of parallelograms","Special parallelograms: rectangles, rhombi, and squares","Trapezoids and kites","Coordinate proofs classifying quadrilaterals"]},{"unit":"Area & Perimeter of 2D Figures","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(10)","111.41(c)(11)"],"items":["Area and perimeter of triangles and quadrilaterals","Area of regular polygons using the apothem","Composite figures and shaded-region problems","Effects of proportional dimension change on perimeter and area","Area of polygons on the coordinate plane"]},{"unit":"Surface Area & Volume of 3D Figures","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(10)","111.41(c)(11)"],"items":["Nets, cross-sections, and rotating 2D figures into solids","Surface area and volume of prisms and cylinders","Surface area and volume of pyramids and cones","Surface area and volume of spheres","Dimensional-change effects on volume; density and modeling"]},{"unit":"Circles","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(12)"],"items":["Central angles, arcs, and arc measures","Inscribed angles and inscribed polygon theorems","Chords, secants, tangents, and segment/angle relationships","Arc length and sector area","Equation of a circle in standard form via completing the square"]},{"unit":"Probability","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.41(c)(13)"],"items":["Sample spaces; experimental vs. theoretical probability","Independent and dependent events and the multiplication rule","Conditional probability and two-way frequency tables","Permutations, combinations, and the fundamental counting principle","Geometric probability with length and area models"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Exam (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"assessment","teks":["111.41(c)(1)","111.41(c)(13)"],"items":["Cumulative spiral review across all strands","Circles and probability reinforcement","Mixed released-item practice and test-taking strategy","Comprehensive final exam","Final reflection and error analysis"]}],"pacingSource":"CK-12","tacSection":"19 TAC §111.41"},{"course":"Algebra II","completeness":"COMPLETE — All 8 knowledge-and-skills strands (2A.1–2A.8) and every lettered student expectation captured verbatim from 19 TAC §111.40 (adopted 2012). No STAAR EOC for Algebra II, so no Readiness/Supporting tags exist. Pacing from Fort Bend ISD 2025-2026 (district-representative, not EPISD-official); 9 units run the full year. NOTE: pacing assigns 2A.7A (write quadratic given three points) to Unit 3 although that SE lives in strand 2A.4 in the TEKS — likely a district scope-and-sequence label; treat as a coverage note, not a TEKS error.","hasStaarEOC":false,"strands":[{"code":"111.40(c)(1)","statement":"Mathematical process standards. The student uses mathematical processes to acquire and demonstrate mathematical understanding. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.40(c)(1)(A)","text":"apply mathematics to problems arising in everyday life, society, and the workplace;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(1)(B)","text":"use a problem-solving model that incorporates analyzing given information, formulating a plan or strategy, determining a solution, justifying the solution, and evaluating the problem-solving process and the reasonableness of the solution;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(1)(C)","text":"select tools, including real objects, manipulatives, paper and pencil, and technology as appropriate, and techniques, including mental math, estimation, and number sense as appropriate, to solve problems;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(1)(D)","text":"communicate mathematical ideas, reasoning, and their implications using multiple representations, including symbols, diagrams, graphs, and language as appropriate;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(1)(E)","text":"create and use representations to organize, record, and communicate mathematical ideas;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(1)(F)","text":"analyze mathematical relationships to connect and communicate mathematical ideas; and"},{"code":"111.40(c)(1)(G)","text":"display, explain, or justify mathematical ideas and arguments using precise mathematical language in written or oral communication."}]},{"code":"111.40(c)(2)","statement":"Attributes of functions and their inverses. The student applies mathematical processes to understand that functions have distinct key attributes and understand the relationship between a function and its inverse. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.40(c)(2)(A)","text":"graph the functions f(x)=√x, f(x)=1/x, f(x)=x^3, f(x)=∛x, f(x)=b^x, f(x)=|x|, and f(x)=log_b(x) where b is 2, 10, and e, and, when applicable, analyze the key attributes such as domain, range, intercepts, symmetries, asymptotic behavior, and maximum and minimum given an interval;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(2)(B)","text":"graph and write the inverse of a function using notation such as f^-1(x);"},{"code":"111.40(c)(2)(C)","text":"describe and analyze the relationship between a function and its inverse (quadratic and square root, logarithmic and exponential), including the restriction(s) on domain, which will restrict its range; and"},{"code":"111.40(c)(2)(D)","text":"use the composition of two functions, including the necessary restrictions on the domain, to determine if the functions are inverses of each other."}]},{"code":"111.40(c)(3)","statement":"Systems of equations and inequalities. The student applies mathematical processes to formulate systems of equations and inequalities, use a variety of methods to solve, and analyze reasonableness of solutions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.40(c)(3)(A)","text":"formulate systems of equations, including systems consisting of three linear equations in three variables and systems consisting of two equations, the first linear and the second quadratic;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(3)(B)","text":"solve systems of three linear equations in three variables by using Gaussian elimination, technology with matrices, and substitution;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(3)(C)","text":"solve, algebraically, systems of two equations in two variables consisting of a linear equation and a quadratic equation;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(3)(D)","text":"determine the reasonableness of solutions to systems of a linear equation and a quadratic equation in two variables;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(3)(E)","text":"formulate systems of at least two linear inequalities in two variables;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(3)(F)","text":"solve systems of two or more linear inequalities in two variables; and"},{"code":"111.40(c)(3)(G)","text":"determine possible solutions in the solution set of systems of two or more linear inequalities in two variables."}]},{"code":"111.40(c)(4)","statement":"Quadratic and square root functions, equations, and inequalities. The student applies mathematical processes to understand that quadratic and square root functions, equations, and quadratic inequalities can be used to model situations, solve problems, and make predictions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.40(c)(4)(A)","text":"write the quadratic function given three specified points in the plane;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(4)(B)","text":"write the equation of a parabola using given attributes, including vertex, focus, directrix, axis of symmetry, and direction of opening;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(4)(C)","text":"determine the effect on the graph of f(x) = √x when f(x) is replaced by af(x), f(x) + d, f(bx), and f(x - c) for specific positive and negative values of a, b, c, and d;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(4)(D)","text":"transform a quadratic function f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c to the form f(x) = a(x - h)^2 + k to identify the different attributes of f(x);"},{"code":"111.40(c)(4)(E)","text":"formulate quadratic and square root equations using technology given a table of data;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(4)(F)","text":"solve quadratic and square root equations;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(4)(G)","text":"identify extraneous solutions of square root equations; and"},{"code":"111.40(c)(4)(H)","text":"solve quadratic inequalities."}]},{"code":"111.40(c)(5)","statement":"Exponential and logarithmic functions and equations. The student applies mathematical processes to understand that exponential and logarithmic functions can be used to model situations and solve problems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.40(c)(5)(A)","text":"determine the effects on the key attributes on the graphs of f(x) = b^x and f(x) = log_b(x) where b is 2, 10, and e when f(x) is replaced by af(x), f(x) + d, and f(x - c) for specific positive and negative real values of a, c, and d;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(5)(B)","text":"formulate exponential and logarithmic equations that model real-world situations, including exponential relationships written in recursive notation;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(5)(C)","text":"rewrite exponential equations as their corresponding logarithmic equations and logarithmic equations as their corresponding exponential equations;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(5)(D)","text":"solve exponential equations of the form y = ab^x where a is a nonzero real number and b is greater than zero and not equal to one and single logarithmic equations having real solutions; and"},{"code":"111.40(c)(5)(E)","text":"determine the reasonableness of a solution to a logarithmic equation."}]},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)","statement":"Cubic, cube root, absolute value and rational functions, equations, and inequalities. The student applies mathematical processes to understand that cubic, cube root, absolute value and rational functions, equations, and inequalities can be used to model situations, solve problems, and make predictions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(A)","text":"analyze the effect on the graphs of f(x) = x^3 and f(x) = ∛x when f(x) is replaced by af(x), f(bx), f(x - c), and f(x) + d for specific positive and negative real values of a, b, c, and d;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(B)","text":"solve cube root equations that have real roots;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(C)","text":"analyze the effect on the graphs of f(x) = |x| when f(x) is replaced by af(x), f(bx), f(x-c), and f(x) + d for specific positive and negative real values of a, b, c, and d;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(D)","text":"formulate absolute value linear equations;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(E)","text":"solve absolute value linear equations;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(F)","text":"solve absolute value linear inequalities;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(G)","text":"analyze the effect on the graphs of f(x) = 1/x when f(x) is replaced by af(x), f(bx), f(x-c), and f(x) + d for specific positive and negative real values of a, b, c, and d;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(H)","text":"formulate rational equations that model real-world situations;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(I)","text":"solve rational equations that have real solutions;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(J)","text":"determine the reasonableness of a solution to a rational equation;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(K)","text":"determine the asymptotic restrictions on the domain of a rational function and represent domain and range using interval notation, inequalities, and set notation; and"},{"code":"111.40(c)(6)(L)","text":"formulate and solve equations involving inverse variation."}]},{"code":"111.40(c)(7)","statement":"Number and algebraic methods. The student applies mathematical processes to simplify and perform operations on expressions and to solve equations. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.40(c)(7)(A)","text":"add, subtract, and multiply complex numbers;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(7)(B)","text":"add, subtract, and multiply polynomials;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(7)(C)","text":"determine the quotient of a polynomial of degree three and of degree four when divided by a polynomial of degree one and of degree two;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(7)(D)","text":"determine the linear factors of a polynomial function of degree three and of degree four using algebraic methods;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(7)(E)","text":"determine linear and quadratic factors of a polynomial expression of degree three and of degree four, including factoring the sum and difference of two cubes and factoring by grouping;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(7)(F)","text":"determine the sum, difference, product, and quotient of rational expressions with integral exponents of degree one and of degree two;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(7)(G)","text":"rewrite radical expressions that contain variables to equivalent forms;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(7)(H)","text":"solve equations involving rational exponents; and"},{"code":"111.40(c)(7)(I)","text":"write the domain and range of a function in interval notation, inequalities, and set notation."}]},{"code":"111.40(c)(8)","statement":"Data. The student applies mathematical processes to analyze data, select appropriate models, write corresponding functions, and make predictions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.40(c)(8)(A)","text":"analyze data to select the appropriate model from among linear, quadratic, and exponential models;"},{"code":"111.40(c)(8)(B)","text":"use regression methods available through technology to write a linear function, a quadratic function, and an exponential function from a given set of data; and"},{"code":"111.40(c)(8)(C)","text":"predict and make decisions and critical judgments from a given set of data using linear, quadratic, and exponential models."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Polynomials","weeks":8.5,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Rational and Radical Equations","weeks":4.5,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Exponential and Logarithmic Functions","weeks":6.5,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Trigonometry","weeks":5,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Statistics","weeks":5,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Advanced Statistics Supplement","weeks":5,"teks":[]}],"pacingSource":"CK-12","tacSection":"19 TAC §111.40"},{"course":"Precalculus","completeness":"COMPLETE — All 5 knowledge-and-skills strands (P.1–P.5) and every lettered student expectation captured verbatim from 19 TAC §111.42 (adopted 2012). No STAAR EOC for Precalculus, so no Readiness/Supporting tags exist. Pacing from Fort Bend ISD 2025-2026 (district-representative, not EPISD-official); 10 units. PACING GAP: P.5E (nth term / partial sum / infinite sum of a geometric series) is not assigned to any pacing unit — Unit 10 covers P.5A,B,C,D,F but omits P.5E; teacher should verify it is taught. (Source flagged minor OCR artifacts in the overview's success-criteria narrative, but the per-unit TEKS code lists are intact.)","hasStaarEOC":false,"strands":[{"code":"111.42(c)(1)","statement":"Mathematical process standards. The student uses mathematical processes to acquire and demonstrate mathematical understanding. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.42(c)(1)(A)","text":"apply mathematics to problems arising in everyday life, society, and the workplace;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(1)(B)","text":"use a problem-solving model that incorporates analyzing given information, formulating a plan or strategy, determining a solution, justifying the solution, and evaluating the problem-solving process and the reasonableness of the solution;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(1)(C)","text":"select tools, including real objects, manipulatives, paper and pencil, and technology as appropriate, and techniques, including mental math, estimation, and number sense as appropriate, to solve problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(1)(D)","text":"communicate mathematical ideas, reasoning, and their implications using multiple representations, including symbols, diagrams, graphs, and language as appropriate;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(1)(E)","text":"create and use representations to organize, record, and communicate mathematical ideas;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(1)(F)","text":"analyze mathematical relationships to connect and communicate mathematical ideas; and"},{"code":"111.42(c)(1)(G)","text":"display, explain, and justify mathematical ideas and arguments using precise mathematical language in written or oral communication."}]},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)","statement":"Functions. The student uses process standards in mathematics to explore, describe, and analyze the attributes of functions. The student makes connections between multiple representations of functions and algebraically constructs new functions. The student analyzes and uses functions to model real-world problems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(A)","text":"use the composition of two functions to model and solve real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(B)","text":"demonstrate that function composition is not always commutative;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(C)","text":"represent a given function as a composite function of two or more functions;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(D)","text":"describe symmetry of graphs of even and odd functions;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(E)","text":"determine an inverse function, when it exists, for a given function over its domain or a subset of its domain and represent the inverse using multiple representations;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(F)","text":"graph exponential, logarithmic, rational, polynomial, power, trigonometric, inverse trigonometric, and piecewise defined functions, including step functions;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(G)","text":"graph functions, including exponential, logarithmic, sine, cosine, rational, polynomial, and power functions and their transformations, including af(x), f(x) + d, f(x - c), f(bx) for specific values of a, b, c, and d, in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(H)","text":"graph arcsin x and arccos x and describe the limitations on the domain;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(I)","text":"determine and analyze the key features of exponential, logarithmic, rational, polynomial, power, trigonometric, inverse trigonometric, and piecewise defined functions, including step functions such as domain, range, symmetry, relative maximum, relative minimum, zeros, asymptotes, and intervals over which the function is increasing or decreasing;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(J)","text":"analyze and describe end behavior of functions, including exponential, logarithmic, rational, polynomial, and power functions, using infinity notation to communicate this characteristic in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(K)","text":"analyze characteristics of rational functions and the behavior of the function around the asymptotes, including horizontal, vertical, and oblique asymptotes;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(L)","text":"determine various types of discontinuities in the interval (-∞, ∞) as they relate to functions and explore the limitations of the graphing calculator as it relates to the behavior of the function around discontinuities;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(M)","text":"describe the left-sided behavior and the right-sided behavior of the graph of a function around discontinuities;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(N)","text":"analyze situations modeled by functions, including exponential, logarithmic, rational, polynomial, and power functions, to solve real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(O)","text":"develop and use a sinusoidal function that models a situation in mathematical and real-world problems; and"},{"code":"111.42(c)(2)(P)","text":"determine the values of the trigonometric functions at the special angles and relate them in mathematical and real-world problems."}]},{"code":"111.42(c)(3)","statement":"Relations and geometric reasoning. The student uses the process standards in mathematics to model and make connections between algebraic and geometric relations. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.42(c)(3)(A)","text":"graph a set of parametric equations;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(3)(B)","text":"convert parametric equations into rectangular relations and convert rectangular relations into parametric equations;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(3)(C)","text":"use parametric equations to model and solve mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(3)(D)","text":"graph points in the polar coordinate system and convert between rectangular coordinates and polar coordinates;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(3)(E)","text":"graph polar equations by plotting points and using technology;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(3)(F)","text":"determine the conic section formed when a plane intersects a double-napped cone;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(3)(G)","text":"make connections between the locus definition of conic sections and their equations in rectangular coordinates;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(3)(H)","text":"use the characteristics of an ellipse to write the equation of an ellipse with center (h, k); and"},{"code":"111.42(c)(3)(I)","text":"use the characteristics of a hyperbola to write the equation of a hyperbola with center (h, k)."}]},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)","statement":"Number and measure. The student uses process standards in mathematics to apply appropriate techniques, tools, and formulas to calculate measures in mathematical and real-world problems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(A)","text":"determine the relationship between the unit circle and the definition of a periodic function to evaluate trigonometric functions in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(B)","text":"describe the relationship between degree and radian measure on the unit circle;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(C)","text":"represent angles in radians or degrees based on the concept of rotation and find the measure of reference angles and angles in standard position;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(D)","text":"represent angles in radians or degrees based on the concept of rotation in mathematical and real-world problems, including linear and angular velocity;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(E)","text":"determine the value of trigonometric ratios of angles and solve problems involving trigonometric ratios in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(F)","text":"use trigonometry in mathematical and real-world problems, including directional bearing;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(G)","text":"use the Law of Sines in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(H)","text":"use the Law of Cosines in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(I)","text":"use vectors to model situations involving magnitude and direction;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(J)","text":"represent the addition of vectors and the multiplication of a vector by a scalar geometrically and symbolically; and"},{"code":"111.42(c)(4)(K)","text":"apply vector addition and multiplication of a vector by a scalar in mathematical and real-world problems."}]},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)","statement":"Algebraic reasoning. The student uses process standards in mathematics to evaluate expressions, describe patterns, formulate models, and solve equations and inequalities using properties, procedures, or algorithms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(A)","text":"evaluate finite sums and geometric series, when possible, written in sigma notation;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(B)","text":"represent arithmetic sequences and geometric sequences using recursive formulas;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(C)","text":"calculate the nth term and the nth partial sum of an arithmetic series in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(D)","text":"represent arithmetic series and geometric series using sigma notation;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(E)","text":"calculate the nth term of a geometric series, the nth partial sum of a geometric series, and sum of an infinite geometric series when it exists;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(F)","text":"apply the Binomial Theorem for the expansion of (a + b)^n in powers of a and b for a positive integer n, where a and b are any numbers;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(G)","text":"use the properties of logarithms to evaluate or transform logarithmic expressions;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(H)","text":"generate and solve logarithmic equations in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(I)","text":"generate and solve exponential equations in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(J)","text":"solve polynomial equations with real coefficients by applying a variety of techniques in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(K)","text":"solve polynomial inequalities with real coefficients by applying a variety of techniques and write the solution set of the polynomial inequality in interval notation in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(L)","text":"solve rational inequalities with real coefficients by applying a variety of techniques and write the solution set of the rational inequality in interval notation in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(M)","text":"use trigonometric identities such as reciprocal, quotient, Pythagorean, cofunctions, even/odd, and sum and difference identities for cosine and sine to simplify trigonometric expressions; and"},{"code":"111.42(c)(5)(N)","text":"generate and solve trigonometric equations in mathematical and real-world problems."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Unit 1: Fundamental Skills","weeks":2,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 2: Functions and Their Graphs","weeks":3,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 3: Polynomial and Rational Functions","weeks":3.5,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 4: Exponential & Logarithmic Functions","weeks":3,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 5: Trigonometric Functions","weeks":4,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 6: Trigonometric Identities & Equations","weeks":3,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 7: Polar and Parametric Equations","weeks":2.5,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 8: Vectors","weeks":2,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 9: Conic Sections","weeks":3,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 10: Systems of Equations and Matrices","weeks":2.5,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 11: Sequences and Series","weeks":2,"teks":[]},{"unit":"Unit 12: Introduction to Calculus","weeks":2.5,"teks":[]}],"pacingSource":"All Things Algebra (owned)","tacSection":"19 TAC §111.42"},{"course":"Principles of Applied Engineering","completeness":"COMPLETE (TEKS) — All 11 knowledge-and-skills strands (d)(1)–(d)(11) and every lettered student expectation captured verbatim from 19 TAC §127.781 (One Credit, adopted 2021, eff. 2022; STEM cluster). This is the current law; the older §130.402 citation was repealed/relocated. NO PACING / SEQUENCE was provided for this course — sequence omitted; teacher must build or source a scope-and-sequence. NO STAAR (CTE course). Strand codes normalized to PAE.1–PAE.11 and expectations to PAE.1A etc. for consistency with the math courses.","hasStaarEOC":false,"strands":[{"code":"127.781(d)(1)","statement":"(d)(1) The student demonstrates professional standards/employability skills as required by business and industry. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(1)(A)","text":"demonstrate knowledge of how to dress, speak, and conduct oneself in a manner appropriate for the profession;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(1)(B)","text":"cooperate, contribute, and collaborate as a member of a group in an effort to achieve a positive collective outcome;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(1)(C)","text":"present written and oral communication in a clear, concise, and effective manner;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(1)(D)","text":"demonstrate time-management skills in prioritizing tasks, following schedules, and performing goal-relevant activities in a way that produces efficient results; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(1)(E)","text":"demonstrate punctuality, dependability, reliability, and responsibility in performing assigned tasks."}]},{"code":"127.781(d)(2)","statement":"(d)(2) The student investigates the components of engineering and technology systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(2)(A)","text":"investigate and report on the history of engineering disciplines, including chemical, civil, electrical, and mechanical engineering;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(2)(B)","text":"identify the inputs, processes, and outputs associated with technological systems;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(2)(C)","text":"describe the difference between open and closed systems;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(2)(D)","text":"describe how technological systems interact to achieve common goals;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(2)(E)","text":"compare engineering, science, and technology career paths, including entry-level employment, military service, apprenticeships, community and technical colleges, and universities;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(2)(F)","text":"conduct and present research on emerging and innovative technology; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(2)(G)","text":"demonstrate proficiency of the engineering design process."}]},{"code":"127.781(d)(3)","statement":"(d)(3) The student presents conclusions, research findings, and designs using a variety of media throughout the course. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(3)(A)","text":"use clear and concise written, verbal, and visual communication techniques;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(3)(B)","text":"maintain a design and computation engineering notebook;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(3)(C)","text":"develop and present ideas using sketching and computer-aided design and drafting (CADD);"},{"code":"127.781(d)(3)(D)","text":"draw conclusions using industry-standard visualization techniques and media;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(3)(E)","text":"maintain a paper or digital portfolio using the engineering documentation process; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(3)(F)","text":"use collaborative tools such as desktop or web-based applications to share and develop information."}]},{"code":"127.781(d)(4)","statement":"(d)(4) The student uses appropriate tools and demonstrates safe work habits. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(4)(A)","text":"master relevant safety tests;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(4)(B)","text":"follow lab safety guidelines as prescribed by instructor in compliance with local, state, and federal regulations;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(4)(C)","text":"identify industry safety terminology related to the personal work environment such as Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA), American Society of Mechanical Engineers (ASME), and personal protective equipment (PPE);"},{"code":"127.781(d)(4)(D)","text":"recognize the classification of hazardous materials and wastes;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(4)(E)","text":"describe appropriate ways to dispose of hazardous materials and wastes;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(4)(F)","text":"maintain, safely handle, and properly store laboratory equipment;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(4)(G)","text":"describe the implications of negligent or improper maintenance; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(4)(H)","text":"demonstrate the use of precision measuring instruments."}]},{"code":"127.781(d)(5)","statement":"(d)(5) The student describes the factors that affect the progression of technology and analyzes the potential intended and unintended consequences of technological advances. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(5)(A)","text":"describe how technology has affected individuals, societies, cultures, economies, and environments;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(5)(B)","text":"describe how the development and use of technology influenced past events;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(5)(C)","text":"describe how and why technology progresses; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(5)(D)","text":"predict possible changes caused by the advances of technology."}]},{"code":"127.781(d)(6)","statement":"(d)(6) The student thinks critically and applies fundamental principles of system modeling and design to multiple design projects. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(6)(A)","text":"identify and describe an engineering design process needed for a project, including the design process and prototype development and initiating, planning, executing, monitoring and controlling, and closing a project;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(6)(B)","text":"identify the chemical, mechanical, and physical properties of engineering materials and identify testing methods associated with the materials;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(6)(C)","text":"use problem-solving techniques to develop technological solutions such as product, process, or system;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(6)(D)","text":"use consistent units for all measurements and computations; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(6)(E)","text":"assess the risks and benefits of a design solution."}]},{"code":"127.781(d)(7)","statement":"(d)(7) The student understands the opportunities and careers in fields related to robotics, process control, and automation systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(7)(A)","text":"describe applications of robotics, process control, and automation systems;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(7)(B)","text":"apply design concepts to problems in robotics, process control, and automation systems;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(7)(C)","text":"identify fields and career opportunities related to robotics, process control, and automation systems; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(7)(D)","text":"identify emerging trends in robotics, process control, and automation systems."}]},{"code":"127.781(d)(8)","statement":"(d)(8) The student understands the opportunities and careers in fields related to electrical and mechanical systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(8)(A)","text":"describe the applications of electrical and mechanical systems;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(8)(B)","text":"describe career opportunities in electrical and mechanical systems;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(8)(C)","text":"identify emerging trends in electrical and mechanical systems; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(8)(D)","text":"describe and apply basic electronic theory."}]},{"code":"127.781(d)(9)","statement":"(d)(9) The student collaborates as a team member while completing a comprehensive project. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(9)(A)","text":"apply the design process, including decision matrices, as a team participant;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(9)(B)","text":"perform different roles within the project as a team member;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(9)(C)","text":"formulate decisions using collaborative strategies such as decision and design matrices and conflict resolution;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(9)(D)","text":"maintain an engineering notebook for the project;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(9)(E)","text":"develop and test the model for the project; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(9)(F)","text":"demonstrate communication skills by preparing and presenting the project, including building consensus setback resolution and decision matrices."}]},{"code":"127.781(d)(10)","statement":"(d)(10) The student demonstrates a knowledge of drafting by completing a series of drawings that can be published by various media. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(10)(A)","text":"set up, create, and modify drawings;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(10)(B)","text":"store and retrieve geometry;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(10)(C)","text":"demonstrate and use appropriate line types in engineering drawings;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(10)(D)","text":"draw two-dimensional, single-view objects;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(10)(E)","text":"create multi-view working drawings using orthographic projection;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(10)(F)","text":"dimension objects using current American National Standards Institute (ANSI) standards;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(10)(G)","text":"draw single-line two-dimensional pictorial representations; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(10)(H)","text":"create working drawings that include section views."}]},{"code":"127.781(d)(11)","statement":"(d)(11) The student creates justifiable solutions to open-ended real-world problems using engineering design practices and processes. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.781(d)(11)(A)","text":"identify and define an engineering problem;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(11)(B)","text":"formulate goals, objectives, and requirements to solve an engineering problem;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(11)(C)","text":"determine the design parameters such as materials, personnel, resources, funding, manufacturability, feasibility, and time associated with an engineering problem;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(11)(D)","text":"establish and evaluate potential constraints, including health, safety, social, environmental, ethical, political, regulatory, and legal, pertaining to a problem;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(11)(E)","text":"identify or create alternative solutions to a problem using a variety of techniques such as brainstorming, reverse engineering, and researching engineered and natural solutions;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(11)(F)","text":"test and evaluate proposed solutions using engineering methods such as creating models, prototypes, mock-ups, or simulations or performing critical design review, statistical analysis, or experiments;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(11)(G)","text":"apply structured techniques such as a decision tree, design matrix, or cost-benefit analysis to select and justify a preferred solution to a problem;"},{"code":"127.781(d)(11)(H)","text":"predict performance, failure modes, and reliability of a design solution; and"},{"code":"127.781(d)(11)(I)","text":"prepare a project report that clearly documents the designs, decisions, and activities during each phase of the engineering design process."}]}],"sequence":[],"tacSection":"19 TAC §127.781"},{"course":"Communication Applications","completeness":"COMPLETE (TEKS) — All 4 knowledge-and-skills strands (b)(1)–(b)(4) and every lettered student expectation captured verbatim from 19 TAC §110.58 (One-Half Credit). CHAPTER FLAG: This course lives in Chapter 110 (English Language Arts and Reading), NOT a CTE chapter — it is the speech/communication graduation elective. NO PACING / SEQUENCE provided — sequence omitted. NO STAAR. Strand codes normalized to CA.1–CA.4.","hasStaarEOC":false,"strands":[{"code":"110.58(b)(1)","statement":"(b)(1) Communication process. The student demonstrates knowledge of various communication processes in professional and social contexts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(A)","text":"explain the importance of effective communication skills in professional and social contexts;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(B)","text":"identify the components of the communication process and their functions;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(C)","text":"identify standards for making appropriate communication choices for self, listener, occasion, and task;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(D)","text":"identify the characteristics of oral language and analyze standards for using informal, standard, and technical language appropriately;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(E)","text":"identify types of nonverbal communication and their effects;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(F)","text":"recognize the importance of effective nonverbal strategies such as appearance, a firm handshake, direct eye contact, and appropriate use of space and distance;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(G)","text":"identify the components of the listening process;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(H)","text":"identify specific kinds of listening such as critical, deliberative, and empathic;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(I)","text":"recognize the importance of gathering and using accurate and complete information as a basis for making communication decisions;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(J)","text":"identify and analyze ethical and social responsibilities of communicators; and"},{"code":"110.58(b)(1)(K)","text":"recognize and analyze appropriate channels of communication in organizations."}]},{"code":"110.58(b)(2)","statement":"(b)(2) Interpersonal. The student uses appropriate interpersonal communication strategies in professional and social contexts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"110.58(b)(2)(A)","text":"identify types of professional and social relationships, their importance, and the purposes they serve;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(2)(B)","text":"employ appropriate verbal, nonverbal, and listening skills to enhance interpersonal relationships;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(2)(C)","text":"use communication management skills to develop appropriate assertiveness, tact, and courtesy;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(2)(D)","text":"use professional etiquette and protocol in situations such as making introductions, speaking on the telephone, and offering and receiving criticism;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(2)(E)","text":"send clear and appropriate requests, provide clear and accurate directions, ask appropriate and purposeful questions, and respond appropriately to the requests, directions, and questions of others;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(2)(F)","text":"participate appropriately in conversations;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(2)(G)","text":"communicate effectively in interviews;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(2)(H)","text":"identify and use appropriate strategies for dealing with differences, including gender, ethnicity, and age; and"},{"code":"110.58(b)(2)(I)","text":"analyze and evaluate the effectiveness of one's own and others' communication."}]},{"code":"110.58(b)(3)","statement":"(b)(3) Group communication. The student communicates effectively in groups in professional and social contexts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"110.58(b)(3)(A)","text":"identify kinds of groups, their importance, and the purposes they serve;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(3)(B)","text":"analyze group dynamics and processes for participating effectively in groups;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(3)(C)","text":"identify and analyze the roles of group members and their influence on group dynamics;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(3)(D)","text":"demonstrate understanding of group roles and their impact on group effectiveness;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(3)(E)","text":"use appropriate verbal, nonverbal, and listening skills to promote group effectiveness;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(3)(F)","text":"identify and analyze leadership styles;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(3)(G)","text":"use effective communication strategies in leadership roles;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(3)(H)","text":"use effective communication strategies for solving problems, managing conflicts, and building consensus in groups; and"},{"code":"110.58(b)(3)(I)","text":"analyze the participation and contributions of group members and evaluate group effectiveness."}]},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)","statement":"(b)(4) Presentations. The student makes and evaluates formal and informal professional presentations. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(A)","text":"analyze the audience, occasion, and purpose when designing presentations;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(B)","text":"determine specific topics and purposes for presentations;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(C)","text":"research topics using primary and secondary sources, including electronic technology;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(D)","text":"use effective strategies to organize and outline presentations;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(E)","text":"use information effectively to support and clarify points in presentations;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(F)","text":"prepare scripts or notes for presentations;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(G)","text":"prepare and use visual or auditory aids, including technology, to enhance presentations;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(H)","text":"use appropriate techniques to manage communication apprehension, build self-confidence, and gain command of the information;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(I)","text":"use effective verbal and nonverbal strategies in presentations;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(J)","text":"make group presentations to inform, persuade, or motivate an audience;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(K)","text":"make individual presentations to inform, persuade, or motivate an audience;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(L)","text":"participate in question-and-answer sessions following presentations;"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(M)","text":"apply critical-listening strategies to evaluate presentations; and"},{"code":"110.58(b)(4)(N)","text":"evaluate effectiveness of his/her own presentation."}]}],"sequence":[],"tacSection":"19 TAC §110.58"},{"course":"Career and College Exploration","completeness":"COMPLETE (TEKS) — All 8 knowledge-and-skills strands (d)(1)–(d)(8) and every lettered student expectation captured verbatim from 19 TAC §127.2 (Grades 7-8; adopted 2015, re-adopted eff. 2023). COURSE-IDENTITY FLAG: There is NO standalone Texas HS 'College and Career Readiness' or 'AVID' course with its own TEKS; AVID is proprietary (no TEKS) and CCRS is a separate non-TEKS framework. §127.2 is a MIDDLE-SCHOOL course and is the closest authentic TEKS match — verify this is the intended course before using as an HS dataset. NO PACING / SEQUENCE provided — sequence omitted. NO STAAR. Strand codes normalized to CCE.1–CCE.8.","hasStaarEOC":false,"strands":[{"code":"127.2(d)(1)","statement":"(d)(1) The student takes one or more career interest surveys, aptitude tests, or career assessments and explores various college and career options. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.2(d)(1)(A)","text":"analyze and discuss the initial results of the assessments;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(1)(B)","text":"explore and describe the CTE career clusters;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(1)(C)","text":"identify various career opportunities within one or more career clusters; and"},{"code":"127.2(d)(1)(D)","text":"research and evaluate emerging occupations related to career interest areas."}]},{"code":"127.2(d)(2)","statement":"(d)(2) The student investigates educational and training requirements for career and education pathways in one or more of the career clusters. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.2(d)(2)(A)","text":"research and describe applicable academic, technical, certification, and training requirements for one or more of the careers in an identified career cluster; and"},{"code":"127.2(d)(2)(B)","text":"use available resources to research and evaluate educational and training options for one or more of the careers in an identified career cluster."}]},{"code":"127.2(d)(3)","statement":"(d)(3) The student analyzes educational and career opportunities. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.2(d)(3)(A)","text":"describe academic requirements for transitioning from middle school to high school and from high school to career or postsecondary education;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(3)(B)","text":"explore and list opportunities for earning college credit in high school such as Advanced Placement examinations, International Baccalaureate examinations, dual credit courses, and local and statewide articulated credit courses;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(3)(C)","text":"investigate and describe various methods available to pay for college and other postsecondary training, including financial aid, scholarships, college savings, employee benefits, and other sources of income;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(3)(D)","text":"discuss the impact of effective college and career planning;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(3)(E)","text":"identify how performance on assessments such as the PSAT/NMSQT®, SAT®, ACT®, ASVAB®, and Texas Success Initiative (TSI®) impact personal academic and career goals;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(3)(F)","text":"investigate and describe the importance of co-curricular, extracurricular, career preparation, and extended learning experiences in developing college applications or resumes;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(3)(G)","text":"investigate and report on the steps required to participate or enroll in a variety of career and educational opportunities, including entry-level employment, military service, apprenticeships, community and technical colleges, and universities, as applicable to the career;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(3)(H)","text":"identify professional associations affiliated with a particular career pathway; and"},{"code":"127.2(d)(3)(I)","text":"define entrepreneurship and identify entrepreneurial opportunities within a field of personal interest."}]},{"code":"127.2(d)(4)","statement":"(d)(4) The student develops skills for personal success. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.2(d)(4)(A)","text":"demonstrate effective time-management and goal-setting strategies;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(4)(B)","text":"identify skills that can be transferable among a variety of careers;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(4)(C)","text":"give oral professional presentations on a topic related to career and college exploration using appropriate technology;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(4)(D)","text":"apply core academic skills to meet personal, academic, and career goals;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(4)(E)","text":"explain the value of community service and volunteerism; and"},{"code":"127.2(d)(4)(F)","text":"define and identify examples in the workplace of characteristics required for personal and professional success such as work ethic, integrity, dedication, and perseverance."}]},{"code":"127.2(d)(5)","statement":"(d)(5) The student investigates labor market information and recognizes the impact of college and career choices on personal lifestyle. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.2(d)(5)(A)","text":"analyze labor market trends related to a career of interest;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(5)(B)","text":"classify evidence of high-skill, high-wage, or high-demand occupations based on analysis of labor market information;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(5)(C)","text":"analyze the effects of changing employment trends, societal needs, and economic conditions on career choices;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(5)(D)","text":"prepare a personal budget reflecting the student's desired lifestyle; and"},{"code":"127.2(d)(5)(E)","text":"use resources to compare salaries of at least three careers in the student's interest area."}]},{"code":"127.2(d)(6)","statement":"(d)(6) The student investigates job-seeking skills. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.2(d)(6)(A)","text":"identify the steps of an effective job search;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(6)(B)","text":"describe appropriate appearance for an interview; and"},{"code":"127.2(d)(6)(C)","text":"participate in a mock interview."}]},{"code":"127.2(d)(7)","statement":"(d)(7) The student creates professional documents required for employment. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.2(d)(7)(A)","text":"write a resume;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(7)(B)","text":"write appropriate business correspondence such as a cover letter and a thank you letter;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(7)(C)","text":"complete sample job applications; and"},{"code":"127.2(d)(7)(D)","text":"explain protocol for selecting and using references."}]},{"code":"127.2(d)(8)","statement":"(d)(8) The student creates an individual career and academic plan. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.2(d)(8)(A)","text":"select a career pathway in a desired field, such as military service, entrepreneurship, or industry;"},{"code":"127.2(d)(8)(B)","text":"document high school courses and postsecondary educational requirements for that career pathway; and"},{"code":"127.2(d)(8)(C)","text":"write a plan for starting one's career after the completion of high school and any post-secondary education."}]}],"sequence":[],"tacSection":"19 TAC §127.2"},{"course":"Principles of Information Technology","completeness":"COMPLETE (TEKS) — All 13 knowledge-and-skills strands (c)(1)–(c)(13) and every lettered student expectation captured verbatim from 19 TAC §130.302 (One Credit, adopted eff. 2017; IT cluster). Under the 2025 SBOE CTE relocation this is migrating to Chapter 127, but §130.302 is the cleanly published adopted version. NO PACING / SEQUENCE provided — sequence omitted. NO STAAR. Strand codes normalized to POIT.1–POIT.13.","hasStaarEOC":false,"strands":[{"code":"127.671(c)(1)","statement":"(c)(1) The student demonstrates professional standards/employability skills as required by business and industry. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(1)(A)","text":"identify and demonstrate work behaviors and qualities that enhance employability and job advancement such as regular attendance, attention to proper attire, maintenance of a clean and safe work environment, pride in work, flexibility, and initiative;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(1)(B)","text":"employ effective verbal and nonverbal communication skills;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(1)(C)","text":"employ effective reading and writing skills;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(1)(D)","text":"solve problems and think critically;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(1)(E)","text":"demonstrate leadership skills and function effectively as a team member;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(1)(F)","text":"identify and implement proper safety procedures; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(1)(G)","text":"demonstrate planning and time-management skills such as storyboarding and project management, including initiating, planning, executing, monitoring and controlling, and closing a project."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(2)","statement":"(c)(2) The student identifies various employment opportunities in the IT field. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(2)(A)","text":"identify job opportunities and accompanying job duties and tasks;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(2)(B)","text":"research careers of personal interest along with the education, job skills, and experience required to achieve personal career goals; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(2)(C)","text":"describe the functions of resumes and portfolios."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(3)","statement":"(c)(3) The student uses evolving and emerging technologies to exchange information. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(3)(A)","text":"identify and describe functions of various evolving and emerging technologies;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(3)(B)","text":"send and receive text information and file attachments using electronic methods such as email, electronic bulletin boards, and instant message services;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(3)(C)","text":"demonstrate effective Internet search strategies, including keywords and Boolean logic, using various available search engines;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(3)(D)","text":"identify the various components of a Uniform Resource Locator;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(3)(E)","text":"demonstrate ability to effectively test acquired information from the Internet for accuracy, relevance, and validity;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(3)(F)","text":"explain issues concerning computer-based threats such as computer viruses, malware, and hacking; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(3)(G)","text":"explain issues concerning Internet safety such as identity theft, online predators, cyber-bullying, and phishing."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(4)","statement":"(c)(4) The student demonstrates knowledge of the hardware components associated with information systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(4)(A)","text":"identify major hardware components and their functions;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(4)(B)","text":"use available reference tools as appropriate; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(4)(C)","text":"connect and use a variety of peripheral devices such as mouse, keyboard, microphone, digital camera, and printer."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(5)","statement":"(c)(5) The student demonstrates knowledge of the different software associated with information systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(5)(A)","text":"differentiate between systems and application software;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(5)(B)","text":"identify and explain major operating system fundamentals and components such as disk operations, graphical user interface components, and hardware drivers;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(5)(C)","text":"explain the purpose of file types across software products;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(5)(D)","text":"demonstrate use of computer numbering systems and internal data representation such as identifying the hexadecimal value of a color;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(5)(E)","text":"compare and contrast open source and proprietary software;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(5)(F)","text":"explain use of system management tools;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(5)(G)","text":"apply proper file management techniques such as creating, naming, organizing, copying, moving, and deleting files;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(5)(H)","text":"use appropriate file protection and security; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(5)(I)","text":"explain the process for discovering, quarantining, and removing viruses from a computer system."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(6)","statement":"(c)(6) The student analyzes network systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(6)(A)","text":"identify hardware associated with telecommunications and data networking such as servers, routers, switches, and network connectors;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(6)(B)","text":"identify and describe various types of networks such as peer-to-peer, local area networks, wide area networks, wireless, and Ethernet;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(6)(C)","text":"identify functions of network operating systems; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(6)(D)","text":"explain troubleshooting techniques for various network connection issues."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(7)","statement":"(c)(7) The student applies word-processing technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(7)(A)","text":"identify the terminology associated with word-processing software;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(7)(B)","text":"edit a variety of text documents using functions such as pagination, appropriate white space, tab settings, and font style, size, and color; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(7)(C)","text":"create professional documents such as memorandums, technical manuals, or proposals using advanced word-processing features."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(8)","statement":"(c)(8) The student applies spreadsheet technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(8)(A)","text":"identify the terminology associated with spreadsheet software;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(8)(B)","text":"use numerical content to perform mathematical calculations;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(8)(C)","text":"use student-created and preprogrammed functions to produce documents such as budget, payroll, statistical tables, and personal checkbook register;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(8)(D)","text":"identify, generate, and describe the function of comma separated value files;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(8)(E)","text":"create and analyze spreadsheets incorporating advanced features such as lookup tables, nested IF statements, subtotals, cell protection conditional formatting, charts, and graphs; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(8)(F)","text":"perform sorting, searching, and data filtering in documents."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(9)","statement":"(c)(9) The student explores computer programming concepts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(9)(A)","text":"identify the function of compilers and interpreters;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(9)(B)","text":"explain the difference between the operation of compilers and interpreters;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(9)(C)","text":"identify various computer languages and how the languages are used in software development;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(9)(D)","text":"recognize data representation in software development such as string, numeric, character, integer, and date;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(9)(E)","text":"identify and explain the concept of algorithms; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(9)(F)","text":"describe the flow of a structured algorithm, including linear and iterative instructions such as using a flow chart."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(10)","statement":"(c)(10) The student explores database technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(10)(A)","text":"identify the terminology associated with database software and database functions;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(10)(B)","text":"explore the application of databases;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(10)(C)","text":"identify and explain the purpose and elements of a query language;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(10)(D)","text":"identify and explain the purpose of fields and records; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(10)(E)","text":"describe the process of constructing a query, including multiple search parameters."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(11)","statement":"(c)(11) The student applies presentation management technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(11)(A)","text":"identify the terminology and functions of presentation software; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(11)(B)","text":"create, save, edit, and produce presentations incorporating advanced features such as links, hyperlinks, audio, and graphics."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(12)","statement":"(c)(12) The student applies design and web publishing techniques. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(12)(A)","text":"identify the terminology associated with web page development and interactive media;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(12)(B)","text":"identify and explain design elements such as typeface, color, shape, texture, space, and form;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(12)(C)","text":"identify and explain design principles such as unity, harmony, balance, scale, and contrast;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(12)(D)","text":"identify and explain common elements of Hyper Text Markup Language (HTML) such as tags, stylesheets, and hyperlinks; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(12)(E)","text":"create a web page containing links, graphics, and text using appropriate design principles."}]},{"code":"127.671(c)(13)","statement":"(c)(13) The student understands and demonstrates legal and ethical procedures as they apply to the use of information technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"127.671(c)(13)(A)","text":"explain and demonstrate ethical use of technology and online resources;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(13)(B)","text":"adhere to intellectual property laws;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(13)(C)","text":"explain the concept of intellectual property laws, including copyright, trademarks, and patents and consequences of violating each type of law;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(13)(D)","text":"examine the consequences of plagiarism;"},{"code":"127.671(c)(13)(E)","text":"identify and explain unethical practices such as hacking, online piracy, and data vandalism; and"},{"code":"127.671(c)(13)(F)","text":"demonstrate ethical use of online resources, including citation of source."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"IT Careers & Employability Skills","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(1)","127.671(c)(2)"],"items":["Explore IT career clusters: support, networking, programming, cybersecurity, web/design","Research education paths, salaries, and industry certifications (CompTIA, Microsoft) using labor-market data","Build a professional resume, cover letter, and mock digital portfolio","Practice workplace soft skills: teamwork, time management, communication, professional etiquette","Conduct a mock job interview and self-assess against an employability rubric"]},{"unit":"Legal, Ethical & Safe Computing","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(13)","127.671(c)(1)"],"items":["Define copyright, fair use, licensing, creative commons, and plagiarism","Analyze the Acceptable Use Policy, cyberbullying, and digital footprint/reputation","Investigate cybersecurity threats: phishing, malware, social engineering, and strong-password habits","Examine privacy and IP law (FERPA/HIPAA basics, software piracy)","Debate ethical dilemmas in AI, data collection, and real case-study scenarios"]},{"unit":"Computer Hardware & Information Systems","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(4)"],"items":["Identify internal components: CPU, RAM, motherboard, storage, GPU, power supply","Compare input, output, processing, and storage devices and their units of measure","Explain the information-processing cycle and binary/data representation","Connect and configure peripherals; troubleshoot common hardware issues","Compare device types (desktop, laptop, mobile, server) and evaluate specs for a use case","Complete a teardown/build simulation and label a full system diagram"]},{"unit":"Software & Operating Systems","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(5)"],"items":["Distinguish system software, application software, and utilities","Compare operating systems (Windows, macOS, Linux, mobile) and their functions","Manage files, folders, and formats; use compression and cloud storage","Install, update, and license software; open-source vs proprietary models","Use OS utilities for maintenance, security, and performance tuning"]},{"unit":"Networks & The Internet","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(6)"],"items":["Compare LAN, WAN, and common network topologies and their components","Identify networking hardware: routers, switches, modems, access points","Explain IP addressing, protocols (TCP/IP, HTTP, DNS), and bandwidth","Compare wired vs wireless and evaluate network-security basics","Trace how data travels the Internet; diagram a home/school network"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["127.671(c)(4)","127.671(c)(5)","127.671(c)(6)"],"items":["Reteach hardware, software, and networking concepts flagged by formative data","Grading-period benchmark assessment and structured error analysis","Certification-style practice questions (CompTIA IT Fundamentals aligned)","Catch-up lab time and unfinished-project completion","Absorb calendar disruptions (assemblies, weather, testing days)"]},{"unit":"Emerging Technologies & Digital Communication","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(3)"],"items":["Explore cloud computing, IoT, AI, VR/AR, and automation trends","Use collaborative tools (shared docs, video conferencing, project boards) to exchange information","Evaluate digital communication methods and email/messaging etiquette","Assess emerging-tech impact on society, jobs, and daily life","Curate, evaluate, and cite credible online sources for reliability"]},{"unit":"Word Processing","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(7)","127.671(c)(1)"],"items":["Format documents: fonts, styles, paragraphs, margins, headers/footers","Insert and format tables, images, columns, and page layout","Apply styles, templates, and mail-merge for professional documents","Use editing tools: spell/grammar check, track changes, comments","Produce a business letter, flyer, and MLA-formatted report","Apply professional standards to a real-world document workflow (recurring employability)"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Benchmark (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["127.671(c)(4)","127.671(c)(5)","127.671(c)(6)","127.671(c)(7)"],"items":["Cumulative review of hardware, software, networks, and productivity skills","Semester benchmark exam with data-driven error analysis","Spiral practice on core vocabulary and applied tasks","Portfolio check-in and student reflection","Reteach and enrichment stations; flexible pre-break calendar cushion"]},{"unit":"Spreadsheets","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(8)"],"items":["Build worksheets: cells, rows, columns, data entry, and formatting","Write formulas and functions (SUM, AVERAGE, IF, COUNT, MIN/MAX)","Use relative and absolute references, fill, and autofill","Create and interpret charts and graphs from data sets","Sort, filter, and analyze data to solve problems","Design a budget/data-analysis project with formulas and visualization"]},{"unit":"Databases","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(10)"],"items":["Explain database purpose: tables, records, fields, and data types","Create tables and enter/edit data with validation rules","Build queries to sort, filter, and retrieve information","Design data-entry forms and generate reports","Understand relational concepts and primary/foreign keys","Complete a small database project (inventory or contacts)"]},{"unit":"Presentation Management","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(11)","127.671(c)(1)"],"items":["Plan and storyboard an effective presentation for a target audience","Design slides: layout, themes, typography, and visual hierarchy","Insert media purposefully: images, charts, transitions, animations","Apply design principles (CRAP) and accessibility considerations","Deliver with professional public-speaking skills (recurring employability)","Peer-review and revise against a scoring rubric"]},{"unit":"Computer Programming Concepts","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(9)"],"items":["Explore algorithms, pseudocode, and flowcharts to solve problems","Use variables, data types, and input/output","Apply sequence, selection (if/else), and iteration (loops)","Use logical/relational operators and simple functions","Trace, test, and debug programs (block-based or intro text language)","Build a small program or game demonstrating core constructs"]},{"unit":"Design & Web Publishing","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["127.671(c)(12)"],"items":["Apply design principles, color, and typography for the web","Build web pages with HTML structure and CSS styling","Add hyperlinks, images, and navigation across a multi-page site","Plan site architecture, wireframes, and user experience","Address accessibility, responsive design, and web copyright/ethics","Publish and present a personal/topic website project"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & IT Capstone/Final (wiggle)","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["127.671(c)(1)","127.671(c)(2)","127.671(c)(9)","127.671(c)(11)","127.671(c)(12)"],"items":["Cumulative course review across all strands with certification-prep practice","Capstone project integrating word-processing, spreadsheet, presentation, and web skills","Refine a professional digital portfolio and career plan (recurring employability)","Final exam and performance-based assessment","Reflection, showcase, and next-steps in the IT pathway","Flexible cushion for spring testing-window schedule disruptions and end-of-year events"]}],"tacSection":"19 TAC §127.671"},{"course":"English I","tacSection":"19 TAC §110.36","hasStaarEOC":true,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter110_sec.110.36","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-110"],"strands":[{"code":"110.36(c)(1)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, discussion, and thinking--oral language. The student develops oral language through listening, speaking, and discussion. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"engage in meaningful and respectful discourse by listening actively, responding appropriately, and adjusting communication to audiences and purposes;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"follow and give complex oral instructions to perform specific tasks, answer questions, or solve problems and complex processes;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"give a presentation using informal, formal, and technical language effectively to meet the needs of audience, purpose, and occasion, employing eye contact, speaking rate such as pauses for effect, volume, enunciation, purposeful gestures, and conventions of language to communicate ideas effectively; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"participate collaboratively, building on the ideas of others, contributing relevant information, developing a plan for consensus building, and setting ground rules for decision making."}]},{"code":"110.36(c)(2)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking--vocabulary. The student uses newly acquired vocabulary expressively. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use print or digital resources such as glossaries or technical dictionaries to clarify and validate understanding of the precise and appropriate meaning of technical or discipline-based vocabulary;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze context to distinguish between the denotative and connotative meanings of words; and"},{"code":"(C)","text":"determine the meaning of foreign words or phrases used frequently in English such as bona fide, caveat, carte blanche, tête-à-tête, bon appétit, and quid pro quo."}]},{"code":"110.36(c)(3)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking--self-sustained reading. The student reads grade-appropriate texts independently. The student is expected to self-select text and read independently for a sustained period of time.","expectations":[]},{"code":"110.36(c)(4)","statement":"Comprehension skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student uses metacognitive skills to both develop and deepen comprehension of increasingly complex texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"establish purpose for reading assigned and self-selected texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"generate questions about text before, during, and after reading to deepen understanding and gain information;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"make and correct or confirm predictions using text features, characteristics of genre, and structures;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"create mental images to deepen understanding;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"make connections to personal experiences, ideas in other texts, and society;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"make inferences and use evidence to support understanding;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"evaluate details read to determine key ideas;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"synthesize information from two texts to create new understanding; and"},{"code":"(I)","text":"monitor comprehension and make adjustments such as re-reading, using background knowledge, asking questions, and annotating when understanding breaks down."}]},{"code":"110.36(c)(5)","statement":"Response skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student responds to an increasingly challenging variety of sources that are read, heard, or viewed. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"describe personal connections to a variety of sources, including self-selected texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"write responses that demonstrate understanding of texts, including comparing texts within and across genres;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"use text evidence and original commentary to support a comprehensive response;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"paraphrase and summarize texts in ways that maintain meaning and logical order;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"interact with sources in meaningful ways such as notetaking, annotating, freewriting, or illustrating;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"respond using acquired content and academic vocabulary as appropriate;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"discuss and write about the explicit or implicit meanings of text;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"respond orally or in writing with appropriate register, vocabulary, tone, and voice;"},{"code":"(I)","text":"reflect on and adjust responses when valid evidence warrants; and"},{"code":"(J)","text":"defend or challenge the authors' claims using relevant text evidence."}]},{"code":"110.36(c)(6)","statement":"Multiple genres: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--literary elements. The student recognizes and analyzes literary elements within and across increasingly complex traditional, contemporary, classical, and diverse literary texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"analyze how themes are developed through characterization and plot in a variety of literary texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze how authors develop complex yet believable characters in works of fiction through a range of literary devices, including character foils;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"analyze non-linear plot development such as flashbacks, foreshadowing, subplots, and parallel plot structures and compare it to linear plot development; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"analyze how the setting influences the theme."}]},{"code":"110.36(c)(7)","statement":"Multiple genres: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--genres. The student recognizes and analyzes genre-specific characteristics, structures, and purposes within and across increasingly complex traditional, contemporary, classical, and diverse texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"read and respond to American, British, and world literature;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze the structure, prosody, and graphic elements such as line length and word position in poems across a variety of poetic forms;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"analyze the function of dramatic conventions such as asides, soliloquies, dramatic irony, and satire;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"analyze characteristics and structural elements of informational texts such as: (i) clear thesis, relevant supporting evidence, pertinent examples, and conclusion; and (ii) multiple organizational patterns within a text to develop the thesis;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"analyze characteristics and structural elements of argumentative texts such as: (i) clear arguable claim, appeals, and convincing conclusion; (ii) various types of evidence and treatment of counterarguments, including concessions and rebuttals; and (iii) identifiable audience or reader; and"},{"code":"(F)","text":"analyze characteristics of multimodal and digital texts."}]},{"code":"110.36(c)(8)","statement":"Author's purpose and craft: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student uses critical inquiry to analyze the authors' choices and how they influence and communicate meaning within a variety of texts. The student analyzes and applies author's craft purposefully in order to develop his or her own products and performances. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"analyze the author's purpose, audience, and message within a text;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze use of text structure to achieve the author's purpose;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"evaluate the author's use of print and graphic features to achieve specific purposes;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"analyze how the author's use of language achieves specific purposes;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"analyze the use of literary devices such as irony and oxymoron to achieve specific purposes;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"analyze how the author's diction and syntax contribute to the mood, voice, and tone of a text; and"},{"code":"(G)","text":"explain the purpose of rhetorical devices such as understatement and overstatement and the effect of logical fallacies such as straw man and red herring arguments."}]},{"code":"110.36(c)(9)","statement":"Composition: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--writing process. The student uses the writing process recursively to compose multiple texts that are legible and use appropriate conventions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"plan a piece of writing appropriate for various purposes and audiences by generating ideas through a range of strategies such as brainstorming, journaling, reading, or discussing;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"develop drafts into a focused, structured, and coherent piece of writing in timed and open-ended situations by: (i) using an organizing structure appropriate to purpose, audience, topic, and context; and (ii) developing an engaging idea reflecting depth of thought with specific details, examples, and commentary;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"revise drafts to improve clarity, development, organization, style, diction, and sentence effectiveness, including use of parallel constructions and placement of phrases and dependent clauses;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"edit drafts using standard English conventions, including: (i) a variety of complete, controlled sentences and avoidance of unintentional splices, run-ons, and fragments; (ii) consistent, appropriate use of verb tense and active and passive voice; (iii) pronoun-antecedent agreement; (iv) correct capitalization; (v) punctuation, including commas, semicolons, colons, and dashes to set off phrases and clauses as appropriate; and (vi) correct spelling; and"},{"code":"(E)","text":"publish written work for appropriate audiences."}]},{"code":"110.36(c)(10)","statement":"Composition: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--genres. The student uses genre characteristics and craft to compose multiple texts that are meaningful. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"compose literary texts such as fiction and poetry using genre characteristics and craft;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"compose informational texts such as explanatory essays, reports, and personal essays using genre characteristics and craft;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"compose argumentative texts using genre characteristics and craft; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"compose correspondence in a professional or friendly structure."}]},{"code":"110.36(c)(11)","statement":"Inquiry and research: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student engages in both short-term and sustained recursive inquiry processes for a variety of purposes. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"develop questions for formal and informal inquiry;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"critique the research process at each step to implement changes as needs occur and are identified;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"develop and revise a plan;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"modify the major research question as necessary to refocus the research plan;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"locate relevant sources;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"synthesize information from a variety of sources;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"examine sources for: (i) credibility and bias, including omission; and (ii) faulty reasoning such as ad hominem, loaded language, and slippery slope;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"display academic citations, including for paraphrased and quoted text, and use source materials ethically to avoid plagiarism; and"},{"code":"(I)","text":"use an appropriate mode of delivery, whether written, oral, or multimodal, to present results."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Launch: Foundations of Reading, Discussion & Independent Reading","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(1)","110.36(c)(2)","110.36(c)(3)","110.36(c)(4)"],"items":["Class norms and collaborative discussion protocols (accountable talk, listening moves)","Launch independent reading: book selection, reading logs, sustained reading routines","Metacognitive strategies: annotation, think-alouds, monitoring and repairing comprehension","Academic vocabulary routines: context clues, word parts, vocabulary notebook","Diagnostic reading and on-demand writing baseline"]},{"unit":"Narrative & Personal Memoir","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(3)","110.36(c)(6)","110.36(c)(9)","110.36(c)(10)"],"items":["Plot structure and the narrative arc (exposition through resolution)","Characterization, point of view, and narrator reliability","Setting, conflict, and emerging theme in short narratives","Prewriting and drafting a personal memoir (writing process)","Revising for sensory detail, dialogue, and pacing","Editing and publishing the memoir with a reflection"]},{"unit":"Short Fiction: Literary Elements & Author's Craft","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(2)","110.36(c)(5)","110.36(c)(6)","110.36(c)(8)"],"items":["Theme and central idea across multiple short stories","Figurative language and imagery as author's craft","Tone, mood, and diction analysis","Connotation, denotation, and using new vocabulary expressively","Text-dependent short-answer response (evidence plus commentary)","Socratic seminar on a complex short story"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["110.36(c)(3)","110.36(c)(4)"],"items":["Data-driven reteach of targeted comprehension and literary-element skills","Common formative assessment and error analysis","Small-group conferencing and independent reading check-in","Flex day to absorb calendar drift and makeup work"]},{"unit":"Poetry & Figurative Language","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(6)","110.36(c)(7)","110.36(c)(8)","110.36(c)(10)"],"items":["Poetry genre features: form, stanza, line, and meter","Sound devices: rhyme, rhythm, alliteration, assonance","Figurative language and extended metaphor in poems","Analyzing how structure builds meaning (TP-CASTT)","Composing an original poem with intentional craft"]},{"unit":"Argument & Rhetoric: Reading Nonfiction","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(1)","110.36(c)(5)","110.36(c)(7)","110.36(c)(8)"],"items":["Features and structure of argumentative and persuasive nonfiction","Rhetorical appeals: ethos, pathos, and logos","Identifying claim, evidence, and reasoning","Evaluating source credibility and detecting bias","Structured academic discussion and debate","Written response evaluating an author's argument"]},{"unit":"Informational Reading & Multiple-Text Comprehension","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(2)","110.36(c)(4)","110.36(c)(5)"],"items":["Text structures: cause/effect, compare/contrast, problem/solution","Central idea and objective summary across texts","Making inferences and drawing conclusions (metacognition)","Synthesizing information from paired informational texts","Academic vocabulary and signal words","Synthesis written response"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Exam","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["110.36(c)(2)","110.36(c)(4)","110.36(c)(6)"],"items":["Spiral review of literary elements and comprehension strategies","Vocabulary and figurative-language review","Timed passage practice with answer-rationale analysis","Semester exam and reflection on growth"]},{"unit":"Argumentative Writing","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(2)","110.36(c)(5)","110.36(c)(9)","110.36(c)(10)"],"items":["Forming a defensible claim and acknowledging a counterclaim","Gathering, integrating, and citing textual evidence","Organizing the argument: intro, body, rebuttal, conclusion","Drafting the argumentative essay (recursive writing process)","Peer revision for logic, transitions, and voice","Editing for grammar, usage, and citation conventions"]},{"unit":"Drama: Romeo and Juliet","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(1)","110.36(c)(6)","110.36(c)(7)","110.36(c)(8)"],"items":["Drama conventions: acts, scenes, stage directions, soliloquy, aside","Shakespeare's language and dramatic devices (dramatic irony, foreshadowing)","Character motivation and evolving conflict across acts","Theme analysis: love, fate, honor, and the feud","Oral performance and close reading of key scenes","Written literary analysis of a pivotal scene"]},{"unit":"Inquiry & Research Project","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(1)","110.36(c)(5)","110.36(c)(9)","110.36(c)(11)"],"items":["Developing a research question and an inquiry plan","Locating and evaluating credible print and digital sources","Note-taking, paraphrasing, and avoiding plagiarism","Synthesizing sources into an organized outline","Drafting the research product with in-text citations and works cited","Presenting findings orally with multimedia support"]},{"unit":"STAAR EOC Review","weeks":3,"kind":"review","teks":["110.36(c)(4)","110.36(c)(6)","110.36(c)(8)","110.36(c)(9)"],"items":["Reading: comprehension and inference on complex paired passages","Literary elements and author's-craft question review","Revising and editing passages in STAAR format","Short constructed response (SCR) practice and scoring","Extended constructed response (ECR) essay practice","Full-length practice EOC and data-driven reteach"]},{"unit":"Post-EOC: Media Literacy & Multimodal Project","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(1)","110.36(c)(7)","110.36(c)(8)"],"items":["Analyzing purpose and craft in media (ads, film, digital texts)","Rhetoric of visual and multimodal texts","Creating a multimodal argument or PSA","Collaborative production and oral presentation"]},{"unit":"Independent Reading Showcase & Book Talks","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.36(c)(1)","110.36(c)(3)","110.36(c)(5)"],"items":["Finish self-selected independent reading book and reflection","Prepare and deliver a persuasive book talk","Written reader response and recommendation","Peer sharing in literature circles"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Exam","weeks":1,"kind":"assessment","teks":["110.36(c)(4)","110.36(c)(9)"],"items":["Portfolio compilation and reflection on year-long growth","Comprehensive final-exam review","Final exam administration","Goal-setting and preview of English II"]}]},{"course":"English II","tacSection":"19 TAC §110.37","hasStaarEOC":true,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter110_sec.110.37","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-110"],"strands":[{"code":"110.37(c)(1)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, discussion, and thinking--oral language. The student develops oral language through listening, speaking, and discussion. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"engage in meaningful and respectful discourse by listening actively, responding appropriately, and adjusting communication to audiences and purposes;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"follow and give complex oral instructions to perform specific tasks, answer questions, or solve problems and complex processes;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"give a formal presentation that incorporates a clear thesis and a logical progression of valid evidence from reliable sources and that employs eye contact, speaking rate such as pauses for effect, volume, enunciation, purposeful gestures, and conventions of language to communicate ideas effectively; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"participate collaboratively, building on the ideas of others, contributing relevant information, developing a plan for consensus building, and setting ground rules for decision making."}]},{"code":"110.37(c)(2)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking--vocabulary. The student uses newly acquired vocabulary expressively. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use print or digital resources such as glossaries or technical dictionaries to clarify and validate understanding of the precise and appropriate meaning of technical or discipline-based vocabulary;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze context to distinguish among denotative, connotative, and figurative meanings of words; and"},{"code":"(C)","text":"determine the meaning of foreign words or phrases used frequently in English such as pas de deux, status quo, déjà vu, avant-garde, and coup d'état."}]},{"code":"110.37(c)(3)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking--self-sustained reading. The student reads grade-appropriate texts independently. The student is expected to self-select text and read independently for a sustained period of time.","expectations":[]},{"code":"110.37(c)(4)","statement":"Comprehension skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student uses metacognitive skills to both develop and deepen comprehension of increasingly complex texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"establish purpose for reading assigned and self-selected texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"generate questions about text before, during, and after reading to deepen understanding and gain information;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"make and correct or confirm predictions using text features, characteristics of genre, and structures;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"create mental images to deepen understanding;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"make connections to personal experiences, ideas in other texts, and society;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"make inferences and use evidence to support understanding;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"evaluate details read to determine key ideas;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"synthesize information from multiple texts to create new understanding; and"},{"code":"(I)","text":"monitor comprehension and make adjustments such as re-reading, using background knowledge, asking questions, and annotating when understanding breaks down."}]},{"code":"110.37(c)(5)","statement":"Response skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student responds to an increasingly challenging variety of sources that are read, heard, or viewed. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"describe personal connections to a variety of sources, including self-selected texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"write responses that demonstrate understanding of texts, including comparing texts within and across genres;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"use text evidence and original commentary to support an interpretive response;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"paraphrase and summarize texts in ways that maintain meaning and logical order;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"interact with sources in meaningful ways such as notetaking, annotating, freewriting, or illustrating;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"respond using acquired content and academic vocabulary as appropriate;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"discuss and write about the explicit or implicit meanings of text;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"respond orally or in writing with appropriate register, vocabulary, tone, and voice;"},{"code":"(I)","text":"reflect on and adjust responses when valid evidence warrants; and"},{"code":"(J)","text":"defend or challenge the authors' claims using relevant text evidence."}]},{"code":"110.37(c)(6)","statement":"Multiple genres: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--literary elements. The student recognizes and analyzes literary elements within and across increasingly complex traditional, contemporary, classical, and diverse literary texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"analyze how themes are developed through characterization and plot, including comparing similar themes in a variety of literary texts representing different cultures;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze how authors develop complex yet believable characters, including archetypes, through historical and cultural settings and events;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"analyze isolated scenes and their contribution to the success of the plot as a whole; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"analyze how historical and cultural settings influence characterization, plot, and theme across texts."}]},{"code":"110.37(c)(7)","statement":"Multiple genres: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--genres. The student recognizes and analyzes genre-specific characteristics, structures, and purposes within and across increasingly complex traditional, contemporary, classical, and diverse texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"read and analyze world literature across literary periods;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze the effects of metrics; rhyme schemes; types of rhymes such as end, internal, slant, and eye; and other conventions in poems across a variety of poetic forms;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"analyze the function of dramatic conventions such as asides, soliloquies, dramatic irony, and satire;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"analyze characteristics and structural elements of informational texts such as: (i) clear thesis, relevant supporting evidence, pertinent examples, and conclusion; and (ii) the relationship between organizational design and thesis;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"analyze characteristics and structural elements of argumentative texts such as: (i) clear arguable claim, appeals, and convincing conclusion; (ii) various types of evidence and treatment of counterarguments, including concessions and rebuttals; and (iii) identifiable audience or reader; and"},{"code":"(F)","text":"analyze characteristics of multimodal and digital texts."}]},{"code":"110.37(c)(8)","statement":"Author's purpose and craft: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student uses critical inquiry to analyze the authors' choices and how they influence and communicate meaning within a variety of texts. The student analyzes and applies author's craft purposefully in order to develop his or her own products and performances. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"analyze the author's purpose, audience, and message within a text;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze use of text structure to achieve the author's purpose;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"evaluate the author's use of print and graphic features to achieve specific purposes;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"analyze how the author's use of language informs and shapes the perception of readers;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"analyze the use of literary devices such as irony, sarcasm, and motif to achieve specific purposes;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"analyze how the author's diction and syntax contribute to the mood, voice, and tone of a text; and"},{"code":"(G)","text":"analyze the purpose of rhetorical devices such as appeals, antithesis, parallelism, and shifts and the effects of logical fallacies."}]},{"code":"110.37(c)(9)","statement":"Composition: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--writing process. The student uses the writing process recursively to compose multiple texts that are legible and use appropriate conventions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"plan a piece of writing appropriate for various purposes and audiences by generating ideas through a range of strategies such as brainstorming, journaling, reading, or discussing;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"develop drafts into a focused, structured, and coherent piece of writing in timed and open-ended situations by: (i) using an organizing structure appropriate to purpose, audience, topic, and context; and (ii) developing an engaging idea reflecting depth of thought with specific details, examples, and commentary;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"revise drafts to improve clarity, development, organization, style, diction, and sentence effectiveness, including use of parallel constructions and placement of phrases and dependent clauses;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"edit drafts using standard English conventions, including: (i) a variety of complete, controlled sentences and avoidance of unintentional splices, run-ons, and fragments; (ii) consistent, appropriate use of verb tense and active and passive voice; (iii) pronoun-antecedent agreement; (iv) correct capitalization; (v) punctuation, including commas, semicolons, colons, dashes, and parentheses to set off phrases and clauses as appropriate; and (vi) correct spelling; and"},{"code":"(E)","text":"publish written work for appropriate audiences."}]},{"code":"110.37(c)(10)","statement":"Composition: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--genres. The student uses genre characteristics and craft to compose multiple texts that are meaningful. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"compose literary texts such as fiction and poetry using genre characteristics and craft;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"compose informational texts such as explanatory essays, reports, and personal essays using genre characteristics and craft;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"compose argumentative texts using genre characteristics and craft; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"compose correspondence in a professional or friendly structure."}]},{"code":"110.37(c)(11)","statement":"Inquiry and research: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student engages in both short-term and sustained recursive inquiry processes for a variety of purposes. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"develop questions for formal and informal inquiry;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"critique the research process at each step to implement changes as needs occur and are identified;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"develop and revise a plan;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"modify the major research question as necessary to refocus the research plan;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"locate relevant sources;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"synthesize information from a variety of sources;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"examine sources for: (i) credibility and bias, including omission; and (ii) faulty reasoning such as incorrect premise, hasty generalizations, and either-or;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"display academic citations, including for paraphrased and quoted text, and use source materials ethically to avoid plagiarism; and"},{"code":"(I)","text":"use an appropriate mode of delivery, whether written, oral, or multimodal, to present results."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Launch: Reading, Writing & Discussion Routines","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(1)","110.37(c)(2)","110.37(c)(3)","110.37(c)(4)"],"items":["Establish independent reading (SSR): self-select texts and set sustained reading goals","Set up reader's/writer's notebook and an academic vocabulary log used expressively","Discussion norms: Socratic seminar protocols and accountable-talk stems","Diagnostic on-demand writing sample and reading interest inventory","Metacognitive strategies: annotation, monitoring comprehension, and fix-up moves","Word-study routine: context clues, roots/affixes, and using new words in speech and writing"]},{"unit":"Elements of Fiction: Short Stories","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(6)","110.37(c)(4)","110.37(c)(5)"],"items":["Plot, conflict, and narrative structure in a short story","Characterization: direct vs. indirect and character motivation","Theme: identify and trace a controlling idea across a text","Setting, mood, tone, and point of view analysis","Text-based short constructed response: cite evidence to support analysis","Compare the treatment of a theme across two short stories"]},{"unit":"Personal Narrative Writing Workshop","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(9)","110.37(c)(10)","110.37(c)(8)"],"items":["Generate and plan a personal narrative (prewriting and organization)","Study a mentor text for author's craft, structure, and voice","Draft with narrative craft: scene, dialogue, and sensory detail","Revise for organization, pacing, and coherence","Edit for conventions: sentence boundaries, punctuation, and spelling","Publish, share, and reflect on the recursive writing process"]},{"unit":"Literary Nonfiction & Memoir (Genre Study)","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(7)","110.37(c)(4)","110.37(c)(6)"],"items":["Genre characteristics of memoir and literary nonfiction","Author's perspective and purpose in autobiographical writing","Analyze structure: chronology, flashback, and reflection","Determine central idea and supporting details in nonfiction","Voice and tone in memoir","Written response connecting the text to personal experience"]},{"unit":"Argument & Rhetoric: Analyzing Persuasion","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(8)","110.37(c)(5)","110.37(c)(1)"],"items":["Claim, counterclaim, evidence, and reasoning in argument","Rhetorical appeals: ethos, pathos, and logos","Identify rhetorical devices and analyze their effect on meaning","Evaluate source credibility and detect logical fallacies","Structured academic debate/discussion on a current issue","Respond in writing to a persuasive text with an evaluation of its craft"]},{"unit":"Argumentative Essay Writing Workshop","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(9)","110.37(c)(10)","110.37(c)(11)"],"items":["Develop a debatable thesis and gather relevant evidence","Locate and evaluate credible sources; paraphrase and avoid plagiarism","Organize argument: intro, reasons, counterargument, and conclusion","Draft with clear claims and integrated textual evidence","Revise for logic, transitions, and academic tone","Edit and finalize with basic MLA-style in-text citations"]},{"unit":"The Novel: Extended Reading & Analysis","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(6)","110.37(c)(4)","110.37(c)(3)","110.37(c)(2)"],"items":["Establish a reading schedule and track plot and characters","Analyze dynamic character development across the arc","Trace theme and symbolism across chapters","Academic vocabulary and connotation study drawn from the novel","Socratic seminar on a central question in the text","Analytical paragraph supported with textual evidence","Sustained independent reading connected to unit themes"]},{"unit":"Poetry & Figurative Language","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(6)","110.37(c)(8)","110.37(c)(7)"],"items":["Poetic forms and structural elements (stanza, line, meter)","Figurative language: metaphor, simile, personification, and imagery","Sound devices: rhyme, alliteration, and their effects","How a poet's word choices shape meaning and tone (craft)","Compare a poem with another genre on a shared theme","Write and share an original poem using studied techniques"]},{"unit":"Semester Review & Mid-Year Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["110.37(c)(4)","110.37(c)(6)","110.37(c)(9)"],"items":["Review literary elements and analysis skills from GP1-GP3","Targeted reteach in small groups based on common errors","Timed on-demand analytical writing practice","Mid-year benchmark assessment","Portfolio check-in and second-semester goal setting"]},{"unit":"Drama & Shakespeare Study","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(6)","110.37(c)(7)","110.37(c)(1)"],"items":["Genre characteristics of drama: acts, scenes, stage directions, dialogue","Shakespearean language and reading strategies (paraphrasing)","Dramatic devices: soliloquy, aside, and dramatic irony","Character and conflict analysis across acts","Perform and read a scene aloud with interpretive choices","Analyze a theme and connect it to a modern context","Seminar discussion on a central dramatic question"]},{"unit":"Research Project: The Inquiry Process","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(11)","110.37(c)(9)","110.37(c)(2)"],"items":["Formulate a research question and plan an inquiry","Locate, evaluate, and synthesize multiple credible sources","Take notes, paraphrase, and quote responsibly to avoid plagiarism","Organize findings and develop a working thesis","Draft a research-based product with citations and a works-cited page","Present findings using domain-specific academic vocabulary"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["110.37(c)(5)","110.37(c)(4)"],"items":["Flex days to absorb calendar shifts and catch up on units","Reteach high-need skills identified by formative data","Small-group conferences and guided revision","Formative checkpoint with targeted feedback","Independent reading and vocabulary catch-up"]},{"unit":"Synthesis Across Genres & Multimodal Texts","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(7)","110.37(c)(5)","110.37(c)(4)"],"items":["Compare how a topic is treated across paired genres","Analyze multimodal and media texts and their techniques","Synthesize ideas from multiple sources into a coherent response","Evaluate an author's argument and evidence across texts","Written synthesis connecting a theme across several sources"]},{"unit":"STAAR EOC Review & Practice","weeks":3,"kind":"review","teks":["110.37(c)(4)","110.37(c)(5)","110.37(c)(8)","110.37(c)(9)","110.37(c)(10)"],"items":["Review reading comprehension across fiction, nonfiction, and poetry","Author's craft and rhetorical-analysis practice items","Short constructed response strategy and scoring rubric practice","Extended constructed response (argumentative/analytical) practice","Revising, editing, and language conventions practice","Full-length practice test with data-driven reteach"]},{"unit":"STAAR EOC Testing Window (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"assessment","teks":["110.37(c)(3)","110.37(c)(1)"],"items":["STAAR English II EOC administration (reading and writing)","Independent reading during non-testing blocks","Low-stakes discussion and reflection","Make-up testing support and logistics"]},{"unit":"After-EOC: Multimodal & Creative Projects","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.37(c)(10)","110.37(c)(11)","110.37(c)(1)"],"items":["Choose a creative or multimodal project (podcast, video, or zine)","Apply research and inquiry to a self-selected topic","Compose in a chosen genre with attention to audience and purpose","Collaborate and rehearse for presentation","Peer feedback and public-speaking practice"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review, Portfolio & Final","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["110.37(c)(9)","110.37(c)(5)","110.37(c)(3)"],"items":["Compile and reflect on a year-long writing portfolio","Revise a selected piece to demonstrate growth","Final exam: reading analysis and on-demand writing","Independent reading celebration and student book talks","Reflect on year-long goals and set summer reading plans"]}]},{"course":"English III","tacSection":"19 TAC §110.38","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter110_sec.110.38","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-110"],"strands":[{"code":"110.38(c)(1)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, discussion, and thinking--oral language. The student develops oral language through listening, speaking, and discussion. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"engage in meaningful and respectful discourse when evaluating the clarity and coherence of a speaker's message and critiquing the impact of a speaker's use of diction and syntax;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"follow and give complex instructions, clarify meaning by asking pertinent questions, and respond appropriately;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"give a formal presentation that exhibits a logical structure, smooth transitions, accurate evidence, well-chosen details, and rhetorical devices and that employs eye contact, speaking rate such as pauses for effect, volume, enunciation, purposeful gestures, and conventions of language to communicate ideas effectively; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"participate collaboratively, offering ideas or judgments that are purposeful in moving the team toward goals, asking relevant and insightful questions, tolerating a range of positions and ambiguity in decision making, and evaluating the work of the group based on agreed-upon criteria."}]},{"code":"110.38(c)(2)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking--vocabulary. The student uses newly acquired vocabulary expressively. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use print or digital resources to clarify and validate understanding of multiple meanings of advanced vocabulary;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze context to draw conclusions about nuanced meanings such as in imagery; and"},{"code":"(C)","text":"determine the meaning of foreign words or phrases used frequently in English such as ad hoc, faux pas, non sequitur, and modus operandi."}]},{"code":"110.38(c)(3)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking--self-sustained reading. The student reads grade-appropriate texts independently. The student is expected to self-select text and read independently for a sustained period of time.","expectations":[]},{"code":"110.38(c)(4)","statement":"Comprehension skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student uses metacognitive skills to both develop and deepen comprehension of increasingly complex texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"establish purpose for reading assigned and self-selected texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"generate questions about text before, during, and after reading to deepen understanding and gain information;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"make and correct or confirm predictions using text features, characteristics of genre, and structures;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"create mental images to deepen understanding;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"make connections to personal experiences, ideas in other texts, and society;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"make inferences and use evidence to support understanding;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"evaluate details read to understand key ideas;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"synthesize information from a variety of text types to create new understanding; and"},{"code":"(I)","text":"monitor comprehension and make adjustments such as re-reading, using background knowledge, asking questions, annotating, and using outside sources when understanding breaks down."}]},{"code":"110.38(c)(5)","statement":"Response skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student responds to an increasingly challenging variety of sources that are read, heard, or viewed. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"describe personal connections to a variety of sources, including self-selected texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"write responses that demonstrate analysis of texts, including comparing texts within and across genres;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"use text evidence and original commentary to support an analytic response;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"paraphrase and summarize texts in ways that maintain meaning and logical order;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"interact with sources in meaningful ways such as notetaking, annotating, freewriting, or illustrating;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"respond using acquired content and academic vocabulary as appropriate;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"discuss and write about the explicit and implicit meanings of text;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"respond orally or in writing with appropriate register and effective vocabulary, tone, and voice;"},{"code":"(I)","text":"reflect on and adjust responses when valid evidence warrants; and"},{"code":"(J)","text":"defend or challenge the authors' claims using relevant text evidence."}]},{"code":"110.38(c)(6)","statement":"Multiple genres: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--literary elements. The student recognizes and analyzes literary elements within and across increasingly complex traditional, contemporary, classical, and diverse literary texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"analyze relationships among thematic development, characterization, point of view, significance of setting, and plot in a variety of literary texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze how characters' behaviors and underlying motivations contribute to moral dilemmas that influence the plot and theme;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"evaluate how different literary elements shape the author's portrayal of the plot; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"analyze how the historical, social, and economic context of setting(s) influences the plot, characterization, and theme."}]},{"code":"110.38(c)(7)","statement":"Multiple genres: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--genres. The student recognizes and analyzes genre-specific characteristics, structures, and purposes within and across increasingly complex traditional, contemporary, classical, and diverse texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"read and analyze American literature across literary periods;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze relationships among characteristics of poetry, including stanzas, line breaks, speaker, and sound devices in poems across a variety of poetic forms;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"analyze how the relationships among dramatic elements advance the plot;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"analyze characteristics and structural elements of informational texts such as: (i) clear thesis, strong supporting evidence, pertinent examples, commentary, summary, and conclusion; and (ii) the relationship between organizational design and author's purpose;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"analyze characteristics and structural elements of argumentative texts such as: (i) clear arguable thesis, appeals, structure of the argument, convincing conclusion, and call to action; (ii) various types of evidence and treatment of counterarguments, including concessions and rebuttals; and (iii) identifiable audience or reader; and"},{"code":"(F)","text":"analyze the effectiveness of characteristics of multimodal and digital texts."}]},{"code":"110.38(c)(8)","statement":"Author's purpose and craft: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student uses critical inquiry to analyze the authors' choices and how they influence and communicate meaning within a variety of texts. The student analyzes and applies author's craft purposefully in order to develop his or her own products and performances. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"analyze the author's purpose, audience, and message within a text;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"evaluate use of text structure to achieve the author's purpose;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"evaluate the author's use of print and graphic features to achieve specific purposes;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"evaluate how the author's use of language informs and shapes the perception of readers;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"evaluate the use of literary devices such as paradox, satire, and allegory to achieve specific purposes;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"evaluate how the author's diction and syntax contribute to the mood, voice, and tone of a text; and"},{"code":"(G)","text":"analyze the effects of rhetorical devices and logical fallacies on the way the text is read and understood."}]},{"code":"110.38(c)(9)","statement":"Composition: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--writing process. The student uses the writing process recursively to compose multiple texts that are legible and use appropriate conventions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"plan a piece of writing appropriate for various purposes and audiences by generating ideas through a range of strategies such as brainstorming, journaling, reading, or discussing;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"develop drafts into a focused, structured, and coherent piece of writing in timed and open-ended situations by: (i) using strategic organizational structures appropriate to purpose, audience, topic, and context; and (ii) developing an engaging idea reflecting depth of thought with effective use of rhetorical devices, details, examples, and commentary;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"revise drafts to improve clarity, development, organization, style, diction, and sentence fluency, both within and between sentences;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"edit drafts to demonstrate a command of standard English conventions using a style guide as appropriate; and"},{"code":"(E)","text":"publish written work for appropriate audiences."}]},{"code":"110.38(c)(10)","statement":"Composition: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--genres. The student uses genre characteristics and craft to compose multiple texts that are meaningful. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"compose literary texts such as fiction and poetry using genre characteristics and craft;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"compose informational texts such as explanatory essays, reports, resumes, and personal essays using genre characteristics and craft;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"compose argumentative texts using genre characteristics and craft;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"compose correspondence in a professional or friendly structure;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"compose literary analysis using genre characteristics and craft; and"},{"code":"(F)","text":"compose rhetorical analysis using genre characteristics and craft."}]},{"code":"110.38(c)(11)","statement":"Inquiry and research: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student engages in both short-term and sustained recursive inquiry processes for a variety of purposes. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"develop questions for formal and informal inquiry;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"critique the research process at each step to implement changes as needs occur and are identified;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"develop and revise a plan;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"modify the major research question as necessary to refocus the research plan;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"locate relevant sources;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"synthesize information from a variety of sources;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"examine sources for: (i) credibility, bias, and accuracy; and (ii) faulty reasoning such as post hoc-ad hoc, circular reasoning, red herring, and assumptions;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"display academic citations, including for paraphrased and quoted text, and use source materials ethically to avoid plagiarism; and"},{"code":"(I)","text":"use an appropriate mode of delivery, whether written, oral, or multimodal, to present results."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Launch: Academic Discourse & Independent Reading","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(1)","110.38(c)(2)","110.38(c)(3)"],"items":["Establish Socratic seminar and collaborative discussion norms","Launch self-selected independent reading with reading logs and goals","Vocabulary routines: roots, affixes, context clues, denotation vs. connotation","Diagnostic on-demand argumentative writing sample","Active-listening and note-taking protocols; goal-setting conferences"]},{"unit":"American Origins: Native Voices & Colonial Belief","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(6)","110.38(c)(7)","110.38(c)(4)"],"items":["Native American origin myths and the oral storytelling tradition","Puritan worldview: Bradstreet's poetry and Winthrop's \"City upon a Hill\"","Jonathan Edwards, \"Sinners in the Hands of an Angry God\"","Identifying theme, cultural context, and universal ideas","Genre features of myth, sermon, and personal narrative; close annotation"]},{"unit":"Rhetoric of Revolution","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(8)","110.38(c)(5)","110.38(c)(1)"],"items":["Franklin's aphorisms and excerpts from the Autobiography","The Declaration of Independence: structure and purpose","Patrick Henry, \"Speech to the Virginia Convention\"","Rhetorical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) and persuasive devices","Structured academic debate responding to founding arguments"]},{"unit":"Argument in Writing: The Rhetorical Analysis Essay","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(9)","110.38(c)(10)","110.38(c)(2)"],"items":["The recursive writing process: plan, draft, revise, edit, publish","Crafting an arguable thesis and defensible claims","Embedding, citing, and analyzing textual evidence","Organizing a rhetorical analysis (SOAPSTone / claim-evidence-reasoning)","Editing for grammar, sentence variety, and precise academic diction"]},{"unit":"Romanticism, Transcendentalism & the Dark Romantics","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(6)","110.38(c)(8)","110.38(c)(3)"],"items":["Emerson, \"Self-Reliance,\" and the Transcendentalist ideal","Thoreau, \"Walden\" and \"Civil Disobedience\"","Dark Romanticism: Poe's \"The Raven\" and a short story","Hawthorne: symbolism, allegory, and the gothic","Figurative language, symbol, and tone as authorial craft","Connecting individualism and nature to independent reading choices"]},{"unit":"A Nation Divided: Slavery, Freedom & the Civil War","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(7)","110.38(c)(5)","110.38(c)(1)"],"items":["Frederick Douglass, Narrative of the Life (excerpts)","Sojourner Truth, \"Ain't I a Woman?\"","Lincoln's Gettysburg Address and Second Inaugural Address","Slave narrative and speech as genres; primary vs. secondary sources","Responding to and discussing multiple perspectives on freedom"]},{"unit":"Regionalism & Realism","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(6)","110.38(c)(4)","110.38(c)(8)"],"items":["Mark Twain: dialect, humor, and satire (excerpts)","Kate Chopin, \"The Story of an Hour\"","Realism and regionalism vs. Romantic idealism","Irony, satire, and social critique as authorial choices","Tracking narrator perspective, tone, and characterization"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Benchmark (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["110.38(c)(4)","110.38(c)(9)","110.38(c)(2)"],"items":["Cumulative literary- and rhetorical-analysis review","Timed on-demand writing practice scored with rubrics","Vocabulary review and academic-word application","Midyear benchmark assessment","Error analysis, reflection, and targeted reteach"]},{"unit":"The Modern American Voice: Harlem Renaissance & Modernism","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(6)","110.38(c)(7)","110.38(c)(8)"],"items":["Langston Hughes and the poetry of the Harlem Renaissance","Zora Neale Hurston and voice/dialect in fiction","Fitzgerald, The Great Gatsby (core excerpts and themes)","Modernist fragmentation, imagery, and disillusionment","Poetic form, sound devices, and motif tracking"]},{"unit":"Research & Inquiry: The American Dream Investigation","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(11)","110.38(c)(9)","110.38(c)(10)"],"items":["Developing a focused research question and inquiry plan","Evaluating source credibility and bias; avoiding plagiarism","Note-taking, paraphrasing, and synthesizing multiple sources","MLA in-text citation and works-cited formatting","Drafting and revising a documented argumentative research paper","Presenting research findings to an audience"]},{"unit":"Drama & the American Stage","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(6)","110.38(c)(7)","110.38(c)(1)"],"items":["Arthur Miller, The Crucible (full play)","Dramatic structure, dialogue, and stage directions","Allegory and historical context: Salem and McCarthyism","Character motivation, conflict, and the tragic arc","Readers-theater performance and oral interpretation"]},{"unit":"Contemporary & Diverse American Voices","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(5)","110.38(c)(6)","110.38(c)(3)"],"items":["Contemporary short fiction and poetry from diverse authors","Comparing themes and perspectives across multiple texts","Analyzing multimodal and media texts (spoken word, film clip, essay)","Independent reading synthesis and book talks","Response essay comparing an anchor text with a self-selected text"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["110.38(c)(4)","110.38(c)(5)"],"items":["Flexible reteach of skills flagged by recent data","Independent reading catch-up and one-on-one conferences","Make-up assessments and revision recovery","Targeted skill mini-lessons (evidence, commentary, conventions)","Low-stakes formative check and goal reset"]},{"unit":"Narrative & Personal Voice: The College/Personal Essay","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.38(c)(10)","110.38(c)(9)","110.38(c)(8)"],"items":["Narrative craft: scene, sensory detail, dialogue, and reflection","Mentor memoirs and personal-essay models","Drafting a college-application or personal narrative essay","Revising for authentic voice, style, and syntax","Peer-review workshop and editing conferences"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review, Portfolio & Final (wiggle)","weeks":2,"kind":"assessment","teks":["110.38(c)(9)","110.38(c)(11)","110.38(c)(5)"],"items":["Compiling and reflecting on a year-long writing portfolio","Independent reading capstone and final book talk","Cumulative final exam: reading analysis and writing","Culminating Socratic seminar on year-long themes","Presentation of portfolio reflection and celebration of growth"]}]},{"course":"English IV","tacSection":"19 TAC §110.39","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter110_sec.110.39","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-110"],"strands":[{"code":"110.39(c)(1)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, discussion, and thinking--oral language. The student develops oral language through listening, speaking, and discussion. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"engage in meaningful and respectful discourse when evaluating the clarity and coherence of a speaker's message and critiquing the impact of a speaker's use of diction, syntax, and rhetorical strategies;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"follow and give complex instructions, clarify meaning by asking pertinent questions, and respond appropriately;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"formulate sound arguments and present using elements of classical speeches such as introduction, first and second transitions, body, conclusion, the art of persuasion, rhetorical devices, employing eye contact, speaking rate such as pauses for effect, volume, enunciation, purposeful gestures, and conventions of language to communicate ideas effectively; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"participate collaboratively, offering ideas or judgments that are purposeful in moving the team toward goals, asking relevant and insightful questions, tolerating a range of positions and ambiguity in decision making, and evaluating the work of the group based on agreed-upon criteria."}]},{"code":"110.39(c)(2)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking--vocabulary. The student uses newly acquired vocabulary expressively. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use print or digital resources to clarify and validate understanding of multiple meanings of advanced vocabulary;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze context to draw conclusions about nuanced meanings such as in imagery; and"},{"code":"(C)","text":"determine the meaning of foreign words or phrases used frequently in English such as ad nauseum, in loco parentis, laissez-faire, and caveat emptor."}]},{"code":"110.39(c)(3)","statement":"Developing and sustaining foundational language skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking--self-sustained reading. The student reads grade-appropriate texts independently. The student is expected to self-select text and read independently for a sustained period of time.","expectations":[]},{"code":"110.39(c)(4)","statement":"Comprehension skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student uses metacognitive skills to both develop and deepen comprehension of increasingly complex texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"establish purpose for reading assigned and self-selected texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"generate questions about text before, during, and after reading to deepen understanding and gain information;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"make and correct or confirm predictions using text features, characteristics of genre, and structures;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"create mental images to deepen understanding;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"make connections to personal experiences, ideas in other texts, and society;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"make inferences and use evidence to support understanding;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"evaluate details read to analyze key ideas;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"synthesize information from a variety of text types to create new understanding; and"},{"code":"(I)","text":"monitor comprehension and make adjustments such as re-reading, using background knowledge, asking questions, annotating, and using outside sources when understanding breaks down."}]},{"code":"110.39(c)(5)","statement":"Response skills: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student responds to an increasingly challenging variety of sources that are read, heard, or viewed. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"describe personal connections to a variety of sources, including self-selected texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"write responses that demonstrate analysis of texts, including comparing texts within and across genres;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"use text evidence and original commentary to support an evaluative response;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"paraphrase and summarize texts in ways that maintain meaning and logical order;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"interact with sources in meaningful ways such as notetaking, annotating, freewriting, or illustrating;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"respond using acquired content and academic vocabulary as appropriate;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"discuss and write about the explicit and implicit meanings of text;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"respond orally or in writing with appropriate register and purposeful vocabulary, tone, and voice;"},{"code":"(I)","text":"reflect on and adjust responses when valid evidence warrants; and"},{"code":"(J)","text":"defend or challenge the authors' claims using relevant text evidence."}]},{"code":"110.39(c)(6)","statement":"Multiple genres: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--literary elements. The student recognizes and analyzes literary elements within and across increasingly complex traditional, contemporary, classical, and diverse literary texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"analyze relationships among thematic development, characterization, point of view, significance of setting, and plot in a variety of literary texts;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze how characters' behaviors and underlying motivations contribute to moral dilemmas that influence the plot and theme;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"critique and evaluate how complex plot structures such as subplots contribute to and advance the action; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"evaluate how the historical, social, and economic context of setting(s) influences the plot, characterization, and theme."}]},{"code":"110.39(c)(7)","statement":"Multiple genres: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--genres. The student recognizes and analyzes genre-specific characteristics, structures, and purposes within and across increasingly complex traditional, contemporary, classical, and diverse texts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"read and analyze British literature across literary periods;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze the effects of sound, form, figurative language, graphics, and dramatic structure in poetry across literary time periods and cultures;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"analyze and evaluate how the relationships among the dramatic elements advance the plot;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"critique and evaluate characteristics and structural elements of informational texts such as: (i) clear thesis, effective supporting evidence, pertinent examples, commentary, summary, and conclusion; and (ii) the relationship between organizational design and author's purpose;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"critique and evaluate characteristics and structural elements of argumentative texts such as: (i) clear arguable thesis, appeals, structure of the argument, convincing conclusion, and call to action; (ii) various types of evidence and treatment of counterarguments, including concessions and rebuttals; and (iii) identifiable audience or reader; and"},{"code":"(F)","text":"critique and evaluate the effectiveness of characteristics of multimodal and digital texts."}]},{"code":"110.39(c)(8)","statement":"Author's purpose and craft: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student uses critical inquiry to analyze the authors' choices and how they influence and communicate meaning within a variety of texts. The student analyzes and applies author's craft purposefully in order to develop his or her own products and performances. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"evaluate the author's purpose, audience, and message within a text;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"evaluate use of text structure to achieve the author's purpose;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"evaluate the author's use of print and graphic features to achieve specific purposes;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"critique and evaluate how the author's use of language informs and shapes the perception of readers;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"evaluate the use of literary devices such as paradox, satire, and allegory to achieve specific purposes;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"evaluate how the author's diction and syntax contribute to the effectiveness of a text; and"},{"code":"(G)","text":"analyze the effects of rhetorical devices and logical fallacies on the way the text is read and understood."}]},{"code":"110.39(c)(9)","statement":"Composition: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--writing process. The student uses the writing process recursively to compose multiple texts that are legible and use appropriate conventions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"plan a piece of writing appropriate for various purposes and audiences by generating ideas through a range of strategies such as brainstorming, journaling, reading, or discussing;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"develop drafts into a focused, structured, and coherent piece of writing in timed and open-ended situations by: (i) using strategic organizational structures appropriate to purpose, audience, topic, and context; and (ii) developing an engaging idea reflecting depth of thought with effective use of rhetorical devices, details, examples, and commentary;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"revise drafts to improve clarity, development, organization, style, diction, and sentence fluency, both within and between sentences;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"edit drafts to demonstrate a command of standard English conventions using a style guide as appropriate; and"},{"code":"(E)","text":"publish written work for appropriate audiences."}]},{"code":"110.39(c)(10)","statement":"Composition: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts--genres. The student uses genre characteristics and craft to compose multiple texts that are meaningful. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"compose literary texts such as fiction and poetry using genre characteristics and craft;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"compose informational texts such as explanatory essays, reports, resumes, and personal essays using genre characteristics and craft;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"compose argumentative texts using genre characteristics and craft;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"compose correspondence in a professional or friendly structure;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"compose literary analysis using genre characteristics and craft; and"},{"code":"(F)","text":"compose rhetorical analysis using genre characteristics and craft."}]},{"code":"110.39(c)(11)","statement":"Inquiry and research: listening, speaking, reading, writing, and thinking using multiple texts. The student engages in both short-term and sustained recursive inquiry processes for a variety of purposes. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"develop questions for formal and informal inquiry;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"critique the research process at each step to implement changes as needs occur and are identified;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"develop and revise a plan;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"modify the major research question as necessary to refocus the research plan;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"locate relevant sources;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"synthesize information from a variety of sources;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"examine sources for: (i) credibility, bias, and accuracy; and (ii) faulty reasoning such as straw man, false dilemma, faulty analogies, and non-sequitur;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"display academic citations, including for paraphrased and quoted text, and use source materials ethically to avoid plagiarism; and"},{"code":"(I)","text":"use an appropriate mode of delivery, whether written, oral, or multimodal, to present results."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Course Launch: Reading, Discussion & Close-Analysis Foundations","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(1)","110.39(c)(3)","110.39(c)(4)"],"items":["Establish discussion norms, Socratic-seminar protocols, and academic listening/speaking routines","Launch self-sustained reading: self-select a text and set a sustained silent-reading routine + reading log","Annotation and close-reading strategies for increasingly complex texts","Metacognitive monitoring: questioning, fix-up strategies, and think-alouds","Diagnostic writing sample and year-long goal setting"]},{"unit":"Anglo-Saxon Epic: Beowulf & the Heroic Code","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(6)","110.39(c)(2)","110.39(c)(5)"],"items":["Anglo-Saxon context, oral tradition, and the epic as a form","Trace the epic hero and the heroic code through Beowulf","Analyze literary elements: kennings, alliteration, epithets, and tone","Vocabulary study: archaic/Old-English-rooted terms used expressively in talk and writing","Written response comparing Beowulf's values to a modern hero"]},{"unit":"Medieval Frame Narrative: The Canterbury Tales","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(7)","110.39(c)(8)","110.39(c)(1)"],"items":["Medieval England, pilgrimage, and the frame-narrative structure","Analyze characterization and estates satire in the General Prologue","Chaucer's craft: irony, tone, and social commentary","Compare two tales for genre-specific features and purpose","Small-group discussion: perform and analyze a pilgrim's character"]},{"unit":"The Argumentative Essay: Rhetoric & Persuasion","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(9)","110.39(c)(8)","110.39(c)(5)"],"items":["Elements of argument: claim, evidence, reasoning, counterclaim, rebuttal","Rhetorical appeals (ethos/pathos/logos) and rhetorical devices","Analyze mentor argument texts and respond to competing sources","Draft, peer-review, and revise a thesis-driven argumentative essay","Edit for conventions, style, and integration of cited evidence","Deliver and defend a claim in a structured debate"]},{"unit":"Inquiry & Research: The MLA Research Paper","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(11)","110.39(c)(9)","110.39(c)(2)"],"items":["Develop and refine a research question through recursive inquiry","Locate, evaluate, and annotate credible primary and secondary sources","Note-taking, paraphrasing, quoting, and avoiding plagiarism","MLA in-text citation and Works Cited formatting","Draft, synthesize sources, and revise the research paper recursively","Use discipline-specific research vocabulary purposefully in writing"]},{"unit":"Shakespearean Tragedy: Macbeth","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(6)","110.39(c)(7)","110.39(c)(1)"],"items":["Shakespearean tragedy: conventions, the tragic hero, and dramatic structure","Analyze character, ambition, and the arc of Macbeth and Lady Macbeth","Literary elements: soliloquy, dramatic irony, and motif (blood, sleep)","Blank verse, figurative language, and close reading of key speeches","Perform scenes aloud and discuss staging and interpretation"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["110.39(c)(4)","110.39(c)(5)"],"items":["Reteach targeted skills from data (comprehension, response, or citation)","Small-group conferencing and revision workshop","Common-assessment retake or performance task","Independent-reading check-in and flexible catch-up day"]},{"unit":"Renaissance & Metaphysical Poetry","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(8)","110.39(c)(2)","110.39(c)(6)"],"items":["Renaissance context; the sonnet (Shakespearean vs. Petrarchan)","Metaphysical conceit and wit in Donne and Marvell","Analyze author's craft: paradox, extended metaphor, and structure","Vocabulary of poetic form and figurative language","Write an analytical paragraph on a poem's meaning and craft"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Synthesis (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["110.39(c)(4)","110.39(c)(9)"],"items":["Semester synthesis: connect texts from Anglo-Saxon through the Renaissance","Review comprehension and analysis strategies with cumulative texts","Timed on-demand analytical writing practice","Portfolio check and reflection on writing growth"]},{"unit":"Enlightenment Satire: Swift & Pope","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(8)","110.39(c)(7)","110.39(c)(5)"],"items":["Enlightenment/Neoclassical context and the purpose of satire","Analyze verbal irony, hyperbole, and understatement in 'A Modest Proposal'","Genre study: satire, mock epic, and the periodical essay","Author's craft: how tone and persona shape argument","Respond by drafting an original short satire on a modern issue"]},{"unit":"Romantic Poetry: Nature, Imagination & the Sublime","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(6)","110.39(c)(2)","110.39(c)(1)"],"items":["Romanticism: nature, imagination, emotion, and the sublime","Close read Wordsworth, Blake, Keats, and Shelley","Literary elements: imagery, symbolism, and speaker/tone","Vocabulary of Romantic ideals used in analytical talk and writing","Poetry discussion circles and dramatic reading"]},{"unit":"Victorian Literature: The Novel & Social Criticism","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(7)","110.39(c)(6)","110.39(c)(5)"],"items":["Victorian era: industrialization, class, and social reform","The novel as a genre; excerpts from Dickens and the Brontes","Analyze theme, characterization, and social criticism","Compare Victorian concerns with contemporary issues","Written response evaluating an author's social argument"]},{"unit":"Modern & Contemporary British Voices","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(8)","110.39(c)(3)","110.39(c)(4)"],"items":["Modernism and postcolonial/contemporary voices writing in English","Analyze fragmentation, stream of consciousness, and shifting perspective","Author's craft across modern poetry and short fiction","Independent-reading connection: apply analysis to a self-selected text","Metacognitive discussion of how form shapes meaning"]},{"unit":"Multimodal Media Analysis & Response","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(5)","110.39(c)(1)"],"items":["Analyze and respond to film, speech, and digital/visual texts","Rhetoric of image, sound, and design","Compare a print text with its multimodal adaptation","Deliver a short multimodal response and presentation"]},{"unit":"Senior Capstone: Multi-Genre Project","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(10)","110.39(c)(11)","110.39(c)(9)"],"items":["Select a theme and conduct sustained inquiry to gather sources","Compose across genres (argument, narrative, poem, informational) on one theme","Apply genre characteristics and craft purposefully to each piece","Draft, workshop, and revise the multi-genre project recursively","Design and rehearse a formal presentation of the project"]},{"unit":"Independent Reading Showcase & Book Talks","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["110.39(c)(3)","110.39(c)(1)","110.39(c)(2)"],"items":["Finish a self-selected independent-reading text","Prepare and deliver a book talk and recommendation","Vocabulary-in-context share-out from independent reading","Reflective discussion on year-long reading habits and next steps"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Portfolio","weeks":2,"kind":"assessment","teks":["110.39(c)(9)","110.39(c)(10)","110.39(c)(4)"],"items":["Cumulative review of literary movements and analysis skills","Assemble and reflect on a year-long writing portfolio","Final on-demand analytical and argument writing across genres","Comprehensive final exam / culminating performance assessment","Course reflection and post-secondary reading/writing goals"]}]},{"course":"Biology","tacSection":"19 TAC §112.42 (Biology, One Credit, Adopted 2020), Subchapter C (High School), Chapter 112 (TEKS for Science)","hasStaarEOC":true,"sources":["https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-112","https://tea.texas.gov/laws-and-rules/sboe-rules-tac/sboe-tac-currently-effect/ch112c-0.pdf","http://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter112_sec.112.34"],"strands":[{"code":"112.42(c)(1)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student, for at least 40% of instructional time, asks questions, identifies problems, and plans and safely conducts classroom, laboratory, and field investigations to answer questions, explain phenomena, or design solutions using appropriate tools and models. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"ask questions and define problems based on observations or information from text, phenomena, models, or investigations;"},{"code":"B","text":"apply scientific practices to plan and conduct descriptive, comparative, and experimental investigations and use engineering practices to design solutions to problems;"},{"code":"C","text":"use appropriate safety equipment and practices during laboratory, classroom, and field investigations as outlined in Texas Education Agency-approved safety standards;"},{"code":"D","text":"use appropriate tools such as microscopes, slides, Petri dishes, laboratory glassware, metric rulers, digital balances, pipets, filter paper, micropipettes, gel electrophoresis and polymerase chain reaction (PCR) apparatuses, microcentrifuges, water baths, incubators, thermometers, hot plates, data collection probes, test tube holders, lab notebooks or journals, hand lenses, and models, diagrams, or samples of biological specimens or structures;"},{"code":"E","text":"collect quantitative data using the International System of Units (SI) and qualitative data as evidence;"},{"code":"F","text":"organize quantitative and qualitative data using scatter plots, line graphs, bar graphs, charts, data tables, digital tools, diagrams, scientific drawings, and student-prepared models;"},{"code":"G","text":"develop and use models to represent phenomena, systems, processes, or solutions to engineering problems; and"},{"code":"H","text":"distinguish among scientific hypotheses, theories, and laws."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(2)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student analyzes and interprets data to derive meaning, identify features and patterns, and discover relationships or correlations to develop evidence-based arguments or evaluate designs. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify advantages and limitations of models such as their size, scale, properties, and materials;"},{"code":"B","text":"analyze data by identifying significant statistical features, patterns, sources of error, and limitations;"},{"code":"C","text":"use mathematical calculations to assess quantitative relationships in data; and"},{"code":"D","text":"evaluate experimental and engineering designs."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(3)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student develops evidence-based explanations and communicates findings, conclusions, and proposed solutions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"develop explanations and propose solutions supported by data and models and consistent with scientific ideas, principles, and theories;"},{"code":"B","text":"communicate explanations and solutions individually and collaboratively in a variety of settings and formats; and"},{"code":"C","text":"engage respectfully in scientific argumentation using applied scientific explanations and empirical evidence."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(4)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student knows the contributions of scientists and recognizes the importance of scientific research and innovation on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze, evaluate, and critique scientific explanations and solutions by using empirical evidence, logical reasoning, and experimental and observational testing, so as to encourage critical thinking by the student;"},{"code":"B","text":"relate the impact of past and current research on scientific thought and society, including research methodology, cost-benefit analysis, and contributions of diverse scientists as related to the content; and"},{"code":"C","text":"research and explore resources such as museums, libraries, professional organizations, private companies, online platforms, and mentors employed in a science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) field in order to investigate STEM careers."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(5)","statement":"Science concepts--biological structures, functions, and processes. The student knows that biological structures at multiple levels of organization perform specific functions and processes that affect life. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"relate the functions of different types of biomolecules, including carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, and nucleic acids, to the structure and function of a cell;"},{"code":"B","text":"compare and contrast prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells, including their complexity, and compare and contrast scientific explanations for cellular complexity;"},{"code":"C","text":"investigate homeostasis through the cellular transport of molecules; and"},{"code":"D","text":"compare the structures of viruses to cells and explain how viruses spread and cause disease."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(6)","statement":"Science concepts--biological structures, functions, and processes. The student knows how an organism grows and the importance of cell differentiation. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"explain the importance of the cell cycle to the growth of organisms, including an overview of the stages of the cell cycle and deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) replication models;"},{"code":"B","text":"explain the process of cell specialization through cell differentiation, including the role of environmental factors; and"},{"code":"C","text":"relate disruptions of the cell cycle to how they lead to the development of diseases such as cancer."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(7)","statement":"Science concepts--mechanisms of genetics. The student knows the role of nucleic acids in gene expression. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify components of DNA, explain how the nucleotide sequence specifies some traits of an organism, and examine scientific explanations for the origin of DNA;"},{"code":"B","text":"describe the significance of gene expression and explain the process of protein synthesis using models of DNA and ribonucleic acid (RNA);"},{"code":"C","text":"identify and illustrate changes in DNA and evaluate the significance of these changes; and"},{"code":"D","text":"discuss the importance of molecular technologies such as polymerase chain reaction (PCR), gel electrophoresis, and genetic engineering that are applicable in current research and engineering practices."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(8)","statement":"Science concepts--mechanisms of genetics. The student knows the role of nucleic acids and the principles of inheritance and variation of traits in Mendelian and non-Mendelian genetics. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze the significance of chromosome reduction, independent assortment, and crossing-over during meiosis in increasing diversity in populations of organisms that reproduce sexually; and"},{"code":"B","text":"predict possible outcomes of various genetic combinations using monohybrid and dihybrid crosses, including non-Mendelian traits of incomplete dominance, codominance, sex-linked traits, and multiple alleles."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(9)","statement":"Science concepts--biological evolution. The student knows evolutionary theory is a scientific explanation for the unity and diversity of life that has multiple lines of evidence. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze and evaluate how evidence of common ancestry among groups is provided by the fossil record, biogeography, and homologies, including anatomical, molecular, and developmental; and"},{"code":"B","text":"examine scientific explanations for varying rates of change such as gradualism, abrupt appearance, and stasis in the fossil record."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(10)","statement":"Science concepts--biological evolution. The student knows evolutionary theory is a scientific explanation for the unity and diversity of life that has multiple mechanisms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze and evaluate how natural selection produces change in populations and not in individuals;"},{"code":"B","text":"analyze and evaluate how the elements of natural selection, including inherited variation, the potential of a population to produce more offspring than can survive, and a finite supply of environmental resources, result in differential reproductive success;"},{"code":"C","text":"analyze and evaluate how natural selection may lead to speciation; and"},{"code":"D","text":"analyze evolutionary mechanisms other than natural selection, including genetic drift, gene flow, mutation, and genetic recombination, and their effect on the gene pool of a population."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(11)","statement":"Science concepts--biological structures, functions, and processes. The student knows the significance of matter cycling, energy flow, and enzymes in living organisms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"explain how matter is conserved and energy is transferred during photosynthesis and cellular respiration using models, including the chemical equations for these processes; and"},{"code":"B","text":"investigate and explain the role of enzymes in facilitating cellular processes."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(12)","statement":"Science concepts--biological structures, functions, and processes. The student knows that multicellular organisms are composed of multiple systems that interact to perform complex functions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze the interactions that occur among systems that perform the functions of regulation, nutrient absorption, reproduction, and defense from injury or illness in animals; and"},{"code":"B","text":"explain how the interactions that occur among systems that perform functions of transport, reproduction, and response in plants are facilitated by their structures."}]},{"code":"112.42(c)(13)","statement":"Science concepts--interdependence within environmental systems. The student knows that interactions at various levels of organization occur within an ecosystem to maintain stability. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"investigate and evaluate how ecological relationships, including predation, parasitism, commensalism, mutualism, and competition, influence ecosystem stability;"},{"code":"B","text":"analyze how ecosystem stability is affected by disruptions to the cycling of matter and flow of energy through trophic levels using models;"},{"code":"C","text":"explain the significance of the carbon and nitrogen cycles to ecosystem stability and analyze the consequences of disrupting these cycles; and"},{"code":"D","text":"explain how environmental change, including change due to human activity, affects biodiversity and analyze how changes in biodiversity impact ecosystem stability."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Scientific Practices, Lab Safety & Nature of Biology","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(1)","112.42(c)(2)","112.42(c)(3)","112.42(c)(4)"],"items":["Lab safety contract, SDS sheets, and safe use of lab equipment","Characteristics of life and levels of biological organization","Experimental design: independent/dependent variables, controls, and hypotheses","Metric measurement, building data tables, and constructing/interpreting graphs","Claim-Evidence-Reasoning: writing evidence-based conclusions from data","Contributions of biologists and the impact of scientific innovation on society"]},{"unit":"Chemistry of Life: Biomolecules & Enzymes","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(5)","112.42(c)(11)","112.42(c)(1)"],"items":["Properties of water and their importance to living systems","Carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, and nucleic acids: monomers and functions","Structure-function of macromolecules; dehydration synthesis and hydrolysis","Enzyme structure, active site, and substrate specificity","Enzyme investigation: effects of temperature, pH, and concentration on reaction rate"]},{"unit":"Cell Structure & Function","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(5)","112.42(c)(12)","112.42(c)(4)"],"items":["Cell theory and the history of the microscope (contributions of scientists)","Prokaryotic vs. eukaryotic cells; animal vs. plant cells","Organelle structures and their specific functions","Microscope lab: preparing wet mounts and observing cells","How organelles cooperate as an integrated system within the cell"]},{"unit":"Cell Transport & Homeostasis","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(5)","112.42(c)(12)"],"items":["Cell membrane structure: phospholipid bilayer and fluid mosaic model","Passive transport: diffusion, osmosis, and facilitated diffusion","Active transport, endocytosis, and exocytosis","Osmosis lab: hypertonic, hypotonic, and isotonic solutions","Maintaining homeostasis at the cellular level"]},{"unit":"Energy in Cells: Photosynthesis & Cellular Respiration","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(11)","112.42(c)(2)"],"items":["ATP as the energy currency of the cell","Photosynthesis: reactants, products, and the role of chloroplasts","Cellular respiration: the aerobic pathway and role of mitochondria","Fermentation and anaerobic respiration","Tracing energy flow and matter cycling through both processes","Lab: measuring photosynthesis/respiration rate and analyzing the data"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["112.42(c)(2)","112.42(c)(3)"],"items":["Reteach targeted misconceptions from the cells and energy units","Data-analysis and error-analysis practice stations","Cumulative checkpoint quiz and one-on-one conferencing on CER writing","Flex day to absorb calendar shifts or make up missed labs"]},{"unit":"Cell Cycle, Mitosis & Cell Differentiation","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(6)","112.42(c)(5)"],"items":["Purpose of cell division: growth, repair, and asexual reproduction","Phases of the cell cycle and mitosis (prophase-metaphase-anaphase-telophase)","Cytokinesis and comparison in plant vs. animal cells","Cell-cycle regulation, checkpoints, and uncontrolled growth (cancer)","Cell differentiation, stem cells, and specialized cell types","Onion-root-tip lab: identifying and counting cells in each phase"]},{"unit":"DNA, RNA & Protein Synthesis","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(7)","112.42(c)(1)"],"items":["DNA structure: double helix, nucleotides, and base pairing","DNA replication: the semiconservative model and key enzymes","Transcription: building mRNA from a DNA template","Translation: codons, tRNA, ribosomes, and reading the genetic code","Gene expression and how mutations change proteins and traits","Modeling protein synthesis from gene to functional protein"]},{"unit":"Meiosis, Mendelian & Non-Mendelian Genetics","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(8)","112.42(c)(2)","112.42(c)(3)"],"items":["Meiosis, homologous chromosomes, crossing over, and genetic variation","Mendel's laws of segregation and independent assortment","Monohybrid and dihybrid Punnett squares and probability","Non-Mendelian inheritance: incomplete dominance, codominance, multiple alleles","Sex-linked traits and pedigree analysis","Analyzing genetic-cross data and reasoning about observed vs. expected ratios"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Exam","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["112.42(c)(5)","112.42(c)(6)","112.42(c)(7)","112.42(c)(11)"],"items":["Spiral review: biomolecules, cells, transport, and energy","Spiral review: cell cycle, DNA, protein synthesis, and genetics","Semester-exam study stations with released EOC-style items","Semester exam and reflection/error analysis"]},{"unit":"Evolution: Evidence for Change Over Time","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(9)","112.42(c)(4)"],"items":["Fossil record and geologic time as evidence of change","Comparative anatomy: homologous, analogous, and vestigial structures","Molecular and DNA evidence for common ancestry","Embryological and biogeographical evidence","History of evolutionary thought (Darwin, Wallace, and contemporaries)"]},{"unit":"Mechanisms of Evolution & Natural Selection","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(10)","112.42(c)(2)"],"items":["Natural selection and differential reproductive success","Variation, adaptation, and fitness in populations","Types of selection, isolation, and speciation","Genetic drift, gene flow, and mutation as mechanisms of change","Modeling natural selection and interpreting allele-frequency data"]},{"unit":"Classification & Biodiversity","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(9)","112.42(c)(5)"],"items":["Taxonomy, binomial nomenclature, and the Linnaean hierarchy","Domains and kingdoms of life and their defining characteristics","Cladograms and phylogenetic trees as evidence of relationships","Dichotomous key lab: identifying organisms"]},{"unit":"Human Body Systems & Homeostasis","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(12)","112.42(c)(5)"],"items":["Levels of organization: cells, tissues, organs, and organ systems","Digestive, circulatory, and respiratory system interactions","Nervous and endocrine control and negative feedback loops","Immune, muscular, and skeletal system functions","Homeostasis case studies: how multiple systems interact to maintain stability"]},{"unit":"Ecology: Ecosystems & Interdependence","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.42(c)(13)","112.42(c)(11)","112.42(c)(2)"],"items":["Levels of ecological organization and biotic/abiotic factors","Energy flow: food chains, food webs, and ecological pyramids","Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, and water","Population dynamics, carrying capacity, and limiting factors","Symbiosis, competition, predation, and ecological succession","Human impact, biodiversity loss, and ecosystem stability"]},{"unit":"STAAR EOC Review","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["112.42(c)(5)","112.42(c)(6)","112.42(c)(7)","112.42(c)(8)","112.42(c)(9)","112.42(c)(10)","112.42(c)(11)","112.42(c)(12)","112.42(c)(13)"],"items":["Reporting Category 1 review: cell structure, function, and biomolecules","Reporting Category 2 review: mechanisms of genetics (DNA, protein synthesis, inheritance)","Reporting Category 3 review: biological evolution and classification","Reporting Category 4 review: biological processes and body systems","Reporting Category 5 review: interdependence and ecology","Released STAAR item practice, data-question strategies, and targeted small-group reteach"]},{"unit":"Post-EOC Enrichment & Biology Capstone Projects","weeks":2,"kind":"buffer","teks":["112.42(c)(4)","112.42(c)(13)"],"items":["Biotechnology and bioethics case studies (CRISPR, cloning, genetic testing)","Local El Paso/Chihuahuan Desert ecology field investigation","Student-choice research project on a current biology innovation","Careers in biology and connecting scientists' work to society","Project presentations and peer feedback"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Cumulative Review & Final Exam","weeks":1,"kind":"assessment","teks":["112.42(c)(1)","112.42(c)(2)","112.42(c)(3)"],"items":["Cumulative review stations spanning all six-week grading periods","Practice communicating findings and defending evidence-based claims","Comprehensive final exam","Reflection, portfolio wrap-up, and course close-out"]}],"pacingSource":"CK-12"},{"course":"Chemistry","tacSection":"19 TAC §112.43 (Chemistry, One Credit, Adopted 2020), Subchapter C (High School), Chapter 112 (TEKS for Science)","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-112","https://tea.texas.gov/laws-and-rules/sboe-rules-tac/sboe-tac-currently-effect/ch112c-0.pdf"],"strands":[{"code":"112.43(c)(1)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student, for at least 40% of instructional time, asks questions, identifies problems, and plans and safely conducts classroom, laboratory, and field investigations to answer questions, explain phenomena, or design solutions using appropriate tools and models. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"ask questions and define problems based on observations or information from text, phenomena, models, or investigations;"},{"code":"B","text":"apply scientific practices to plan and conduct descriptive, comparative, and experimental investigations and use engineering practices to design solutions to problems;"},{"code":"C","text":"use appropriate safety equipment and practices during laboratory, classroom, and field investigations as outlined in Texas Education Agency-approved safety standards;"},{"code":"D","text":"use appropriate tools such as Safety Data Sheets (SDS), scientific or graphing calculators, computers and probes, electronic balances, an adequate supply of consumable chemicals, and sufficient scientific glassware such as beakers, Erlenmeyer flasks, pipettes, graduated cylinders, volumetric flasks, and burettes;"},{"code":"E","text":"collect quantitative data using the International System of Units (SI) and qualitative data as evidence;"},{"code":"F","text":"organize quantitative and qualitative data using oral or written lab reports, labeled drawings, particle diagrams, charts, tables, graphs, journals, summaries, or technology-based reports;"},{"code":"G","text":"develop and use models to represent phenomena, systems, processes, or solutions to engineering problems; and"},{"code":"H","text":"distinguish between scientific hypotheses, theories, and laws."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(2)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student analyzes and interprets data to derive meaning, identify features and patterns, and discover relationships or correlations to develop evidence-based arguments or evaluate designs. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify advantages and limitations of models such as their size, scale, properties, and materials;"},{"code":"B","text":"analyze data by identifying significant statistical features, patterns, sources of error, and limitations;"},{"code":"C","text":"use mathematical calculations to assess quantitative relationships in data; and"},{"code":"D","text":"evaluate experimental and engineering designs."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(3)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student develops evidence-based explanations and communicates findings, conclusions, and proposed solutions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"develop explanations and propose solutions supported by data and models and consistent with scientific ideas, principles, and theories;"},{"code":"B","text":"communicate explanations and solutions individually and collaboratively in a variety of settings and formats; and"},{"code":"C","text":"engage respectfully in scientific argumentation using applied scientific explanations and empirical evidence."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(4)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student knows the contributions of scientists and recognizes the importance of scientific research and innovation on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze, evaluate, and critique scientific explanations and solutions by using empirical evidence, logical reasoning, and experimental and observational testing, so as to encourage critical thinking by the student;"},{"code":"B","text":"relate the impact of past and current research on scientific thought and society, including research methodology, cost-benefit analysis, and contributions of diverse scientists as related to the content; and"},{"code":"C","text":"research and explore resources such as museums, libraries, professional organizations, private companies, online platforms, and mentors employed in a science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) field in order to investigate STEM careers."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(5)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands the development of the Periodic Table and applies its predictive power. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"explain the development of the Periodic Table over time using evidence such as chemical and physical properties;"},{"code":"B","text":"predict the properties of elements in chemical families, including alkali metals, alkaline earth metals, halogens, noble gases, and transition metals, based on valence electrons patterns using the Periodic Table; and"},{"code":"C","text":"analyze and interpret elemental data, including atomic radius, atomic mass, electronegativity, ionization energy, and reactivity to identify periodic trends."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(6)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands the development of atomic theory and applies it to real-world phenomena. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"construct models using Dalton's Postulates, Thomson's discovery of electron properties, Rutherford's nuclear atom, Bohr's nuclear atom, and Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle to show the development of modern atomic theory over time;"},{"code":"B","text":"describe the structure of atoms and ions, including the masses, electrical charges, and locations of protons and neutrons in the nucleus and electrons in the electron cloud;"},{"code":"C","text":"investigate the mathematical relationship between energy, frequency, and wavelength of light using the electromagnetic spectrum and relate it to the quantization of energy in the emission spectrum;"},{"code":"D","text":"calculate average atomic mass of an element using isotopic composition; and"},{"code":"E","text":"construct models to express the arrangement of electrons in atoms of representative elements using electron configurations and Lewis dot structures."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(7)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows how atoms form ionic, covalent, and metallic bonds. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"construct an argument to support how periodic trends such as electronegativity can predict bonding between elements;"},{"code":"B","text":"name and write the chemical formulas for ionic and covalent compounds using International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) nomenclature rules;"},{"code":"C","text":"classify and draw electron dot structures for molecules with linear, bent, trigonal planar, trigonal pyramidal, and tetrahedral molecular geometries as explained by Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion (VSEPR) theory; and"},{"code":"D","text":"analyze the properties of ionic, covalent, and metallic substances in terms of intramolecular and intermolecular forces."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(8)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands how matter is accounted for in chemical substances. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"define mole and apply the concept of molar mass to convert between moles and grams;"},{"code":"B","text":"calculate the number of atoms or molecules in a sample of material using Avogadro's number;"},{"code":"C","text":"calculate percent composition of compounds; and"},{"code":"D","text":"differentiate between empirical and molecular formulas."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(9)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands how matter is accounted for in chemical reactions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"interpret, write, and balance chemical equations, including synthesis, decomposition, single replacement, double replacement, and combustion reactions using the law of conservation of mass;"},{"code":"B","text":"differentiate among acid-base reactions, precipitation reactions, and oxidation-reduction reactions;"},{"code":"C","text":"perform stoichiometric calculations, including determination of mass relationships, gas volume relationships, and percent yield; and"},{"code":"D","text":"describe the concept of limiting reactants in a balanced chemical equation."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(10)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands the principles of the kinetic molecular theory and ideal gas behavior. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"describe the postulates of the kinetic molecular theory;"},{"code":"B","text":"describe and calculate the relationships among volume, pressure, number of moles, and temperature for an ideal gas; and"},{"code":"C","text":"define and apply Dalton's law of partial pressure."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(11)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands and can apply the factors that influence the behavior of solutions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"describe the unique role of water in solutions in terms of polarity;"},{"code":"B","text":"distinguish among types of solutions, including electrolytes and nonelectrolytes and unsaturated, saturated, and supersaturated solutions;"},{"code":"C","text":"investigate how solid and gas solubilities are influenced by temperature using solubility curves and how rates of dissolution are influenced by temperature, agitation, and surface area;"},{"code":"D","text":"investigate the general rules regarding solubility and predict the solubility of the products of a double replacement reaction;"},{"code":"E","text":"calculate the concentration of solutions in units of molarity; and"},{"code":"F","text":"calculate the dilutions of solutions using molarity."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(12)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands and applies various rules regarding acids and bases. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"name and write the chemical formulas for acids and bases using IUPAC nomenclature rules;"},{"code":"B","text":"define acids and bases and distinguish between Arrhenius and Bronsted-Lowry definitions;"},{"code":"C","text":"differentiate between strong and weak acids and bases;"},{"code":"D","text":"predict products in acid-base reactions that form water; and"},{"code":"E","text":"define pH and calculate the pH of a solution using the hydrogen ion concentration."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(13)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands the energy changes that occur in chemical reactions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"explain everyday examples that illustrate the four laws of thermodynamics;"},{"code":"B","text":"investigate the process of heat transfer using calorimetry;"},{"code":"C","text":"classify processes as exothermic or endothermic and represent energy changes that occur in chemical reactions using thermochemical equations or graphical analysis; and"},{"code":"D","text":"perform calculations involving heat, mass, temperature change, and specific heat."}]},{"code":"112.43(c)(14)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands the basic processes of nuclear chemistry. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"describe the characteristics of alpha, beta, and gamma radioactive decay processes in terms of balanced nuclear equations;"},{"code":"B","text":"compare fission and fusion reactions; and"},{"code":"C","text":"give examples of applications of nuclear phenomena such as nuclear stability, radiation therapy, diagnostic imaging, solar cells, and nuclear power."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Lab Safety & Scientific Practices","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(1)","112.43(c)(2)","112.43(c)(3)","112.43(c)(4)"],"items":["Lab safety rules, MSDS/SDS, and safety contract","Lab equipment ID and proper use","Measurement, significant figures, and metric/SI units","Accuracy vs. precision and error analysis","Designing a controlled investigation: variables and claim-evidence-reasoning"]},{"unit":"Matter: Classification & Properties","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(1)","112.43(c)(2)","112.43(c)(3)"],"items":["Physical vs. chemical properties and changes","Elements, compounds, and mixtures","Separation techniques lab (filtration, distillation, chromatography)","States of matter and phase changes","Density calculations and graphing lab data","Communicating findings with data tables and graphs"]},{"unit":"Atomic Theory & Structure","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(6)","112.43(c)(4)","112.43(c)(1)"],"items":["History of atomic models (Dalton, Thomson, Rutherford, Bohr)","Subatomic particles, atomic number, and mass number","Isotopes and average atomic mass calculations","Electron configurations and orbital notation","Bohr models and quantum/electron cloud model","Flame-test / emission spectra investigation","Contributions of scientists to atomic theory"]},{"unit":"The Periodic Table","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(5)","112.43(c)(2)"],"items":["Development and organization of the periodic table (Mendeleev to modern)","Periods, groups/families, and metals/nonmetals/metalloids","Periodic trends: atomic radius, ionization energy, electronegativity","Using position to predict reactivity and properties","Valence electrons and Lewis dot structures","Analyzing trend data to identify patterns"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":[],"items":["Reteach atomic structure and periodic trends as needed","Unit assessment and error analysis","Lab make-up and skills spiral (sig figs, dimensional analysis)","Catch-up day for calendar shifts / holidays"]},{"unit":"Chemical Bonding","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(7)","112.43(c)(3)"],"items":["Ionic bonding and formation of ions","Covalent bonding and molecular compounds","Metallic bonding and properties of metals","Lewis structures and octet rule","Molecular geometry (VSEPR) and polarity","Intermolecular forces and their effect on properties","Bonding lab: comparing ionic vs. covalent substance properties"]},{"unit":"Nomenclature & Chemical Formulas","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(8)","112.43(c)(7)"],"items":["Writing and naming ionic compounds","Polyatomic ions and their compounds","Naming covalent (molecular) compounds","Transition metals and Roman numerals","Acids and hydrates naming","Formula writing practice and check"]},{"unit":"The Mole & Stoichiometry Foundations","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(8)","112.43(c)(2)"],"items":["The mole concept and Avogadro's number","Molar mass and mole-mass-particle conversions","Percent composition","Empirical and molecular formulas","Hydrate lab: determining formula from mass data","Interpreting quantitative composition data"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Exam","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":[],"items":["Spiral review: atomic structure, periodic table, bonding","Nomenclature and mole calculation review stations","Semester exam review and practice","Cumulative semester exam"]},{"unit":"Chemical Reactions & Equations","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(9)","112.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Evidence of chemical reactions and conservation of mass","Writing and balancing chemical equations","Types of reactions (synthesis, decomposition, single/double replacement, combustion)","Predicting products and activity series","Net ionic equations","Lab: observing and classifying reaction types","Designing an investigation of conservation of mass"]},{"unit":"Stoichiometry of Reactions","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(9)","112.43(c)(2)"],"items":["Mole-to-mole ratios from balanced equations","Mass-to-mass stoichiometry calculations","Limiting reactant and excess reactant","Theoretical, actual, and percent yield","Stoichiometry lab with percent yield analysis"]},{"unit":"Gases & Kinetic Molecular Theory","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(10)","112.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Kinetic molecular theory and gas behavior","Pressure, temperature, volume relationships","Boyle's, Charles's, Gay-Lussac's, and combined gas laws","Ideal gas law (PV=nRT)","Gas law investigation and problem solving"]},{"unit":"Solutions & Concentration","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(11)","112.43(c)(3)"],"items":["Solutions, solutes, solvents, and the dissolving process","Solubility, saturation, and solubility curves","Factors affecting rate of dissolving and solubility","Concentration: molarity calculations","Dilutions and preparing solutions lab","Communicating results with concentration data"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":[],"items":["Reteach stoichiometry, gas laws, or solutions as needed","Unit assessment and re-test opportunity","Lab make-up and dimensional-analysis spiral","Flex day to absorb spring calendar shifts"]},{"unit":"Acids & Bases","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(12)","112.43(c)(2)"],"items":["Properties of acids and bases; Arrhenius definitions","The pH and pOH scale","Indicators and measuring pH","Strong vs. weak acids and bases","Neutralization reactions and titration lab","Analyzing titration data to find concentration"]},{"unit":"Thermochemistry & Energy","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(13)","112.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Energy in chemical reactions: endothermic vs. exothermic","Heat, temperature, and specific heat","Calorimetry calculations and lab","Energy diagrams and activation energy","Bond energy and enthalpy of reaction","Investigating heat transfer with a calorimeter"]},{"unit":"Nuclear Chemistry","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.43(c)(14)","112.43(c)(4)"],"items":["Radioactivity and types of nuclear decay (alpha, beta, gamma)","Balancing nuclear equations","Half-life calculations and modeling","Fission, fusion, and nuclear energy","Applications, benefits, and risks of nuclear chemistry in society"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":[],"items":["Cumulative review: bonding, reactions, stoichiometry","Review stations: gases, solutions, acids/bases, thermochemistry","Final exam review and practice problems","Comprehensive final exam","End-of-year lab and reflection"]}],"pacingSource":"CK-12"},{"course":"Physics","tacSection":"19 TAC §112.45 (Physics, One Credit, Adopted 2020), Subchapter C (High School), Chapter 112 (TEKS for Science)","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-112","https://tea.texas.gov/laws-and-rules/sboe-rules-tac/sboe-tac-currently-effect/ch112c-0.pdf"],"strands":[{"code":"112.45(c)(1)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student, for at least 40% of instructional time, asks questions, identifies problems, and plans and safely conducts classroom, laboratory, and field investigations to answer questions, explain phenomena, or design solutions using appropriate tools and models. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"ask questions and define problems based on observations or information from text, phenomena, models, or investigations;"},{"code":"B","text":"apply scientific practices to plan and conduct descriptive, comparative, and experimental investigations, and use engineering practices to design solutions to problems;"},{"code":"C","text":"use appropriate safety equipment and practices during laboratory, classroom, and field investigations as outlined in Texas Education Agency-approved safety standards;"},{"code":"D","text":"use appropriate tools such as balances, ballistic carts or equivalent, batteries, computers, constant velocity cars, convex lenses, copper wire, discharge tubes with power supply (H, He, Ne, Ar), data acquisition probes and software, dynamics and force demonstration equipment, electrostatic generators, electrostatic kits, friction blocks, graph paper, graphing technology, hand-held visual spectroscopes, inclined planes, iron filings, lab masses, laser pointers, magnets, magnetic compasses, metric rulers, motion detectors, multimeters (current, voltage, resistance), optics bench, optics kit, photogates, plane mirrors, prisms, protractors, pulleys, resistors, rope or string, scientific calculators, stopwatches, springs, spring scales, switches, tuning forks, wave generators, or other equipment and materials that will produce the same results;"},{"code":"E","text":"collect quantitative data using the International System of Units (SI) and qualitative data as evidence;"},{"code":"F","text":"organize quantitative and qualitative data using bar charts, line graphs, scatter plots, data tables, labeled diagrams, and conceptual mathematical relationships;"},{"code":"G","text":"develop and use models to represent phenomena, systems, processes, or solutions to engineering problems; and"},{"code":"H","text":"distinguish among scientific hypotheses, theories, and laws."}]},{"code":"112.45(c)(2)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student analyzes and interprets data to derive meaning, identify features and patterns, and discover relationships or correlations to develop evidence-based arguments or evaluate designs. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify advantages and limitations of models such as their size, scale, properties, and materials;"},{"code":"B","text":"analyze data by identifying significant statistical features, patterns, sources of error, and limitations;"},{"code":"C","text":"use mathematical calculations to assess quantitative relationships in data; and"},{"code":"D","text":"evaluate experimental and engineering designs."}]},{"code":"112.45(c)(3)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student develops evidence-based explanations and communicates findings, conclusions, and proposed solutions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"develop explanations and propose solutions supported by data and models and consistent with scientific ideas, principles, and theories;"},{"code":"B","text":"communicate explanations and solutions individually and collaboratively in a variety of settings and formats; and"},{"code":"C","text":"engage respectfully in scientific argumentation using applied scientific explanations and empirical evidence."}]},{"code":"112.45(c)(4)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student knows the contributions of scientists and recognizes the importance of scientific research and innovation on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze, evaluate, and critique scientific explanations and solutions by using empirical evidence, logical reasoning, and experimental and observational testing, so as to encourage critical thinking by the student;"},{"code":"B","text":"relate the impact of past and current research on scientific thought and society, including research methodology, cost-benefit analysis, and contributions of diverse scientists as related to the content; and"},{"code":"C","text":"research and explore resources such as museums, libraries, professional organizations, private companies, online platforms, and mentors employed in a science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) field in order to investigate STEM careers."}]},{"code":"112.45(c)(5)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows and applies the laws governing motion in a variety of situations. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze different types of motion by generating and interpreting position versus time, velocity versus time, and acceleration versus time using hand graphing and real-time technology such as motion detectors, photogates, or digital applications;"},{"code":"B","text":"define scalar and vector quantities related to one- and two-dimensional motion and combine vectors using both graphical vector addition and the Pythagorean theorem;"},{"code":"C","text":"describe and analyze motion in one dimension using equations with the concepts of distance, displacement, speed, velocity, frames of reference, and acceleration;"},{"code":"D","text":"describe and analyze acceleration in uniform circular and horizontal projectile motion in two dimensions using equations;"},{"code":"E","text":"explain and apply the concepts of equilibrium and inertia as represented by Newton's first law of motion using relevant real-world examples such as rockets, satellites, and automobile safety devices;"},{"code":"F","text":"calculate the effect of forces on objects, including tension, friction, normal, gravity, centripetal, and applied forces, using free body diagrams and the relationship between force and acceleration as represented by Newton's second law of motion;"},{"code":"G","text":"illustrate and analyze the simultaneous forces between two objects as represented in Newton's third law of motion using free body diagrams and in an experimental design scenario; and"},{"code":"H","text":"describe and calculate, using scientific notation, how the magnitude of force between two objects depends on their masses and the distance between their centers, and predict the effects on objects in linear and orbiting systems using Newton's law of universal gravitation."}]},{"code":"112.45(c)(6)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows the nature of forces in the physical world. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"use scientific notation and predict how the magnitude of the electric force between two objects depends on their charges and the distance between their centers using Coulomb's law;"},{"code":"B","text":"identify and describe examples of electric and magnetic forces and fields in everyday life such as generators, motors, and transformers;"},{"code":"C","text":"investigate and describe conservation of charge during the processes of induction, conduction, and polarization using different materials such as electroscopes, balloons, rods, fur, silk, and Van de Graaf generators;"},{"code":"D","text":"analyze, design, and construct series and parallel circuits using schematics and materials such as switches, wires, resistors, lightbulbs, batteries, voltmeters, and ammeters; and"},{"code":"E","text":"calculate current through, potential difference across, resistance of, and power used by electric circuit elements connected in both series and parallel circuits using Ohm's law."}]},{"code":"112.45(c)(7)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows that changes occur within a physical system and applies the laws of conservation of energy and momentum. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"calculate and explain work and power in one dimension and identify when work is and is not being done by or on a system;"},{"code":"B","text":"investigate and calculate mechanical, kinetic, and potential energy of a system;"},{"code":"C","text":"apply the concept of conservation of energy using the work-energy theorem, energy diagrams, and energy transformation equations, including transformations between kinetic, potential, and thermal energy;"},{"code":"D","text":"calculate and describe the impulse and momentum of objects in physical systems such as automobile safety features, athletics, and rockets; and"},{"code":"E","text":"analyze the conservation of momentum qualitatively in inelastic and elastic collisions in one dimension using models, diagrams, and simulations."}]},{"code":"112.45(c)(8)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows the characteristics and behavior of waves. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"examine and describe simple harmonic motion such as masses on springs and pendulums and wave energy propagation in various types of media such as surface waves on a body of water and pulses in ropes;"},{"code":"B","text":"compare the characteristics of transverse and longitudinal waves, including electromagnetic and sound waves;"},{"code":"C","text":"investigate and analyze characteristics of waves, including velocity, frequency, amplitude, and wavelength, and calculate using the relationships between wave speed, frequency, and wavelength;"},{"code":"D","text":"investigate behaviors of waves, including reflection, refraction, diffraction, interference, standing wave, the Doppler effect and polarization and superposition; and"},{"code":"E","text":"compare the different applications of the electromagnetic spectrum, including radio telescopes, microwaves, and x-rays;"},{"code":"F","text":"investigate the emission spectra produced by various atoms and explain the relationship to the electromagnetic spectrum; and"},{"code":"G","text":"describe and predict image formation as a consequence of reflection from a plane mirror and refraction through a thin convex lens."}]},{"code":"112.45(c)(9)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows examples of quantum phenomena and their applications. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"describe the photoelectric effect and emission spectra produced by various atoms and how both are explained by the photon model for light;"},{"code":"B","text":"investigate Malus's Law and describe examples of applications of wave polarization, including 3-D movie glasses and LCD computer screens;"},{"code":"C","text":"compare and explain how superposition of quantum states is related to the wave-particle duality nature of light; and"},{"code":"D","text":"give examples of applications of quantum phenomena, including the Heisenberg uncertainty principle, quantum computing, and cybersecurity."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Scientific Practices, Measurement & Lab Safety","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(1)","112.45(c)(2)","112.45(c)(3)","112.45(c)(4)"],"items":["Lab safety contract, equipment handling, and emergency procedures","SI units, metric prefixes, and unit conversions with dimensional analysis","Significant figures, precision vs. accuracy, and measurement uncertainty","Graphing data: slope, intercept, and interpreting linear relationships","Designing a controlled investigation: variables, hypothesis, and procedure","Communicating results: lab report format, data tables, and error analysis"]},{"unit":"Motion in One Dimension (Kinematics)","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(5)","112.45(c)(1)","112.45(c)(2)"],"items":["Distance vs. displacement; scalars vs. vectors","Speed and velocity; interpreting position-time graphs","Acceleration and velocity-time graphs","Kinematic equations for constant acceleration","Free fall and acceleration due to gravity","Lab: measuring g with motion sensors or ticker tape"]},{"unit":"Two-Dimensional & Projectile Motion","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(5)","112.45(c)(2)","112.45(c)(3)"],"items":["Vector addition and resolution into components","Relative velocity in two dimensions","Horizontal projectile motion","Projectiles launched at an angle: range and maximum height","Lab: predicting a projectile's landing point","Analyzing 2D motion data and presenting evidence-based conclusions"]},{"unit":"Forces & Newton's Laws of Motion","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(6)","112.45(c)(5)","112.45(c)(1)"],"items":["Types of forces and drawing free-body diagrams","Newton's First Law, inertia, and equilibrium","Newton's Second Law: F = ma problem solving","Newton's Third Law and force pairs","Weight, normal force, and apparent weight (elevator problems)","Tension and connected-object (Atwood) systems","Inclined-plane force analysis","Lab: verifying F = ma with a cart and force sensor"]},{"unit":"Applications of Forces: Friction, Circular Motion & Gravitation","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(6)","112.45(c)(5)","112.45(c)(2)"],"items":["Static and kinetic friction","Uniform circular motion and centripetal force","Newton's Law of Universal Gravitation","Orbital motion and satellites","Lab: coefficient of friction investigation"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["112.45(c)(3)","112.45(c)(4)"],"items":["Reteach high-miss topics from kinematics and forces","GP2 unit assessment and error-analysis review","Physics in history: featured scientists and their contributions to society","Catch-up lab makeups and skills spiral (graphing, vectors)"]},{"unit":"Work, Energy & Power","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(7)","112.45(c)(2)","112.45(c)(3)"],"items":["Work done by a force; positive, negative, and zero work","Kinetic energy and the work-energy theorem","Gravitational and elastic potential energy","Conservation of mechanical energy","Power and efficiency","Lab: energy conservation on a track or pendulum"]},{"unit":"Momentum & Collisions","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(7)","112.45(c)(1)","112.45(c)(2)"],"items":["Impulse and the impulse-momentum theorem","Conservation of linear momentum","Elastic vs. inelastic collisions","Explosions and recoil","Lab: collision cart investigation and data analysis"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Fall Semester Final","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["112.45(c)(5)","112.45(c)(6)","112.45(c)(7)"],"items":["Cumulative review: kinematics and Newton's laws","Cumulative review: energy and momentum","Fall semester final exam","Reflection and goal-setting for spring semester"]},{"unit":"Electric Forces & Fields","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(6)","112.45(c)(2)","112.45(c)(4)"],"items":["Electric charge, conductors, and insulators","Coulomb's Law and electric force","Electric fields and field lines","Voltage, current, and simple circuits (Ohm's Law)","Lab: electrostatics and circuit building"]},{"unit":"Magnetism & Electromagnetic Induction","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(6)","112.45(c)(5)","112.45(c)(3)"],"items":["Magnets, magnetic fields, and field lines","Magnetic force on moving charges and current-carrying wires","Electromagnets and applications (motors)","Electromagnetic induction and generators","Lab: build a simple motor and explore induction"]},{"unit":"Waves & Wave Behavior","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(8)","112.45(c)(1)","112.45(c)(2)"],"items":["Wave types: transverse vs. longitudinal","Wave properties: wavelength, frequency, amplitude, and speed","The wave equation (v = fλ)","Reflection, refraction, diffraction, and interference","Standing waves and resonance","Lab: wave speed on a spring or slinky investigation"]},{"unit":"Sound & Its Applications","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(8)","112.45(c)(2)","112.45(c)(3)"],"items":["Production and transmission of sound","Speed of sound and the Doppler effect","Pitch, loudness, and the decibel scale","Resonance, harmonics, and musical instruments","Lab: measuring the speed of sound"]},{"unit":"Light, Optics & the Electromagnetic Spectrum","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(8)","112.45(c)(1)","112.45(c)(4)"],"items":["The electromagnetic spectrum and properties of light","Reflection and plane/curved mirrors; ray diagrams","Refraction, Snell's Law, and lenses","Color, dispersion, and total internal reflection","Lab: measuring the index of refraction","Scientists and technologies of optics (fiber optics, imaging)"]},{"unit":"Spring Buffer / reteach / catch-up (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["112.45(c)(3)"],"items":["Reteach high-miss electricity, magnetism, and waves topics","Spring-break catch-up, lab makeups, and reassessment","Skills spiral: graphing, units, and claim-evidence-reasoning writing"]},{"unit":"Quantum Phenomena: Atomic & Nuclear Physics","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.45(c)(9)","112.45(c)(2)","112.45(c)(4)"],"items":["The photoelectric effect, photons, and wave-particle duality","Atomic spectra and the Bohr model","Quantized energy levels and applications (lasers, LEDs)","Nuclear structure, isotopes, and mass-energy (E = mc²)","Radioactivity: alpha, beta, gamma decay and half-life","Fission, fusion, and applications (medical, energy)","Contributions of modern physicists and societal impact"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Comprehensive Final","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["112.45(c)(5)","112.45(c)(6)","112.45(c)(7)","112.45(c)(8)","112.45(c)(9)"],"items":["Cumulative review: motion, forces, and energy/momentum","Cumulative review: waves, electricity/magnetism, and quantum","Comprehensive final exam","Capstone STEM design-challenge presentations","Course reflection and physics-in-the-real-world showcase"]}],"pacingSource":"CK-12"},{"course":"Integrated Physics and Chemistry (IPC), One Credit, Adopted 2020","tacSection":"19 TAC §112.44","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter112_sec.112.44","https://texreg.sos.state.tx.us/public/readtac$ext.TacPage?sl=R&app=9&p_dir=&p_rloc=&p_tloc=&p_ploc=&pg=1&p_tac=&ti=19&pt=2&ch=112&rl=44"],"strands":[{"code":"112.44(c)(1)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student, for at least 40% of instructional time, asks questions, identifies problems, and plans and safely conducts classroom, laboratory, and field investigations to answer questions, explain phenomena, or design solutions using appropriate tools and models. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"ask questions and define problems based on observations or information from text, phenomena, models, or investigations;"},{"code":"B","text":"apply scientific practices to plan and conduct descriptive, comparative, and experimental investigations and use engineering practices to design solutions to problems;"},{"code":"C","text":"use appropriate safety equipment and practices during laboratory, classroom, and field investigations as outlined in Texas Education Agency-approved safety standards;"},{"code":"D","text":"use appropriate tools such as data-collecting probes, software applications, the internet, standard laboratory glassware, metric rulers, meter sticks, spring scales, multimeters, Gauss meters, wires, batteries, light bulbs, switches, magnets, electronic balances, mass sets, Celsius thermometers, hot plates, an adequate supply of consumable chemicals, lab notebooks or journals, timing devices, models, and diagrams;"},{"code":"E","text":"collect quantitative data using the International System of Units (SI) and qualitative data as evidence;"},{"code":"F","text":"organize quantitative and qualitative data using labeled drawings and diagrams, graphic organizers, charts, tables, and graphs;"},{"code":"G","text":"develop and use models to represent phenomena, systems, processes, or solutions to engineering problems; and"},{"code":"H","text":"distinguish between scientific hypotheses, theories, and laws."}]},{"code":"112.44(c)(2)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student analyzes and interprets data to derive meaning, identify features and patterns, and discover relationships or correlations to develop evidence-based arguments or evaluate designs. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify advantages and limitations of models such as their size, scale, properties, and materials;"},{"code":"B","text":"analyze data by identifying significant statistical features, patterns, sources of error, and limitations;"},{"code":"C","text":"use mathematical calculations to assess quantitative relationships in data; and"},{"code":"D","text":"evaluate experimental and engineering designs."}]},{"code":"112.44(c)(3)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student develops evidence-based explanations and communicates findings, conclusions, and proposed solutions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"develop explanations and propose solutions supported by data and models and consistent with scientific ideas, principles, and theories;"},{"code":"B","text":"communicate explanations and solutions individually and collaboratively in a variety of settings and formats; and"},{"code":"C","text":"engage respectfully in scientific argumentation using applied scientific explanations and empirical evidence."}]},{"code":"112.44(c)(4)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student knows the contributions of scientists and recognizes the importance of scientific research and innovation on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze, evaluate, and critique scientific explanations and solutions by using empirical evidence, logical reasoning, and experimental and observational testing, so as to encourage critical thinking by the student;"},{"code":"B","text":"relate the impact of past and current research on scientific thought and society, including research methodology, cost-benefit analysis, and contributions of diverse scientists as related to the content; and"},{"code":"C","text":"research and explore resources such as museums, libraries, professional organizations, private companies, online platforms, and mentors employed in a science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) field in order to investigate STEM careers."}]},{"code":"112.44(c)(5)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows the relationship between force and motion in everyday life. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"investigate, analyze, and model motion in terms of position, velocity, acceleration, and time using tables, graphs, and mathematical relationships;"},{"code":"B","text":"analyze data to explain the relationship between mass and acceleration in terms of the net force on an object in one dimension using force diagrams, tables, and graphs;"},{"code":"C","text":"apply the concepts of momentum and impulse to design, evaluate, and refine a device to minimize the net force on objects during collisions such as those that occur during vehicular accidents, sports activities, or the dropping of personal electronic devices;"},{"code":"D","text":"describe the nature of the four fundamental forces: gravitation; electromagnetic; the strong and weak nuclear forces, including fission and fusion; and mass-energy equivalency; and"},{"code":"E","text":"construct and communicate an explanation based on evidence for how changes in mass, charge, and distance affect the strength of gravitational and electrical forces between two objects."}]},{"code":"112.44(c)(6)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows the impact of energy transfer and energy conservation in everyday life. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"design and construct series and parallel circuits that model real-world circuits such as in-home wiring, automobile wiring, and simple electrical devices to evaluate the transfer of electrical energy;"},{"code":"B","text":"design, evaluate, and refine a device that generates electrical energy through the interaction of electric charges and magnetic fields;"},{"code":"C","text":"plan and conduct an investigation to provide evidence that energy is conserved within a closed system;"},{"code":"D","text":"investigate and demonstrate the movement of thermal energy through solids, liquids, and gases by convection, conduction, and radiation such as weather, living, and mechanical systems;"},{"code":"E","text":"plan and conduct an investigation to evaluate the transfer of energy or information through different materials by different types of waves such as wireless signals, ultraviolet radiation, and microwaves;"},{"code":"F","text":"construct and communicate an evidence-based explanation for how wave interference, reflection, and refraction are used in technology such as medicine, communication, and scientific research; and"},{"code":"G","text":"evaluate evidence from multiple sources to critique the advantages and disadvantages of various renewable and nonrenewable energy sources and their impact on society and the environment."}]},{"code":"112.44(c)(7)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows that relationships exist between the structure and properties of matter. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"model basic atomic structure and relate an element's atomic structure to its bonding, reactivity, and placement on the Periodic Table;"},{"code":"B","text":"use patterns within the Periodic Table to predict the relative physical and chemical properties of elements;"},{"code":"C","text":"explain how physical and chemical properties of substances are related to their usage in everyday life such as in sunscreen, cookware, industrial applications, and fuels;"},{"code":"D","text":"explain how electrons can transition from a high energy level to a low energy state, emitting photons at different frequencies for different energy transitions;"},{"code":"E","text":"explain how atomic energy levels and emission spectra present evidence for the wave particle duality; and"},{"code":"F","text":"plan and conduct an investigation to provide evidence that the rate of reaction or dissolving is affected by multiple factors such as particle size, stirring, temperature, and concentration."}]},{"code":"112.44(c)(8)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows that changes in matter affect everyday life. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"investigate how changes in properties are indicative of chemical reactions such as hydrochloric acid with a metal, oxidation of metal, combustion, and neutralizing an acid with a base;"},{"code":"B","text":"develop and use models to balance chemical equations and support the claim that atoms, and therefore mass, are conserved during a chemical reaction;"},{"code":"C","text":"research and communicate the uses, advantages, and disadvantages of nuclear reactions in current technologies; and"},{"code":"D","text":"construct and communicate an evidence-based explanation of the environmental impact of the end-products of chemical reactions such as those that may result in degradation of water, soil, air quality, and global climate change."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Lab Safety, Scientific Practices & Measurement","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(1)","112.44(c)(2)","112.44(c)(3)","112.44(c)(4)"],"items":["Lab safety contract, equipment ID, and SDS/MSDS scavenger hunt","Scientific method: independent/dependent/controlled variables and testable hypotheses","SI units, metric conversions, and dimensional analysis","Measuring mass, volume, and length; precision vs. accuracy and significant figures","Building data tables and graphs (line vs. bar) with title, labels, and scale","Nature of science: how scientific research and innovation shape society (intro case study)"]},{"unit":"Motion: Speed, Velocity & Acceleration","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(5)","112.44(c)(2)"],"items":["Reference frames, distance vs. displacement, scalars vs. vectors","Calculating average speed and velocity (v = d/t) with unit analysis","Interpreting distance-time graphs (slope = speed)","Acceleration: definition, formula, and reading velocity-time graphs","Lab: track a cart's motion and graph position and velocity vs. time","Analyze motion data to identify constant vs. changing-speed patterns"]},{"unit":"Forces & Newton's Laws of Motion","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(5)","112.44(c)(1)"],"items":["Types of forces (gravity, friction, normal, applied, tension) and free-body diagrams","Balanced vs. unbalanced forces and net-force calculations","Newton's First Law: inertia demonstrations","Newton's Second Law: F = ma problem solving","Newton's Third Law: action-reaction pairs in everyday life","Lab: plan and conduct an investigation of how force and mass affect acceleration","Introduction to momentum and collisions (p = mv)"]},{"unit":"Work, Power & Mechanical Energy","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(6)","112.44(c)(2)"],"items":["Work (W = Fd) and identifying when work is and isn't done","Power (P = W/t) in watts and horsepower","Kinetic and gravitational potential energy calculations","Law of conservation of energy and energy transformations","Simple machines and mechanical advantage","Lab: pendulum/roller-coaster energy conservation investigation and data analysis"]},{"unit":"Thermal Energy & Heat Transfer","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(6)","112.44(c)(3)"],"items":["Temperature vs. thermal energy and the kinetic molecular theory","Conduction, convection, and radiation","Specific heat and calorimetry basics (Q = mcΔT)","Lab: build and test an insulated container; write a CER explaining heat transfer","Communicate conclusions: conductors vs. insulators in everyday life"]},{"unit":"Waves, Sound & Light","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(6)","112.44(c)(4)"],"items":["Wave anatomy: wavelength, frequency, amplitude, and period","Transverse vs. longitudinal waves and wave speed (v = fλ)","Sound: pitch, loudness, and the Doppler effect","The electromagnetic spectrum and properties of light","Reflection, refraction, and lenses/mirrors","Scientist spotlight: how wave and optics research shaped modern technology"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Final (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"assessment","teks":["112.44(c)(1)","112.44(c)(2)","112.44(c)(3)"],"items":["Cumulative review stations: motion, forces, energy, and waves","Data-analysis and graphing skills practice set","Free-body diagram and core formula (v=d/t, F=ma, W=Fd) review","Semester final exam","Error analysis and spring semester goal-setting"]},{"unit":"Electricity & Magnetism","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(6)","112.44(c)(2)"],"items":["Static electricity, charge, and the law of charges","Current, voltage, resistance, and Ohm's Law (V = IR)","Series vs. parallel circuits: building and measuring","Magnetism, magnetic fields, and electromagnets","Electromagnetic induction, generators, and motors as energy transfer","Lab: build circuits and collect and interpret current-voltage data"]},{"unit":"Structure of Matter: Atoms & the Periodic Table","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(7)","112.44(c)(4)"],"items":["History of the atom: Dalton, Thomson, Rutherford, and Bohr models (scientist contributions)","Subatomic particles, atomic number, mass number, and isotopes","Electron configuration and valence electrons","Organization of the periodic table: periods, groups, and families","Periodic trends: reactivity and metals/nonmetals/metalloids","Lab: flame tests and spectra to connect electrons to element identity"]},{"unit":"Bonding & the Properties of Matter","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(7)","112.44(c)(1)"],"items":["Ionic vs. covalent bonding and why atoms bond (octet rule)","Writing chemical formulas and naming simple compounds","Structure-property relationships: melting point, conductivity, and solubility","States of matter and phase changes at the particle level","Physical vs. chemical properties of matter","Lab: classify substances by testing properties to infer bond type"]},{"unit":"Chemical Reactions & Conservation of Mass","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(8)","112.44(c)(3)"],"items":["Signs of a chemical change and reactants vs. products","Writing and balancing chemical equations","Law of conservation of mass (closed-system mass before/after)","Reaction types: synthesis, decomposition, single/double replacement, combustion","Endothermic vs. exothermic reactions and energy in reactions","CER lab report: evidence that mass is conserved in a reaction"]},{"unit":"Solutions, Acids, Bases & Nuclear Changes","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.44(c)(8)","112.44(c)(2)"],"items":["Solutions, solutes, solvents, concentration, and solubility","Acids and bases: properties, the pH scale, and indicators","Neutralization reactions in everyday life","Nuclear changes: fission, fusion, radioactivity, and half-life","Analyze data on reaction rate or pH to draw evidence-based conclusions"]},{"unit":"Spring Buffer / Reteach & Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["112.44(c)(1)","112.44(c)(3)"],"items":["Flex days to absorb calendar shifts (weather, assemblies, testing)","Targeted reteach of low-mastery TEKS from spring data","Reassessment, test corrections, and lab make-ups","Skills spiral: balancing equations and formula practice"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Exam (wiggle)","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["112.44(c)(2)","112.44(c)(3)","112.44(c)(4)"],"items":["Cumulative review: physics half (motion, forces, energy, waves, electricity)","Cumulative review: chemistry half (atoms, bonding, reactions, solutions)","Mixed data-analysis and problem-solving practice","Scientists & innovation gallery walk: how science shapes society (project share-out)","Comprehensive final exam","Reflection and course wrap-up"]}]},{"course":"Aquatic Science, One Credit, Adopted 2021","tacSection":"19 TAC §112.47","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter112_sec.112.47","https://texreg.sos.state.tx.us/public/readtac$ext.TacPage?sl=R&app=9&p_dir=&p_rloc=&p_tloc=&p_ploc=&pg=1&p_tac=&ti=19&pt=2&ch=112&rl=47"],"strands":[{"code":"112.47(c)(1)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student, for at least 40% of instructional time, asks questions, identifies problems, and plans and safely conducts classroom, laboratory, and field investigations to explain phenomena or design solutions using appropriate tools and models. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"ask questions and define problems based on observations or information from text, phenomena, models, or investigations;"},{"code":"B","text":"apply scientific practices to plan and conduct descriptive, comparative, and experimental investigations and use engineering practices to design solutions to problems;"},{"code":"C","text":"use appropriate safety equipment and practices during laboratory, classroom, and field investigations as outlined in Texas Education Agency-approved safety standards;"},{"code":"D","text":"use appropriate tools such as Global Positioning System (GPS), Geographic Information System (GIS), weather balloons, buoys, water testing kits, meter sticks, metric rulers, pipettes, graduated cylinders, standard laboratory glassware, balances, timing devices, pH meters or probes, various data collecting probes, thermometers, calculators, computers, internet access, turbidity testing devices, hand magnifiers, work and disposable gloves, compasses, first aid kits, field guides, water quality test kits or probes, 30-meter tape measures, tarps, ripple tanks, trowels, screens, buckets, sediment samples equipment, cameras, flow meters, cast nets, kick nets, seines, computer models, spectrophotometers, stereomicroscopes, compound microscopes, clinometers, and field journals, various prepared slides, hand lenses, hot plates, Petri dishes, sampling nets, waders, leveling grade rods (Jason sticks), protractors, inclination and height distance calculators, samples of biological specimens or structures, core sampling equipment, fish tanks and associated supplies, and hydrometers;"},{"code":"E","text":"collect quantitative data using the International System of Units (SI) and qualitative data as evidence;"},{"code":"F","text":"organize quantitative and qualitative data using probeware, spreadsheets, lab notebooks or journals, models, diagrams, graphs paper, computers, or cellphone applications;"},{"code":"G","text":"develop and use models to represent phenomena, systems, processes, or solutions to engineering problems; and"},{"code":"H","text":"distinguish between scientific hypotheses, theories, and laws."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(2)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student analyzes and interprets data to derive meaning, identify features and patterns, and discover relationships or correlations to develop evidence-based arguments or evaluate designs. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify advantages and limitations of models such as their size, scale, properties, and materials;"},{"code":"B","text":"analyze data by identifying significant statistical features, patterns, sources of error, and limitations;"},{"code":"C","text":"use mathematical calculations to assess quantitative relationships in data; and"},{"code":"D","text":"evaluate experimental and engineering designs."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(3)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student develops evidence-based explanations and communicates findings, conclusions, and proposed solutions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"develop explanations and propose solutions supported by data and models consistent with scientific ideas, principles, and theories;"},{"code":"B","text":"communicate explanations and solutions individually and collaboratively in a variety of settings and formats; and"},{"code":"C","text":"engage respectfully in scientific argumentation using applied scientific explanations and empirical evidence."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(4)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student knows the contributions of scientists and recognizes the importance of scientific research and innovation on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze, evaluate, and critique scientific explanations and solutions by using empirical evidence, logical reasoning, and experimental and observational testing, so as to encourage critical thinking by the student;"},{"code":"B","text":"relate the impact of past and current research on scientific thought and society, including research methodology, cost-benefit analysis, and contributions of diverse scientists as related to the content; and"},{"code":"C","text":"research and explore resources such as museums, planetariums, observatories, libraries, professional organizations, private companies, online platforms, and mentors employed in a science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) field in order to investigate STEM careers."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(5)","statement":"The student understands how the properties of water build the foundation of aquatic ecosystems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"describe how the shape and polarity of the water molecule make it a \"universal solvent\" in aquatic systems;"},{"code":"B","text":"identify how aquatic ecosystems are affected by water's properties of adhesion, cohesion, surface tension, heat capacity, and thermal conductivity; and"},{"code":"C","text":"explain how the density of water is critical for organisms in cold environments."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(6)","statement":"Students know that aquatic environments are the product of interactions among Earth systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify key features and characteristics of atmospheric, geological, hydrological, and biological systems as they relate to aquatic environments;"},{"code":"B","text":"describe the interrelatedness of atmospheric, geological, hydrological, and biological systems in aquatic ecosystems, including positive and negative feedback loops; and"},{"code":"C","text":"evaluate environmental data using technology such as maps, visualizations, satellite data, Global Positioning System (GPS), Geographic Information System (GIS), weather balloons, and buoys to model the interactions that affect aquatic ecosystems."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(7)","statement":"The student knows about the interdependence and interactions that occur in aquatic environments. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify how energy flows and matter cycles through both freshwater and marine aquatic systems, including food webs, chains, and pyramids;"},{"code":"B","text":"identify biological, chemical, geological, and physical components of an aquatic life zone as they relate to the organisms in it;"},{"code":"C","text":"identify variables that affect the solubility of carbon dioxide and oxygen in water;"},{"code":"D","text":"evaluate factors affecting aquatic population cycles such as lunar cycles, temperature variations, hours of daylight, and predator-prey relationships; and"},{"code":"E","text":"identify the interdependence of organisms in an aquatic environment such as in a pond, a river, a lake, an ocean, or an aquifer and the biosphere."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(8)","statement":"The student conducts short-term and long-term studies on local aquatic environments. Local natural environments are to be preferred over artificial or virtual environments. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"evaluate data over a period of time from an established aquatic environment documenting seasonal changes and the behavior of organisms;"},{"code":"B","text":"collect and analyze pH, salinity, temperature, mineral content, nitrogen compounds, dissolved oxygen, and turbidity data periodically, starting with baseline measurements; and"},{"code":"C","text":"use data from short-term or long-term studies to analyze interrelationships between producers, consumers, and decomposers in aquatic ecosystems."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(9)","statement":"The student knows the role of cycles in an aquatic environment. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify the role of carbon, nitrogen, water, and nutrient cycles in an aquatic environment, including upwellings and turnovers;"},{"code":"B","text":"examine the interrelationships between aquatic systems and climate and weather, including El Niño and La Niña, currents, and hurricanes; and"},{"code":"C","text":"explain how tidal cycles influence intertidal ecology."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(10)","statement":"The student knows the origin and potential uses of fresh water. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify sources of water in a watershed, including rainfall, groundwater, and surface water;"},{"code":"B","text":"identify factors that contribute to how water flows through a watershed;"},{"code":"C","text":"analyze water quantity and quality in a local watershed or aquifer; and"},{"code":"D","text":"describe human uses of fresh water and how human freshwater use competes with that of other organisms."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(11)","statement":"The student knows that geological phenomena and fluid dynamics affect aquatic systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"examine basic principles of fluid dynamics, including hydrostatic pressure, density as a result of salinity, and buoyancy;"},{"code":"B","text":"identify interrelationships between ocean currents, climates, and geologic features such as continental margins, active and passive margins, abyssal plains, island atolls, peninsulas, barrier islands, and hydrothermal vents;"},{"code":"C","text":"explain how fluid dynamics causes upwelling and lake turnover; and"},{"code":"D","text":"describe how erosion and deposition in river systems lead to formation of geologic features."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(12)","statement":"The student understands the types of aquatic ecosystems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"differentiate among freshwater, brackish, and marine ecosystems; and"},{"code":"B","text":"identify the major properties and components of different marine and freshwater life zones."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(13)","statement":"The student knows environmental adaptations of aquatic organisms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"compare different traits in aquatic organisms using tools such as dichotomous keys;"},{"code":"B","text":"describe how adaptations allow an organism to exist within an aquatic environment; and"},{"code":"C","text":"compare adaptations of freshwater and marine organisms."}]},{"code":"112.47(c)(14)","statement":"The student understands how human activities impact aquatic environments. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze the cumulative impact of human population growth on an aquatic ecosystem;"},{"code":"B","text":"predict effects of chemical, organic, physical, and thermal changes due to humans on the living and nonliving components of an aquatic ecosystem;"},{"code":"C","text":"investigate the role of humans in unbalanced systems involving phenomena such as invasive species, fish farming, cultural eutrophication, or red tides;"},{"code":"D","text":"analyze and discuss how human activities such as fishing, transportation, dams, and recreation influence aquatic environments;"},{"code":"E","text":"describe the impact such as costs and benefits of various laws and policies such as The Endangered Species Act, right of capture laws, or Clean Water Act on aquatic systems; and"},{"code":"F","text":"analyze the purpose and effectiveness of human efforts to restore aquatic ecosystems affected by human activities."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Course Launch: Lab Safety & Scientific Practices","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(1)","112.47(c)(4)"],"items":["Syllabus, expectations & signed lab safety contract","Lab safety, equipment handling & chemical safety (SDS)","Nature of science: asking testable questions, hypotheses & variables","Measurement, SI units, tools & building data tables","Contributions of aquatic scientists (Cousteau, Rachel Carson, Sylvia Earle) and the impact of research on society","Designing and safely conducting a first classroom investigation"]},{"unit":"Properties of Water","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(5)","112.47(c)(2)"],"items":["Water molecule structure & polarity","Cohesion, adhesion & surface tension","Water as the universal solvent; salinity & dissolved salts","Density, buoyancy, thermal layering & why ice floats","High specific heat and its moderating effect on aquatic climates","pH, dissolved oxygen & buffering capacity","Lab: measuring temperature, salinity, DO and pH of water samples","Analyzing water-quality data and identifying patterns"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["112.47(c)(1)","112.47(c)(2)"],"items":["Reteach: common properties-of-water misconceptions","Lab makeups & data-table revision","Skills spiral: graphing, trend lines & error analysis","Unit 1–2 checkpoint assessment"]},{"unit":"Aquatic Environments as Earth Systems","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(6)","112.47(c)(3)"],"items":["Earth's spheres: hydrosphere, geosphere, atmosphere, biosphere","Interactions among Earth systems that create aquatic environments","The Sun, uneven heating & energy input to water bodies","Weather, climate and their link to aquatic environments","Watersheds and drainage basins (local Rio Grande watershed)","Communicating an evidence-based explanation of an Earth-systems interaction"]},{"unit":"Cycles in Aquatic Environments","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(9)","112.47(c)(2)"],"items":["The water cycle in depth","The carbon cycle and ocean carbon storage","The nitrogen cycle in aquatic systems","The phosphorus cycle and nutrient loading","Eutrophication as a disruption of nutrient cycles","Interpreting cycle diagrams and nutrient data"]},{"unit":"Fresh Water: Origin & Uses","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(10)","112.47(c)(14)"],"items":["Distribution of Earth's water and how little is fresh","Groundwater, aquifers & recharge (Hueco/Mesilla Bolson)","Surface water: rivers, lakes & reservoirs","Water treatment, distribution & human uses","Water conservation & scarcity in the arid Southwest","Case study: Rio Grande allocation and the El Paso water supply"]},{"unit":"Geology & Fluid Dynamics of Aquatic Systems","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(11)","112.47(c)(2)"],"items":["Plate tectonics, ocean basins & seafloor features","Erosion, deposition & sediment transport shaping water bodies","Waves and tides and their causes","Surface currents and deep thermohaline circulation","Density-driven flow and upwelling","Lab: modeling density currents and analyzing the results"]},{"unit":"Local Aquatic Field Study (short- & long-term)","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(8)","112.47(c)(1)","112.47(c)(3)"],"items":["Planning short-term and long-term local field investigations","Field methods: water sampling, transects & quadrats","Field site data collection and safety in the field","Biotic index & organism/macroinvertebrate sampling","Analyzing and graphing field data collected over time","Communicating field findings in a scientific report"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Exam (wiggle)","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["112.47(c)(1)","112.47(c)(2)","112.47(c)(3)"],"items":["Semester 1 concept review: water, Earth systems, cycles, fresh water","Skills review: data analysis, graphing & argumentation","Lab-practical review and field-data synthesis","Semester exam","Reteach and remediation based on exam data"]},{"unit":"Types of Aquatic Ecosystems: Freshwater Systems","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(12)","112.47(c)(7)"],"items":["Overview: classifying aquatic ecosystems","Lakes and ponds: littoral, limnetic & benthic zones","Streams and rivers as flowing-water ecosystems","Wetlands and marshes and their functions","Abiotic factors and community structure in freshwater","Comparing and contrasting freshwater ecosystem data"]},{"unit":"Types of Aquatic Ecosystems: Marine & Estuarine","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(12)","112.47(c)(6)"],"items":["Ocean zones: intertidal, neritic, pelagic & benthic","Coral reefs and the conditions they require","Estuaries and brackish-water systems","Salt marshes and mangroves","How Earth systems set marine ecosystem boundaries","Mapping and comparing marine ecosystems"]},{"unit":"Interdependence & Interactions in Aquatic Communities","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(7)","112.47(c)(2)"],"items":["Producers, consumers & decomposers in aquatic food webs","Energy flow, trophic levels & ecological pyramids","Symbiosis: mutualism, commensalism & parasitism","Predator–prey and competition dynamics","Carrying capacity and population interactions","Analyzing food-web and population data"]},{"unit":"Adaptations of Aquatic Organisms","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(13)","112.47(c)(7)"],"items":["Structural adaptations for buoyancy, movement & respiration","Osmoregulation in fresh vs. salt water","Adaptations to pressure, light & temperature by depth","Behavioral adaptations: migration, schooling & camouflage","Reproductive strategies of aquatic organisms","Adaptations to extreme environments (hydrothermal vents, hypersaline)","Natural selection and evidence of adaptation from data"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["112.47(c)(1)","112.47(c)(2)","112.47(c)(3)"],"items":["Reteach ecosystems and community interactions","Skills spiral: constructing arguments from evidence","Lab and project makeups","Checkpoint assessment"]},{"unit":"Human Impacts on Aquatic Environments","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.47(c)(14)","112.47(c)(3)"],"items":["Point and nonpoint source pollution","Eutrophication, dead zones & thermal pollution","Plastics, microplastics & marine debris","Overfishing and resource depletion","Invasive species and habitat destruction","Climate change, ocean acidification & sea-level rise","Conservation, restoration & policy solutions","Communicating a solution: an evidence-based proposal"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Project/Exam (wiggle)","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["112.47(c)(1)","112.47(c)(2)","112.47(c)(3)","112.47(c)(4)"],"items":["Long-term field-study data synthesis and conclusions","Year concept review across all strands","Final project: aquatic environment case study & solution","Presenting findings; the role of science in society","Final exam"]}]},{"course":"Environmental Systems, One Credit, Adopted 2021","tacSection":"19 TAC §112.50","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter112_sec.112.50","https://texreg.sos.state.tx.us/public/readtac$ext.TacPage?sl=R&app=9&p_dir=&p_rloc=&p_tloc=&p_ploc=&pg=1&p_tac=&ti=19&pt=2&ch=112&rl=50"],"strands":[{"code":"112.50(c)(1)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student, for at least 40% of instructional time, asks questions, identifies problems, and plans and safely conducts classroom, laboratory, and field investigations to explain phenomena or design solutions using appropriate tools and models. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"ask questions and define problems based on observations or information from text, phenomena, models, or investigations;"},{"code":"B","text":"apply scientific practices to plan and conduct descriptive, comparative, and experimental investigations and use engineering practices to design solutions to problems;"},{"code":"C","text":"use appropriate safety equipment and practices during laboratory, classroom, and field investigations as outlined in Texas Education Agency-approved safety standards;"},{"code":"D","text":"use appropriate tools such as meter sticks, metric rulers, pipettes, graduated cylinders, standard laboratory glassware, balances, timing devices, pH meters or probes, various data collecting probes, thermometers, calculators, computers, internet access, turbidity testing devices, hand magnifiers, work and disposable gloves, compasses, first aid kits, binoculars, field guides, water quality test kits or probes, soil test kits or probes, 30 meter tape measures, tarps, shovels, trowels, screens, buckets, rock and mineral samples equipment, air quality testing devices, cameras, flow meters, Global Positioning System (GPS) units, Geographic Information System (GIS) software, computer models, densiometers, spectrophotometers, stereomicroscopes, compound microscopes, clinometers, field journals, various prepared slides, hand lenses, hot plates, Petri dishes, sampling nets, waders, leveling grade rods (Jason sticks), protractors, inclination and height distance calculators, samples of biological specimens or structures, core sampling equipment, and kick nets;"},{"code":"E","text":"collect quantitative data using the International System of Units (SI) and qualitative data as evidence;"},{"code":"F","text":"organize quantitative and qualitative data using probeware, spreadsheets, lab notebooks or journals, models, diagrams, graphs paper, computers, or cellphone applications;"},{"code":"G","text":"develop and use models to represent phenomena, systems, processes, or solutions to engineering problems; and"},{"code":"H","text":"distinguish between scientific hypotheses, theories, and laws."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(2)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student analyzes and interprets data to derive meaning, identify features and patterns, and discover relationships or correlations to develop evidence-based arguments or evaluate designs. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify advantages and limitations of models such as their size, scale, properties, and materials;"},{"code":"B","text":"analyze data by identifying significant statistical features, patterns, sources of error, and limitations;"},{"code":"C","text":"use mathematical calculations to assess quantitative relationships in data; and"},{"code":"D","text":"evaluate experimental and engineering designs."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(3)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student develops evidence-based explanations and communicates findings, conclusions, and proposed solutions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"develop explanations and propose solutions supported by data and models consistent with scientific ideas, principles, and theories;"},{"code":"B","text":"communicate explanations and solutions individually and collaboratively in a variety of settings and formats; and"},{"code":"C","text":"engage respectfully in scientific argumentation using applied scientific explanations and empirical evidence."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(4)","statement":"Scientific and engineering practices. The student knows the contributions of scientists and recognizes the importance of scientific research and innovation on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze, evaluate, and critique scientific explanations and solutions by using empirical evidence, logical reasoning, and experimental and observational testing, so as to encourage critical thinking by the student;"},{"code":"B","text":"relate the impact of past and current research on scientific thought and society, including research methodology, cost-benefit analysis, and contributions of diverse scientists as related to the content; and"},{"code":"C","text":"research and explore resources such as museums, planetariums, observatories, libraries, professional organizations, private companies, online platforms, and mentors employed in a science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) field in order to investigate STEM careers."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(5)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows the relationships of biotic and abiotic factors within habitats, ecosystems, and biomes. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify native plants and animals within a local ecosystem and compare their roles to those of plants and animals in other biomes, including aquatic, grassland, forest, desert, and tundra;"},{"code":"B","text":"explain the cycling of water, phosphorus, carbon, silicon, and nitrogen through ecosystems, including sinks, and the human interactions that alter these cycles using tools such as models;"},{"code":"C","text":"evaluate the effects of fluctuations in abiotic factors on local ecosystems and local biomes;"},{"code":"D","text":"measure the concentration of dissolved substances such as dissolved oxygen, chlorides, and nitrates and describe their impacts on an ecosystem;"},{"code":"E","text":"use models to predict how the introduction of an invasive species may alter the food chain and affect existing populations in an ecosystem;"},{"code":"F","text":"use models to predict how species extinction may alter the food chain and affect existing populations in an ecosystem; and"},{"code":"G","text":"predict changes that may occur in an ecosystem if genetic diversity is increased or decreased."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(6)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows the interrelationships among the resources within the local environmental system. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"compare and contrast land use and management methods and how they affect land attributes such as fertility, productivity, economic value, and ecological stability;"},{"code":"B","text":"relate how water sources, management, and conservation affect water uses and quality;"},{"code":"C","text":"document the use and conservation of both renewable and non-renewable resources as they pertain to sustainability;"},{"code":"D","text":"identify how changes in limiting resources such as water, food, and energy affect local ecosystems;"},{"code":"E","text":"analyze and evaluate the economic significance and interdependence of resources within the local environmental system; and"},{"code":"F","text":"evaluate the impact of waste management methods such as reduction, reuse, recycling, upcycling, and composting on resource availability in the local environment."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(7)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows the sources and flow of energy through an environmental system. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"describe the interactions between the components of the geosphere, hydrosphere, cryosphere, atmosphere, and biosphere;"},{"code":"B","text":"relate biogeochemical cycles to the flow of energy in ecosystems, including energy sinks such as oil, natural gas, and coal deposits;"},{"code":"C","text":"explain the flow of heat energy in an ecosystem, including conduction, convection, and radiation; and"},{"code":"D","text":"identify and describe how energy is used, transformed, and conserved as it flows through ecosystems."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(8)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows the relationship between carrying capacity and changes in populations and ecosystems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"compare exponential and logistical population growth using graphical representations;"},{"code":"B","text":"identify factors that may alter carrying capacity such as disease; natural disaster; available food, water, and livable space; habitat fragmentation; and periodic changes in weather;"},{"code":"C","text":"calculate changes in population size in ecosystems; and"},{"code":"D","text":"analyze and make predictions about the impact on populations of geographic locales due to diseases, birth and death rates, urbanization, and natural events such as migration and seasonal changes."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(9)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows that environments change naturally. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"analyze and describe how natural events such as tectonic movement, volcanic events, fires, tornadoes, hurricanes, flooding, and tsunamis affect natural populations;"},{"code":"B","text":"explain how regional changes in the environment may have global effects;"},{"code":"C","text":"examine how natural processes such as succession and feedback loops can restore habitats and ecosystems;"},{"code":"D","text":"describe how temperature inversions have short-term and long-term effects, including El Niño and La Niña oscillations, ice cap and glacial melting, and changes in ocean surface temperatures; and"},{"code":"E","text":"analyze the impact of natural global climate change on ice caps, glaciers, ocean currents, and surface temperatures."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(10)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows how humans impact environmental systems through emissions and pollutants. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"identify sources of emissions in air, soil, and water, including point and nonpoint sources;"},{"code":"B","text":"distinguish how an emission becomes a pollutant based on its concentration, toxicity, reactivity, and location within the environment;"},{"code":"C","text":"investigate the effects of pollutants such as chlorofluorocarbons, greenhouse gases, pesticide runoff, nuclear waste, aerosols, metallic ions, and heavy metals, as well as thermal, light, and noise pollution;"},{"code":"D","text":"evaluate indicators of air, soil, and water quality against regulatory standards to determine the health of an ecosystem; and"},{"code":"E","text":"distinguish between the causes and effects of global warming and ozone depletion, including the causes, the chemicals involved, the atmospheric layer, the environmental effects, the human health effects, and the relevant wavelengths on the electromagnetic spectrum (IR and UV)."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(11)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands how individual and collective actions impact environmental systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"evaluate the negative effects of human activities on the environment, including overhunting, overfishing, ecotourism, all-terrain vehicles, and personal watercraft;"},{"code":"B","text":"evaluate the positive effects of human activities on the environment, including habitat restoration projects, species preservation efforts, nature conservancy groups, game and wildlife management, and ecotourism; and"},{"code":"C","text":"research the advantages and disadvantages of \"going green\" such as organic gardening and farming, natural methods of pest control, hydroponics, xeriscaping, energy-efficient homes and appliances, and hybrid cars."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(12)","statement":"Science concepts. The student understands how ethics and economic priorities influence environmental decisions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"evaluate cost-benefit trade-offs of commercial activities such as municipal development, food production, deforestation, over-harvesting, mining, and use of renewable and non-renewable energy sources;"},{"code":"B","text":"evaluate the economic impacts of individual actions on the environment such as overbuilding, habitat destruction, poaching, and improper waste disposal;"},{"code":"C","text":"analyze how ethical beliefs influence environmental scientific and engineering practices such as methods for food production, water distribution, energy production, and the extraction of minerals;"},{"code":"D","text":"discuss the impact of research and technology on social ethics and legal practices in situations such as the design of new buildings, recycling, or emission standards; and"},{"code":"E","text":"argue from evidence whether or not a healthy economy and a healthy environment are mutually exclusive."}]},{"code":"112.50(c)(13)","statement":"Science concepts. The student knows how legislation mediates human impacts on the environment. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"A","text":"describe past and present state and national legislation, including Texas automobile emissions regulations, the National Park Service Act, the Clean Air Act, the Clean Water Act, the Soil and Water Resources Conservation Act, and the Endangered Species Act; and"},{"code":"B","text":"evaluate the goals and effectiveness of past and present international agreements such as the environmental Antarctic Treaty System, the Montreal Protocol, the Kyoto Protocol, and the Paris Climate Accord."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Doing Environmental Science: Safety, Field Methods & Scientific Practices","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.50(c)(1)","112.50(c)(3)","112.50(c)(4)"],"items":["Lab and field safety, safety contract, and proper use of equipment and probeware","Scientific inquiry and designing controlled investigations (independent/dependent variables, controls)","Field methods: quadrats, transects, GPS, and water/soil test kits to collect evidence","Collecting, tabulating, graphing, and communicating environmental data (units, precision, error)","Contributions of environmental scientists and the role of research and innovation in society"]},{"unit":"Ecosystems: Biotic & Abiotic Factors, Habitats & Biomes","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.50(c)(5)","112.50(c)(1)","112.50(c)(2)"],"items":["Levels of organization: organism, population, community, ecosystem, biome","Biotic vs abiotic factors and how they define a habitat","Limiting factors, ranges of tolerance, and the niche concept","Terrestrial biomes and their climate signatures (incl. local Chihuahuan Desert / El Paso)","Aquatic ecosystems: freshwater, marine, and estuarine","Field lab: measuring abiotic factors (light, temperature, soil, water) in a local habitat and analyzing the data"]},{"unit":"Energy Flow Through Ecosystems","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.50(c)(7)","112.50(c)(2)","112.50(c)(3)"],"items":["The Sun as the primary energy source; capturing energy through photosynthesis","Gross vs net primary productivity","Food chains, food webs, and trophic levels","Energy pyramids, the 10% rule, and the laws of thermodynamics in ecosystems","Analyzing and interpreting energy-flow and productivity data","Modeling energy transfer and efficiency loss between trophic levels"]},{"unit":"Cycles of Matter & Local Resources","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.50(c)(6)","112.50(c)(1)","112.50(c)(2)"],"items":["The water cycle and the local watershed (Rio Grande, aquifers)","The carbon cycle and its link to climate","Nitrogen and phosphorus cycles and nutrient limitation","Renewable vs nonrenewable resources","Soil formation, land, and mineral resources","Interdependence of water, soil, air, and energy resources in the local system","Investigation: testing water quality of a local resource and interpreting results"]},{"unit":"Populations & Carrying Capacity","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.50(c)(8)","112.50(c)(2)","112.50(c)(3)"],"items":["Exponential vs logistic population growth","Carrying capacity and environmental resistance","Density-dependent and density-independent limiting factors","Community interactions: competition, predation, and symbiosis","Human population growth and the demographic transition","Lab/model: population dynamics and carrying-capacity simulation with data analysis"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Exam (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["112.50(c)(5)","112.50(c)(6)","112.50(c)(7)","112.50(c)(8)"],"items":["Review stations: ecosystems, energy flow, cycles, and populations","Data-analysis and graphing skills spiral review","Vocabulary and concept synthesis for first-semester content","Semester exam","Reteach and error analysis of exam results"]},{"unit":"Natural Environmental Change","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.50(c)(9)","112.50(c)(2)","112.50(c)(4)"],"items":["Primary and secondary ecological succession","Natural climate variability and long-term geologic change","Natural disturbances: fire, flood, drought, storms, and volcanism","Natural selection, adaptation, and background extinction","Evidence of past environmental change: fossils, ice cores, and tree rings","Scientists and methods used to reconstruct Earth's past environments"]},{"unit":"Human Impacts: Emissions, Pollution & Climate Change","weeks":4,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.50(c)(10)","112.50(c)(1)","112.50(c)(2)"],"items":["Sources of air pollution and emissions; the greenhouse effect","Climate change: evidence, causes, and projected impacts","Water pollution: point vs nonpoint sources and eutrophication","Solid and hazardous waste and land pollution","Toxicology, bioaccumulation, and biomagnification","Investigation: measuring local air or water quality and analyzing the data","Interpreting emissions and pollution trend data to build evidence-based arguments"]},{"unit":"Individual & Collective Action & Sustainability","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.50(c)(11)","112.50(c)(3)"],"items":["Ecological footprint and resource consumption","Conservation vs preservation","Reduce, reuse, recycle, and modern waste management","Renewable energy and sustainable technologies","Community stewardship, citizen science, and collective action","Design and communicate a personal/school sustainability action plan"]},{"unit":"Environmental Ethics & Economics","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.50(c)(12)","112.50(c)(2)","112.50(c)(4)"],"items":["Environmental ethics frameworks: anthropocentric, biocentric, and ecocentric","Cost-benefit analysis of environmental decisions","Ecosystem services and assigning value to nature","Tragedy of the commons and shared resources","Economic tools: incentives, carbon tax, and cap-and-trade","Structured debate on a real resource-use and economic tradeoff"]},{"unit":"Environmental Legislation & Policy","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["112.50(c)(13)","112.50(c)(3)","112.50(c)(4)"],"items":["Landmark U.S. laws: Clean Air Act, Clean Water Act, Endangered Species Act, NEPA","Regulatory agencies: the EPA and Texas TCEQ","International agreements: the Montreal Protocol and the Paris Agreement","Texas and local El Paso environmental policy and ordinances","How legislation mediates human impacts through permitting, enforcement, and penalties","Mock legislative hearing or policy-proposal presentation"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["112.50(c)(9)","112.50(c)(10)","112.50(c)(11)"],"items":["Reteach high-need standards identified from data and spot-check quizzes","Makeup labs, field work, and missed assessments","Unit reassessments and grade recovery","Project and lab workdays plus enrichment","Current environmental events and local case studies"]},{"unit":"Capstone Project & End-of-Year Review / Final Exam","weeks":2,"kind":"assessment","teks":["112.50(c)(5)","112.50(c)(6)","112.50(c)(8)","112.50(c)(10)","112.50(c)(11)","112.50(c)(13)"],"items":["Capstone: research a local or global environmental issue across multiple strands","Synthesize biotic/abiotic, energy, resource, and human-impact evidence","Propose and communicate an evidence-based solution","Comprehensive final-exam review stations","Final exam","Course reflection and portfolio wrap-up"]}]},{"course":"World Geography Studies (One Credit), Adopted 2022","tacSection":"19 TAC §113.43","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["http://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter113_sec.113.43","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-113"],"strands":[{"code":"113.43(d)(1)","statement":"History. The student understands how geography and processes of spatial exchange (diffusion) influenced events in the past and helped to shape the present. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(1)(A)","text":"analyze significant physical features and environmental conditions that have influenced the past and migration patterns and have shaped the distribution of culture groups today; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(1)(B)","text":"trace the spatial diffusion of phenomena such as the Columbian Exchange or the diffusion of American popular culture and describe the effects on regions of contact."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(2)","statement":"History. The student understands how people, places, and environments have changed over time and the effects of these changes. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(2)(A)","text":"describe the human and physical characteristics of the same regions at different periods of time to analyze relationships between past events and current conditions; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(2)(B)","text":"explain how changes in societies such as population shifts, technological advancements, and environmental policies have led to diverse uses of physical features over time such as terrace farming, dams, and polders."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(3)","statement":"Geography. The student understands how physical processes shape patterns in the physical environment. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(3)(A)","text":"explain weather conditions and climate in relation to annual changes in Earth-Sun relationships;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(3)(B)","text":"describe the physical processes that affect the environments of regions, including weather, tectonic forces, erosion, and soil-building processes; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(3)(C)","text":"describe how physical processes such as hurricanes, El Niño, earthquakes, and volcanoes affect the lithosphere, atmosphere, hydrosphere, and biosphere."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(4)","statement":"Geography. The student understands the patterns and characteristics of major landforms, climates, and ecosystems of Earth and the interrelated processes that produce them. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(4)(A)","text":"explain how elevation, latitude, wind systems, ocean currents, position on a continent, and mountain barriers influence temperature, precipitation, and distribution of climate regions;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(4)(B)","text":"describe different landforms such as plains, mountains, and islands and the physical processes that cause their development; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(4)(C)","text":"explain the influence of climate on the distribution of biomes in different regions."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(5)","statement":"Geography. The student understands how political, economic, and social processes shape cultural patterns and characteristics in various places and regions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(5)(A)","text":"analyze how the character of a place is related to its political, economic, social, and cultural elements; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(5)(B)","text":"interpret political, economic, social, and demographic indicators (gross domestic product per capita, life expectancy, literacy, and infant mortality) to determine the level of development and standard of living in nations using the levels as defined by the Human Development Index."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(6)","statement":"Geography. The student understands the types, patterns, and processes of settlement. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(6)(A)","text":"locate and describe human and physical features that influence the size and distribution of settlements; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(6)(B)","text":"explain the processes that have caused changes in settlement patterns, including urbanization, transportation, access to and availability of resources, and economic activities."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(7)","statement":"Geography. The student understands the growth, distribution, movement, and characteristics of world population. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(7)(A)","text":"analyze population pyramids and use other data, graphics, and maps to describe the population characteristics of different societies and to predict future population trends;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(7)(B)","text":"explain how physical geography and push and pull forces, including political, economic, social, and environmental conditions, affect the routes and flows of human migration;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(7)(C)","text":"describe trends in world population growth and distribution; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(7)(D)","text":"analyze how globalization affects connectivity, standard of living, pandemics, and loss of local culture."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(8)","statement":"Geography. The student understands how people, places, and environments are connected and interdependent. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(8)(A)","text":"compare ways that humans depend on, adapt to, and modify the physical environment, including the influences of culture and technology;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(8)(B)","text":"analyze the consequences of extreme weather and other natural disasters such as El Niño, floods, tsunamis, and volcanoes on people and their environment; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(8)(C)","text":"evaluate the economic and political relationships between settlements and the environment, including sustainable development and renewable/non-renewable resources."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(9)","statement":"Geography. The student understands the concept of region as an area of Earth's surface with related geographic characteristics. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(9)(A)","text":"identify physical and/or human factors such as climate, vegetation, language, trade networks, political units, river systems, and religion that constitute a region; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(9)(B)","text":"describe different types of regions, including formal, functional, and perceptual regions."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(10)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the distribution, characteristics, and interactions of the economic systems in the world. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(10)(A)","text":"describe the forces that determine the distribution of goods and services in traditional, free enterprise, socialist, and communist economic systems;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(10)(B)","text":"classify countries along the economic spectrum between free enterprise and communism;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(10)(C)","text":"compare the ways people satisfy their basic needs through the production of goods and services such as subsistence agriculture versus commercial agriculture or cottage industries versus commercial industries; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(10)(D)","text":"compare global trade patterns over time and analyze the implications of globalization, including outsourcing and free trade zones."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(11)","statement":"Economics. The student understands how geography influences economic activities. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(11)(A)","text":"understand the connections between levels of development and economic activities (primary, secondary, tertiary, and quaternary);"},{"code":"113.43(d)(11)(B)","text":"identify the factors affecting the location of different types of economic activities, including subsistence and commercial agriculture, manufacturing, and service industries; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(11)(C)","text":"assess how changes in climate, resources, and infrastructure (technology, transportation, and communication) affect the location and patterns of economic activities."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(12)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the economic importance of, and issues related to, the location and management of resources. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(12)(A)","text":"analyze how the creation, distribution, and management of key natural resources affects the location and patterns of movement of products, money, and people; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(12)(B)","text":"evaluate the geographic and economic impact of policies related to the development, use, and scarcity of natural resources such as regulations of water."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(13)","statement":"Government. The student understands the spatial characteristics of a variety of global political units. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(13)(A)","text":"interpret maps to explain the division of land, including man-made and natural borders, into separate political units such as cities, states, or countries; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(13)(B)","text":"compare maps of voting patterns and political boundaries to make inferences about the distribution of political power."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(14)","statement":"Government. The student understands the processes that influence political divisions, relationships, and policies. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(14)(A)","text":"analyze current events to infer the physical and human processes that lead to the formation of boundaries and other political divisions;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(14)(B)","text":"compare how democracy, dictatorship, monarchy, republic, theocracy, and totalitarian systems operate in specific countries; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(14)(C)","text":"analyze the human and physical factors that influence control of territories and resources, conflict/war, and international relations of sovereign nations such as China, the United States, Japan, and Russia and international organizations such as the United Nations (UN) and the European Union (EU)."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(15)","statement":"Citizenship. The student understands how different points of view influence the development of public policies and decision-making processes at national and international levels. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(15)(A)","text":"identify and give examples of different points of view that influence the development of public policies and decision-making processes at national and international levels; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(15)(B)","text":"explain how citizenship practices, public policies, and decision making may be influenced by cultural beliefs, including nationalism and patriotism."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(16)","statement":"Culture. The student understands how the components of culture affect the way people live and shape the characteristics of regions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(16)(A)","text":"describe distinctive cultural patterns and landscapes associated with different places in Texas, the United States, and other regions of the world and how these patterns influenced the processes of innovation and diffusion;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(16)(B)","text":"describe elements of culture, including language, religion, beliefs, institutions, and technologies; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(16)(C)","text":"describe life in a variety of urban and rural areas in the world to compare political, economic, social, and environmental changes."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(17)","statement":"Culture. The student understands the distribution, patterns, and characteristics of different cultures. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(17)(A)","text":"describe and compare patterns of culture such as language, religion, land use, education, and customs that make specific regions of the world distinctive;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(17)(B)","text":"describe central ideas and spatial distribution of major religious traditions, including Buddhism, Christianity, Hinduism, Islam, Judaism, and Sikhism;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(17)(C)","text":"compare economic, political, or social opportunities in different cultures for underrepresented populations such as women and ethnic and religious minorities; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(17)(D)","text":"evaluate the experiences and contributions of diverse groups to multicultural societies."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(18)","statement":"Culture. The student understands the ways in which cultures change and maintain continuity. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(18)(A)","text":"analyze cultural changes in specific regions caused by migration, war, trade, innovations, and diffusion;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(18)(B)","text":"assess causes and effects of conflicts between groups of people, including modern genocides and terrorism;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(18)(C)","text":"identify examples of cultures that maintain traditional ways, including traditional economies; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(18)(D)","text":"evaluate the spread of cultural traits to find examples of cultural convergence and divergence such as the spread of democratic ideas, language, foods, technology, or global sports."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(19)","statement":"Science, technology, and society. The student understands the impact of technology and human modifications on the physical environment. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(19)(A)","text":"evaluate the significance of major technological innovations in the areas of transportation and energy that have been used to modify the physical environment;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(19)(B)","text":"analyze ways technological innovations such as air conditioning and desalinization have allowed humans to adapt to places; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(19)(C)","text":"analyze the environmental, economic, and social impacts of advances in technology on agriculture and natural resources."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(20)","statement":"Science, technology, and society. The student understands how current technology affects human interaction. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(20)(A)","text":"describe the impact of new information technologies such as the Internet, Global Positioning System (GPS), or Geographic Information Systems (GIS); and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(20)(B)","text":"examine the economic, environmental, and social effects of technology such as medical advancements or changing trade patterns on societies at different levels of development."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(21)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student applies critical-thinking skills to organize and use information acquired through established research methodologies from a variety of valid sources, including technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(21)(A)","text":"analyze and evaluate a variety of sources of geographic information such as primary and secondary sources, aerial photographs, and maps for validity, utility, credibility, bias, and accuracy;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(21)(B)","text":"identify places of contemporary geopolitical significance on a map;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(21)(C)","text":"create and interpret different types of maps to answer geographic questions, infer relationships, and analyze change;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(21)(D)","text":"analyze information by sequencing, categorizing, identifying cause-and-effect relationships, comparing, contrasting, finding the main idea, summarizing, making generalizations and predictions, drawing inferences and conclusions, and developing connections over time;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(21)(E)","text":"identify different points of view about an issue or current topic; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(21)(F)","text":"formulate and communicate visually, orally, or in writing a claim supported by evidence and reasoning for an intended audience and purpose."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(22)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student communicates in written, oral, and visual forms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(22)(A)","text":"create appropriate graphics such as maps, diagrams, tables, and graphs to communicate geographic features, distributions, and relationships;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(22)(B)","text":"generate summaries, generalizations, and thesis statements supported by evidence;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(22)(C)","text":"use social studies terminology correctly;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(22)(D)","text":"create original work using effective written communication skills, including proper citations and understanding and avoiding plagiarism; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(22)(E)","text":"apply foundational language skills to engage in civil discourse about social studies topics, including those with multiple perspectives."}]},{"code":"113.43(d)(23)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student uses problem-solving and decision-making skills, working independently and with others. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.43(d)(23)(A)","text":"explain governmental and democratic processes such as voting, due process, and caucuses using simulations and models;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(23)(B)","text":"plan, organize, and complete a research project that involves asking geographic questions; acquiring, organizing, and analyzing information; answering questions; and communicating results;"},{"code":"113.43(d)(23)(C)","text":"use case studies and GIS to identify contemporary challenges and to answer real-world questions; and"},{"code":"113.43(d)(23)(D)","text":"use problem-solving and decision-making processes to identify a problem, gather information, list and consider options, consider advantages and disadvantages, choose and implement a solution, and evaluate the effectiveness of the solution."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Geographer's Toolkit: Maps, Regions & Spatial Thinking","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(9)","113.43(d)(21)","113.43(d)(22)"],"items":["Five themes of geography and spatial-thinking vocabulary","Map types, projections, scale, and distortion (Mercator vs. Robinson)","Latitude/longitude, GPS, GIS, and remote sensing tools","Formal, functional, and perceptual regions (the region concept)","Reading and building thematic maps, graphs, and infographics","Evaluating credibility of sources for geographic research"]},{"unit":"Physical Processes & Earth's Landforms","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(3)"],"items":["Earth's structure and plate tectonics","Volcanoes, earthquakes, and the Ring of Fire","Weathering, erosion, and deposition","Rivers, glaciers, wind, and coastal landform building","Mapping global landform patterns and their causes"]},{"unit":"Climate, Weather & World Ecosystems","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(4)"],"items":["Weather vs. climate; controls of climate (latitude, elevation, currents)","Global wind belts and ocean current patterns","World climate regions (Koppen overview)","Major biomes and ecosystems and their distribution","Interrelated processes: climate-soil-vegetation links","Reading and interpreting climographs"]},{"unit":"Humans, Resources & Environmental Modification","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(12)","113.43(d)(19)"],"items":["Renewable vs. nonrenewable resources and their global distribution","Resource management, scarcity, and conflict (water, oil, minerals)","Human modification: dams, irrigation, deforestation, urban sprawl","Pollution, climate change, and environmental degradation","Sustainability and conservation strategies","Case study of a major human-engineered landscape (e.g., Aral Sea, Three Gorges Dam)"]},{"unit":"World Population Patterns","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(7)"],"items":["Population distribution and density: where people live and why","Population pyramids and age structure","The Demographic Transition Model","Birth rates, death rates, and natural increase","Migration: push/pull factors and refugee movements","Overpopulation vs. aging-population challenges"]},{"unit":"Settlement & Urbanization","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(6)"],"items":["Rural vs. urban settlement patterns","Site and situation: why cities locate where they do","Urban land-use models","Megacities, urbanization, and megalopolis growth","Challenges of rapid urban growth (informal settlements, infrastructure)"]},{"unit":"Components of Culture","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(16)","113.43(d)(17)"],"items":["Elements of culture: language, religion, customs, institutions","Cultural landscapes and how culture shapes place","World language families and their distribution","Major world religions: origins and spread","Cultural regions, hearths, and realms","Ethnicity, cultural diversity, and pluralism"]},{"unit":"Cultural Change, Diffusion & Continuity","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(1)","113.43(d)(18)"],"items":["Types of diffusion (relocation, expansion, contagious, hierarchical)","Historic spread: trade routes, empires, and the Columbian Exchange","Cultural convergence vs. divergence; effects of globalization","Acculturation, assimilation, and cultural preservation","Tradition and continuity in a changing world"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester 1 Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["113.43(d)(21)","113.43(d)(22)","113.43(d)(23)"],"items":["Spiral-review stations: physical, population, and cultural geography","Skills lab: analyzing maps, data, and primary sources (research methodology)","Structured academic writing: document-based geographic response","Semester exam and error-analysis reflection"]},{"unit":"Economic Systems & Development","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(10)"],"items":["Traditional, command, market, and mixed economies","Levels of economic activity (primary through quaternary)","Measuring development: GDP, HDI, and standard of living","More-developed vs. less-developed countries","Comparing economic systems through country case studies"]},{"unit":"Economic Activity & Global Interdependence","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(11)","113.43(d)(8)"],"items":["How physical geography shapes economic activity (agriculture, industry)","Trade, comparative advantage, and global supply chains","Transportation, communication, and connectivity networks","Interdependence: how events in one region affect others","Globalization, trade blocs, and multinational corporations"]},{"unit":"Political Geography: States & Boundaries","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(13)","113.43(d)(14)"],"items":["States, nations, nation-states, and sovereignty","Types of boundaries and boundary disputes","Shapes of states and their effects (compact, fragmented, elongated)","Supranational organizations (UN, EU, NATO)","Devolution, separatism, and shifting political maps"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["113.43(d)(23)"],"items":["Reteach targeted TEKS based on formative-data gaps","Decision-making simulation: geographic problem-solving scenario","Enrichment/extension or make-up assessment","Cushion for calendar shifts and testing days"]},{"unit":"Citizenship & Public Policy Across Borders","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(15)","113.43(d)(5)"],"items":["How different points of view shape public policy","Political, economic, and social processes behind cultural patterns","Global-issues debate: environment, human rights, or trade policy","National vs. international decision-making processes","Case study of an international policy negotiation"]},{"unit":"Technology, Society & Human Interaction","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(20)"],"items":["Communication technology and time-space compression","Internet, social media, and global information flow","Technology's role in trade, migration, and cultural exchange","The digital divide and unequal access","Emerging technology and its social impact"]},{"unit":"Change Over Time & Sustainability","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(2)"],"items":["How places and environments change over time","Effects of environmental change on human systems","Land-use change and sustainability case studies","Human responses to natural hazards and environmental risk","Planning for sustainable futures"]},{"unit":"Regional Synthesis & Capstone Research Project","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.43(d)(21)","113.43(d)(22)","113.43(d)(23)"],"items":["Compare two world regions across physical, cultural, economic, and political lenses","Independent research using valid, varied sources (methodology)","Constructing a data-supported geographic argument","Building and delivering a visual presentation (oral/visual communication)","Peer review and collaborative decision-making"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Exam (wiggle)","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["113.43(d)(21)","113.43(d)(22)","113.43(d)(23)"],"items":["Cumulative review stations across all six grading periods","Skills consolidation: maps, data, sources, and writing","Comprehensive final exam","Course reflection and synthesis of geographic thinking"]}]},{"course":"World History Studies (One Credit), Adopted 2022","tacSection":"19 TAC §113.42","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["http://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter113_sec.113.42","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-113"],"strands":[{"code":"113.42(d)(1)","statement":"History. The student understands traditional historical points of reference in world history. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(1)(A)","text":"identify major causes and describe the major effects of the following events from 8000 BC to 500 BC: the development of agriculture and the development of the river valley civilizations;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(1)(B)","text":"identify major causes and describe the major effects of the following events from 500 BC to AD 600: the development of the classical civilizations of Greece, Rome, Persia, India (Maurya and Gupta), China (Zhou, Qin, and Han), and the development of major world religions;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(1)(C)","text":"identify major causes and describe the major effects of the following important turning points in world history from 600 to 1450: the spread of major world religions and their impact on Asia, Africa, and Europe and the Mongol invasions and their impact on Europe, China, India, and Southwest Asia;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(1)(D)","text":"identify major causes and describe the major effects of the following important turning points in world history from 1450 to 1750: the rise of the Ottoman Empire, the influence of the Ming dynasty on world trade, European exploration and the Columbian Exchange, European expansion, and the Renaissance and the Reformation;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(1)(E)","text":"identify major causes and describe the major effects of the following important turning points in world history from 1750 to 1914: the Scientific Revolution, the Industrial Revolution and its impact on the development of modern economic systems, European imperialism, and the Enlightenment's impact on political revolutions; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(1)(F)","text":"identify major causes and describe the major effects of the following important turning points in world history from 1914 to the present: the world wars and their impact on political, economic, and social systems; communist revolutions and their impact on the Cold War; independence movements; and globalization."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(2)","statement":"History. The student understands how early civilizations developed from 8000 BC to 500 BC. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(2)(A)","text":"summarize the impact of the development of farming (Neolithic Revolution) on the creation of river valley civilizations;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(2)(B)","text":"identify the characteristics of civilization; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(2)(C)","text":"explain how major river valley civilizations influenced the development of the classical civilizations."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(3)","statement":"History. The student understands the contributions and influence of classical civilizations from 500 BC to AD 600 on subsequent civilizations. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(3)(A)","text":"describe the major political, religious/philosophical, and cultural influences of Persia, India, China, Israel, Greece, and Rome;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(3)(B)","text":"explain the impact of the fall of Rome on Western Europe; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(3)(C)","text":"compare the factors that led to the collapse of Rome and Han China."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(4)","statement":"History. The student understands how, after the collapse of classical empires, new political, economic, and social systems evolved and expanded from 600 to 1450. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(4)(A)","text":"explain the development of Roman Catholicism and Eastern Orthodoxy as social and political factors in medieval Europe and the Byzantine Empire;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(4)(B)","text":"describe the major characteristics of and the factors contributing to the development of the political/social system of feudalism and the economic system of manorialism;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(4)(C)","text":"explain the political, economic, and social impact of Islam on Europe, Asia, and Africa;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(4)(D)","text":"describe the interactions among Muslim, Christian, and Jewish societies in Europe, Asia, and North Africa;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(4)(E)","text":"describe the interactions between Muslim and Hindu societies in South Asia;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(4)(F)","text":"explain how the Crusades, the Black Death, and the Hundred Years' War contributed to the end of medieval Europe;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(4)(G)","text":"summarize the major political, economic, and cultural developments in Tang and Song China and their impact on Eastern Asia;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(4)(H)","text":"explain the evolution and expansion of the slave trade;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(4)(I)","text":"analyze how the Silk Road and the African gold-salt trade facilitated the spread of ideas and trade; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(4)(J)","text":"summarize the changes resulting from the Mongol invasions of Russia, China, and the Islamic world."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(5)","statement":"History. The student understands the causes, characteristics, and impact of the European Renaissance and the Reformation from 1450 to 1750. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(5)(A)","text":"explain the political, intellectual, artistic, economic, and religious impact of the Renaissance; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(5)(B)","text":"explain the political, intellectual, artistic, economic, and religious impact of the Reformation."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(6)","statement":"History. The student understands the characteristics and impact of the Maya, Inca, and Aztec civilizations. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(6)(A)","text":"compare the major political, economic, social, and cultural developments of the Maya, Inca, and Aztec civilizations and explain how prior civilizations influenced their development; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(6)(B)","text":"explain how the Inca and Aztec empires were impacted by European exploration/colonization."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(7)","statement":"History. The student understands the causes and impact of increased global interaction from 1450 to 1750. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(7)(A)","text":"analyze the causes of European expansion from 1450 to 1750;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(7)(B)","text":"explain the impact of the Columbian Exchange;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(7)(C)","text":"explain the impact of the Atlantic slave trade on West Africa and the Americas;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(7)(D)","text":"explain the impact of the Ottoman Empire on Eastern Europe and global trade;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(7)(E)","text":"explain Ming China's impact on global trade; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(7)(F)","text":"explain new economic factors and principles of Europe's Commercial Revolution."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(8)","statement":"History. The student understands the causes and the global impact of the Industrial Revolution and European imperialism from 1750 to 1914. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(8)(A)","text":"explain how the Industrial Revolution led to political, economic, and social changes;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(8)(B)","text":"identify the major political, economic, and social motivations that influenced European imperialism;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(8)(C)","text":"explain the major characteristics and impact of European imperialism; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(8)(D)","text":"explain the effects of free enterprise in the Industrial Revolution."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(9)","statement":"History. The student understands the causes and effects of major political revolutions between 1750 and 1914. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(9)(A)","text":"compare the causes, characteristics, and consequences of the American and French revolutions, emphasizing the role of the Enlightenment;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(9)(B)","text":"explain the impact of Napoleon Bonaparte and the Napoleonic Wars on Europe and Latin America;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(9)(C)","text":"trace the influence of the American and French revolutions on Latin America, including the role of Simón Bolivar; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(9)(D)","text":"identify the influence of ideas such as separation of powers, checks and balances, liberty, equality, democracy, popular sovereignty, human rights, constitutionalism, and nationalism on political revolutions."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(10)","statement":"History. The student understands the causes and impact of World War I. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(10)(A)","text":"identify the importance of imperialism, nationalism, militarism, and the alliance system in causing World War I;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(10)(B)","text":"identify major characteristics of World War I, including total war, trench warfare, modern military technology, and high casualty rates;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(10)(C)","text":"explain the political and economic impact of the Treaty of Versailles, including changes in boundaries and the mandate system; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(10)(D)","text":"identify the causes of the February (March) and October (November) revolutions of 1917 in Russia, their effects on the outcome of World War I, and the Bolshevik establishment of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(11)","statement":"History. The student understands the causes and impact of the global economic depression immediately following World War I. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(11)(A)","text":"summarize the international, political, and economic causes of the global depression; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(11)(B)","text":"explain the responses of governments to the global depression such as in the United States, Germany, Great Britain, and France."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(12)","statement":"History. The student understands the causes and impact of World War II. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(12)(A)","text":"describe the emergence and characteristics of totalitarianism;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(12)(B)","text":"explain the roles of various world leaders, including Benito Mussolini, Adolf Hitler, Hideki Tojo, Joseph Stalin, Franklin D. Roosevelt, and Winston Churchill, prior to and during World War II; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(12)(C)","text":"explain the major causes and events of World War II, including the German invasions of Poland and the Soviet Union, the Holocaust, the attack on Pearl Harbor, the Normandy landings, and the dropping of the atomic bombs."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(13)","statement":"History. The student understands the impact of major events associated with the Cold War and independence movements. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(13)(A)","text":"summarize how the outcome of World War II contributed to the development of the Cold War;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(13)(B)","text":"summarize the factors that contributed to communism in China, including Mao Zedong's role in its rise;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(13)(C)","text":"identify major events of the Cold War, including the Korean War, the Vietnam War, and the arms race;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(13)(D)","text":"explain the roles of modern world leaders, including Ronald Reagan, Mikhail Gorbachev, Lech Walesa, and Pope John Paul II, in the collapse of communism in Eastern Europe and the Soviet Union;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(13)(E)","text":"summarize the rise of independence movements in Africa, the Middle East, and South Asia and reasons for ongoing conflicts; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(13)(F)","text":"discuss factors contributing to the Arab-Israeli conflict, including the rejection of the existence of the state of Israel by the Arab League and a majority of Arab nations."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(14)","statement":"History. The student understands the development and use of radical Islamic terrorism in the second half of the 20th century and the early 21st century. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(14)(A)","text":"explain the impact of geopolitical influences on the development of radical Islamic terrorism;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(14)(B)","text":"explain the impact of radical Islamic terrorism on global events; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(14)(C)","text":"explain the U.S. response to the events surrounding September 11, 2001, and other acts of radical Islamic terrorism."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(15)","statement":"Geography. The student understands the impact of geographic factors on major historic events and processes. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(15)(A)","text":"locate places and regions of historical significance directly related to major eras and turning points in world history;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(15)(B)","text":"analyze the influence of human and physical geographic factors on major events in world history such as the development of river valley civilizations, trade in the Indian Ocean, and the opening of the Panama and Suez canals; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(15)(C)","text":"interpret maps, charts, and graphs to explain how geography has influenced people and events in the past."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(16)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the impact of the Neolithic and Industrial revolutions and globalization on humanity. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(16)(A)","text":"identify important changes in human life caused by the Neolithic Revolution;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(16)(B)","text":"summarize the role of economics in driving political changes as related to the Industrial Revolution; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(16)(C)","text":"describe the economic impact of globalization."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(17)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the historical origins of contemporary economic systems and the benefits of free enterprise in world history. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(17)(A)","text":"identify the historical origins and characteristics of the free enterprise system, including the influence of Adam Smith;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(17)(B)","text":"identify the historical origins and characteristics of communism, including the influence of Karl Marx;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(17)(C)","text":"identify the historical origins and characteristics of socialism;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(17)(D)","text":"identify the historical origins and characteristics of fascism; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(17)(E)","text":"explain why communist command economies collapsed in competition with free market economies at the end of the 20th century."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(18)","statement":"Government. The student understands the characteristics of major political systems throughout history. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(18)(A)","text":"identify the characteristics of monarchies and theocracies as forms of government in early civilizations; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(18)(B)","text":"identify the characteristics of the following political systems: theocracy, absolute monarchy, democracy, republic, oligarchy, limited monarchy, and totalitarianism."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(19)","statement":"Government. The student understands how contemporary political systems have developed from earlier systems of government. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(19)(A)","text":"explain the development of democratic-republican government from its beginnings in Judeo-Christian legal tradition and classical Greece and Rome through the French Revolution;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(19)(B)","text":"identify the impact of political and legal ideas contained in the following documents: Hammurabi's Code, the Jewish Ten Commandments, Justinian's Code of Laws, Magna Carta, the English Bill of Rights, the Declaration of Independence, the U.S. Constitution, and the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(19)(C)","text":"explain the political philosophies of individuals such as John Locke, Thomas Hobbes, Voltaire, Charles de Montesquieu, Jean Jacques Rousseau, Thomas Aquinas, John Calvin, and William Blackstone; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(19)(D)","text":"explain the significance of the League of Nations and the United Nations."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(20)","statement":"Citizenship. The student understands the significance of political choices and decisions made by individuals, groups, and nations throughout history. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(20)(A)","text":"describe how people have participated in supporting or changing their governments;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(20)(B)","text":"describe the rights and responsibilities of citizens and noncitizens in civic participation throughout history; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(20)(C)","text":"identify examples of key persons who were successful in shifting political thought, including William Wilberforce."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(21)","statement":"Citizenship. The student understands the historical development of significant legal and political concepts related to the rights and responsibilities of citizenship. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(21)(A)","text":"summarize the development of the rule of law from ancient to modern times;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(21)(B)","text":"identify the origins of ideas regarding the right to a \"trial by a jury of your peers\" and the concepts of \"innocent until proven guilty\" and \"equality before the law\" from sources including the Judeo-Christian legal tradition and in Greece and Rome;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(21)(C)","text":"identify examples of politically motivated mass murders such as in Cambodia, China, Latin America, and the Soviet Union;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(21)(D)","text":"identify examples of genocide, including the Holocaust and genocide in Armenia, the Balkans, Rwanda, and Darfur;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(21)(E)","text":"identify examples of individuals who led resistance to political oppression such as Nelson Mandela, Mohandas Gandhi, Las Madres de la Plaza de Mayo, and Chinese student protestors in Tiananmen Square; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(21)(F)","text":"identify examples of American ideals that have advanced human rights and democratic ideas throughout the world."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(22)","statement":"Culture. The student understands the history and relevance of major religious and philosophical traditions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(22)(A)","text":"describe the historical origins and central ideas in the development of monotheism;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(22)(B)","text":"describe the historical origins, central ideas, and spread of major religious and philosophical traditions, including Buddhism, Christianity, Confucianism, Hinduism, Islam, Judaism, and Sikhism; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(22)(C)","text":"identify examples of religious influence on various events referenced in the major eras of world history."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(23)","statement":"Culture. The student understands the roles of women, children, and families in different historical cultures. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(23)(A)","text":"describe the changing roles of women, children, and families during major eras of world history; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(23)(B)","text":"describe the major influences of women during major eras of world history such as Elizabeth I, Queen Victoria, Mother Teresa, Indira Gandhi, Margaret Thatcher, and Golda Meir."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(24)","statement":"Culture. The student understands how the development of ideas has influenced institutions and societies. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(24)(A)","text":"summarize the fundamental ideas and institutions of Eastern civilizations that originated in China and India;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(24)(B)","text":"summarize the fundamental ideas and institutions of Western civilizations that originated in Greece and Rome;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(24)(C)","text":"explain how the relationship between Christianity and Humanism that began with the Renaissance influenced subsequent political developments; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(24)(D)","text":"explain how geopolitical and religious influences have impacted law and government in the Muslim world."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(25)","statement":"Culture. The student understands the relationship between the arts and the times during which they were created. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(25)(A)","text":"analyze examples of how art, architecture, literature, music, and drama reflect the history of the cultures in which they are produced; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(25)(B)","text":"describe examples of art, music, and literature that transcend the cultures in which they were created and convey universal themes."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(26)","statement":"Science, technology, and society. The student understands how major scientific and mathematical discoveries and technological innovations affected societies prior to 1750. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(26)(A)","text":"identify the origin and diffusion of major ideas in mathematics, science, and technology that occurred in river valley civilizations, classical Greece and Rome, classical India, the Islamic caliphates between 700 and 1200, and China from the Tang to Ming dynasties;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(26)(B)","text":"summarize the major ideas in astronomy, mathematics, and architectural engineering that developed in the Maya, Inca, and Aztec civilizations;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(26)(C)","text":"explain the impact of the printing press on the Renaissance and the Reformation in Europe;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(26)(D)","text":"describe the origins of the Scientific Revolution in 16th century Europe and explain its impact on scientific thinking worldwide; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(26)(E)","text":"identify the contributions of significant scientists such as Archimedes, Copernicus, Eratosthenes, Galileo, Pythagoras, Isaac Newton, and Robert Boyle."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(27)","statement":"Science, technology, and society. The student understands how major scientific and mathematical discoveries and technological innovations have affected societies from 1750 to the present. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(27)(A)","text":"explain the role of textile manufacturing, steam technology, development of the factory system, and transportation technology in the Industrial Revolution;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(27)(B)","text":"explain the roles of military technology, transportation technology, communication technology, and medical advancements in initiating and advancing 19th century imperialism;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(27)(C)","text":"explain the effects of major new military technologies on World War I, World War II, and the Cold War;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(27)(D)","text":"explain the role of telecommunication technology, computer technology, transportation technology, and medical advancements in developing the modern global economy and society; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(27)(E)","text":"identify the contributions of significant scientists and inventors such as Marie Curie, Thomas Edison, Albert Einstein, Louis Pasteur, and James Watt."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(28)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student understands how historians use historiography to interpret the past and applies critical-thinking skills to organize and use information acquired from a variety of valid sources, including technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(28)(A)","text":"identify methods used by archaeologists, anthropologists, historians, and geographers to analyze evidence;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(28)(B)","text":"explain how historians analyze sources for frame of reference, historical context, and point of view to interpret historical events;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(28)(C)","text":"analyze primary and secondary sources to determine frame of reference, historical context, and point of view;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(28)(D)","text":"evaluate a variety of historical and contemporary sources for validity, credibility, bias, and accuracy;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(28)(E)","text":"analyze information by sequencing, categorizing, identifying cause-and-effect relationships, comparing, contrasting, finding the main idea, summarizing, making generalizations and predictions, drawing inferences and conclusions, and developing connections between historical events over time; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(28)(F)","text":"formulate and communicate visually, orally, or in writing a claim supported by evidence and reasoning for an intended audience and purpose."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(29)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student uses geographic skills and tools to collect, analyze, and interpret data. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(29)(A)","text":"create and interpret thematic maps, graphs, and charts to demonstrate the relationship between geography and the historical development of a region or nation; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(29)(B)","text":"analyze and compare geographic distributions and patterns in world history shown on maps, graphs, charts, and models."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(30)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student communicates in written, oral, and visual forms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(30)(A)","text":"use social studies terminology correctly;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(30)(B)","text":"use effective written communication skills, including proper citations and avoiding plagiarism;"},{"code":"113.42(d)(30)(C)","text":"interpret and create written, oral, and visual presentations of social studies information; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(30)(D)","text":"apply foundational language skills to engage in civil discourse about social studies topics, including those with multiple perspectives."}]},{"code":"113.42(d)(31)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student uses problem-solving and decision-making skills, working independently and with others. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.42(d)(31)(A)","text":"explain governmental and democratic processes such as voting, due process, and caucuses using simulations and models; and"},{"code":"113.42(d)(31)(B)","text":"use problem-solving and decision-making processes to identify a problem, gather information, list and consider options, consider advantages and disadvantages, choose and implement a solution, and evaluate the effectiveness of the solution."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Foundations of History & Geographic Thinking","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(1)","113.42(d)(28)","113.42(d)(29)","113.42(d)(30)","113.42(d)(31)"],"items":["Eras and periodization: build a big-picture timeline of major turning points in world history (TEKS 1)","Reading like a historian: sourcing, corroboration, and context with primary vs. secondary sources (TEKS 28)","Map skills lab: latitude/longitude, thematic maps, and interpreting geographic data and tools (TEKS 29)","Communicating history: claim-evidence-reasoning paragraphs and visual one-pagers (TEKS 30)","Structured decision-making protocol: weighing options and consequences in a historical dilemma, alone and in groups (TEKS 31)"]},{"unit":"Early Civilizations & the Neolithic Revolution (8000–500 BC)","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(2)","113.42(d)(15)","113.42(d)(16)","113.42(d)(23)","113.42(d)(26)"],"items":["The Neolithic Revolution: from hunter-gatherers to agriculture and its lasting economic impact (TEKS 2, 16)","River-valley civilizations—Mesopotamia, Egypt, Indus, China—and why geography put them there (TEKS 2, 15)","Features of early civilizations: cities, writing, job specialization, and social hierarchy (TEKS 2)","Roles of women, children, and families in the earliest societies (TEKS 23)","Early technologies: bronze, the wheel, irrigation, and the first writing systems (TEKS 26)"]},{"unit":"Classical Civilizations: Greece & Rome (500 BC–AD 600)","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(3)","113.42(d)(18)","113.42(d)(20)","113.42(d)(21)","113.42(d)(25)"],"items":["Greek city-states: direct democracy in Athens vs. oligarchy and militarism in Sparta (TEKS 18)","Roman Republic to Empire: Roman law, the Twelve Tables, and evolving concepts of citizenship (TEKS 21)","Political choices of leaders and citizens: Pericles, the Gracchi, Caesar, and Augustus (TEKS 20)","Enduring contributions of Greece and Rome to later civilizations (TEKS 3)","Art and architecture as a mirror of the classical age: temples, sculpture, and drama (TEKS 25)"]},{"unit":"Classical Legacies: World Religions & Philosophies","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(22)","113.42(d)(24)"],"items":["Origins and core beliefs of Hinduism, Buddhism, Judaism, Christianity, and Islam (TEKS 22)","Classical philosophies: Confucianism, Daoism, and Greek philosophy (TEKS 22)","How major ideas shaped institutions, law, and everyday society (TEKS 24)","Comparative-religions graphic organizer and a Socratic seminar on belief and power (TEKS 24)"]},{"unit":"The Post-Classical World (600–1450)","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(4)","113.42(d)(15)","113.42(d)(18)","113.42(d)(23)"],"items":["Rise and spread of Islam and the Caliphates; the Golden Age of learning (TEKS 4)","Byzantine Empire and Eastern Orthodox Christianity (TEKS 4)","Feudal Europe, manorialism, and the power of the Catholic Church (TEKS 18)","Medieval Africa and Asia: Mali/Songhai, Tang/Song China, and feudal Japan (TEKS 4, 15)","The Crusades and growing trade networks: Silk Roads and Indian Ocean routes (TEKS 15)","Roles of women, children, and families in medieval societies (TEKS 23)"]},{"unit":"Empires of the Americas: Maya, Inca & Aztec","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(6)","113.42(d)(25)"],"items":["Maya: city-states, mathematics, astronomy, and the calendar (TEKS 6)","Aztec Empire: Tenochtitlan, the tribute system, and religion (TEKS 6)","Inca Empire: roads, terrace farming, and administration of the Andes (TEKS 6)","Art, architecture, and monumental building across the Americas (TEKS 25)"]},{"unit":"Renaissance & Reformation (1450–1750)","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(5)","113.42(d)(22)","113.42(d)(24)","113.42(d)(25)","113.42(d)(26)"],"items":["The Italian Renaissance: humanism, patronage, and the rebirth of classical ideas (TEKS 5, 24)","Renaissance art and its relationship to its age: da Vinci, Michelangelo, Raphael (TEKS 25)","The Protestant Reformation: Luther, Calvin, and the power of the printing press (TEKS 5, 22)","Catholic Counter-Reformation and the wars of religion (TEKS 5)","Gutenberg's press and scientific/technological change before 1750 (TEKS 26)"]},{"unit":"Age of Global Interaction & Exploration (1450–1750)","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(7)","113.42(d)(15)","113.42(d)(17)","113.42(d)(26)"],"items":["Motives and technology of exploration: caravel, compass, and astrolabe (TEKS 7, 26)","The Columbian Exchange and its global demographic and ecological impact (TEKS 7, 15)","The Atlantic slave trade and the triangular trade system (TEKS 7)","Mercantilism and the historical origins of contemporary economic systems and free enterprise (TEKS 17)","Mapping empires: how geography shaped colonization and trade routes (TEKS 15)"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Benchmark","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["113.42(d)(1)","113.42(d)(28)","113.42(d)(30)"],"items":["Cumulative timeline review: 8000 BC to 1750 turning points and points of reference (TEKS 1)","Document-based-question practice and a historiography skills spiral (TEKS 28)","Semester-1 benchmark assessment with error analysis (TEKS 28)","Written and visual review products: student-built unit one-pagers (TEKS 30)"]},{"unit":"Scientific Revolution, Enlightenment & Political Revolutions (1750–1914)","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(9)","113.42(d)(19)","113.42(d)(21)","113.42(d)(24)","113.42(d)(27)"],"items":["The Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment thinkers: Locke, Montesquieu, Rousseau (TEKS 24, 27)","How Enlightenment ideas reshaped conceptions of government and rights (TEKS 19, 21)","The American and French Revolutions: causes and effects (TEKS 9)","Latin American independence movements: Bolivar, Hidalgo, and San Martin (TEKS 9)","From absolutism to constitutional and representative government (TEKS 19)","Rights of citizens compared: Declaration of the Rights of Man and the Bill of Rights (TEKS 21)"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["113.42(d)(28)","113.42(d)(31)"],"items":["Flex reteach day targeting strands flagged by benchmark data (TEKS 28)","Skills clinic: thesis writing, source sourcing, and DBQ scaffolds (TEKS 28)","Structured academic controversy: problem-solving and decision-making in teams (TEKS 31)","Catch-up and calendar-recovery day for lessons displaced by schedule shifts"]},{"unit":"Industrial Revolution & Imperialism (1750–1914)","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(8)","113.42(d)(16)","113.42(d)(17)","113.42(d)(23)","113.42(d)(27)"],"items":["Causes of the Industrial Revolution and the rise of the factory system (TEKS 8, 16)","Capitalism, free enterprise, and the economic systems of industrial society (TEKS 17)","Living and working conditions: women and children in factories and the changing family (TEKS 23)","Technological innovation 1750–present: steam, rail, telegraph, and mass production (TEKS 27)","Motives and methods of European imperialism in Africa and Asia (TEKS 8)","Resistance and responses to imperialism and its lasting global impact (TEKS 8)"]},{"unit":"World War I & the Post-War Depression","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(10)","113.42(d)(11)","113.42(d)(20)","113.42(d)(29)"],"items":["Long-term causes of WWI: militarism, alliances, imperialism, and nationalism (TEKS 10)","Total war, new technology, and the human cost of the Western Front (TEKS 10)","Political choices at Versailles and the redrawn map of postwar Europe (TEKS 20, 29)","The economic depression immediately following WWI: reparations and hyperinflation (TEKS 11)","Geographic-skills lab: mapping new nations and borders after 1918 (TEKS 29)"]},{"unit":"World War II","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(12)","113.42(d)(23)","113.42(d)(25)","113.42(d)(30)"],"items":["Rise of totalitarian regimes: fascism, Nazism, and militarism (TEKS 12)","Causes, course, and major turning points of WWII (TEKS 12)","The Holocaust and genocide: causes and human impact (TEKS 12, 23)","The home front: roles of women, children, and families in wartime (TEKS 23)","Wartime art, propaganda, and film as reflections of the era (TEKS 25)","Communicating history: an oral-history or visual documentary project (TEKS 30)"]},{"unit":"The Cold War & Independence Movements","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(13)","113.42(d)(15)","113.42(d)(18)","113.42(d)(19)","113.42(d)(29)"],"items":["Origins of the Cold War: superpower rivalry and competing political systems (TEKS 13, 18)","Cold War hot spots: Korea, Cuba, Vietnam, and the arms/space race (TEKS 13)","Decolonization and independence: India, Africa, and the Middle East (TEKS 13, 15)","How contemporary political systems developed from earlier ones—democracy vs. communism (TEKS 19)","The fall of communism and the end of the Cold War (TEKS 13)","Geographic-skills lab: mapping Cold War alliances and newly independent nations (TEKS 29)"]},{"unit":"Terrorism & the Contemporary World","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(14)","113.42(d)(20)","113.42(d)(27)"],"items":["Development of radical Islamic terrorism in the late 20th and early 21st centuries (TEKS 14)","September 11 and global responses to terrorism (TEKS 14, 20)","Technology, media, and globalization in the modern era (TEKS 27)","Political choices and their consequences in responding to terrorism (TEKS 20)"]},{"unit":"Global Economy, Free Enterprise & Modern Political Systems","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.42(d)(16)","113.42(d)(17)","113.42(d)(19)"],"items":["Globalization and the workings of the modern global economy (TEKS 16)","Benefits of free enterprise and the origins of today's economic systems (TEKS 17)","Comparing major modern political systems and how they developed from earlier ones (TEKS 19)","Synthesis project: connecting economics, government, and world history across eras (TEKS 16, 17, 19)"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Exam","weeks":2,"kind":"assessment","teks":["113.42(d)(1)","113.42(d)(28)","113.42(d)(30)","113.42(d)(31)"],"items":["Cumulative course review: traditional points of reference across all eras (TEKS 1)","Historiography and source-analysis capstone review (TEKS 28)","Comprehensive World History final exam (TEKS 1, 28)","Communication capstone: written, oral, and visual synthesis presentation (TEKS 30)","Independent and collaborative problem-solving showcase (TEKS 31)"]}]},{"course":"United States History Studies Since 1877 (One Credit), Adopted 2022","tacSection":"19 TAC §113.41","hasStaarEOC":true,"sources":["http://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter113_sec.113.41","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-113"],"strands":[{"code":"113.41(d)(1)","statement":"History. The student understands the principles included in the Celebrate Freedom Week program. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(1)(A)","text":"analyze and evaluate the text, intent, meaning, and importance of the Declaration of Independence and the U.S. Constitution, including the Bill of Rights;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(1)(B)","text":"analyze and evaluate the application of these founding principles to historical events in U.S. history; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(1)(C)","text":"explain the meaning and historical significance of the mottos \"E Pluribus Unum\" and \"In God We Trust.\""}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(2)","statement":"History. The student understands traditional historical points of reference in U.S. history from 1877 to the present. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(2)(A)","text":"identify the major eras in U.S. history from 1877 to the present and describe their defining characteristics; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(2)(B)","text":"explain the significance of the following years as turning points: 1898 (Spanish-American War), 1914-1918 (World War I), 1929 (the Great Depression begins), 1939-1945 (World War II), 1957 (Sputnik launch ignites U.S.-Soviet space race), 1968 (Martin Luther King Jr. assassination), 1969 (U.S. lands on the moon), 1991 (Cold War ends), 2001 (terrorist attacks on World Trade Center and the Pentagon), and 2008 (election of first black president, Barack Obama)."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(3)","statement":"History. The student understands the political, economic, and social changes in the United States from 1877 to 1898. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(3)(A)","text":"analyze political issues such as Indian policies, the growth of political machines, and civil service reform;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(3)(B)","text":"analyze economic issues such as industrialization, the growth of railroads, the growth of labor unions, farm issues, the cattle industry boom, the growth of entrepreneurship, and the pros and cons of big business; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(3)(C)","text":"analyze social issues affecting women, minorities, children, immigrants, and urbanization."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(4)","statement":"History. The student understands the emergence of the United States as a world power between 1898 and 1920. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(4)(A)","text":"explain why significant events, policies, and individuals, including the Spanish-American War, U.S. expansionism, Alfred Thayer Mahan, Theodore Roosevelt, and Sanford B. Dole moved the United States into the position of a world power;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(4)(B)","text":"evaluate American expansionism, including acquisitions such as Guam, Hawaii, the Philippines, and Puerto Rico;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(4)(C)","text":"identify the causes of World War I and reasons for U.S. entry;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(4)(D)","text":"understand the contributions of the American Expeditionary Forces (AEF) led by General John J. Pershing, including the Battle of Argonne Forest;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(4)(E)","text":"analyze the impact of machine guns, airplanes, tanks, poison gas, and trench warfare as significant technological innovations in World War I on the Western Front; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(4)(F)","text":"analyze major issues raised by U.S. involvement in World War I, including isolationism, neutrality, Woodrow Wilson's Fourteen Points, and the Treaty of Versailles."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(5)","statement":"History. The student understands the effects of reform and third-party movements in the early 20th century. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(5)(A)","text":"analyze the impact of Progressive Era reforms, including initiative, referendum, recall, and the passage of the 16th, 17th, 18th, and 19th amendments;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(5)(B)","text":"evaluate the impact of muckrakers and reform leaders such as Upton Sinclair, Susan B. Anthony, Jane Addams, Ida B. Wells, and W. E. B. DuBois on American society; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(5)(C)","text":"analyze the impact of third parties, including the Populist and Progressive parties."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(6)","statement":"History. The student understands significant events, social issues, and individuals of the 1920s. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(6)(A)","text":"analyze causes and effects of events and social issues such as immigration, Social Darwinism, the Scopes Trial, eugenics, race relations, nativism, the Red Scare, Prohibition, and the changing role of women; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(6)(B)","text":"analyze the impact of significant individuals such as Henry Ford, Marcus Garvey, and Charles A. Lindbergh."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(7)","statement":"History. The student understands the domestic and international impact of U.S. participation in World War II. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(7)(A)","text":"identify reasons for U.S. involvement in World War II, including the aggression of Italian, German, and Japanese dictatorships, especially the attack on Pearl Harbor;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(7)(B)","text":"evaluate the domestic and international leadership of Franklin D. Roosevelt and Harry Truman during World War II, including the U.S. relationship with its allies;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(7)(C)","text":"analyze major issues of World War II, including the Holocaust, the internment of Japanese Americans as a result of Executive Order 9066, and the development of atomic weapons;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(7)(D)","text":"analyze major military events of World War II, including fighting the war on multiple fronts, the Bataan Death March, the U.S. military advancement through the Pacific Islands, the Battle of Midway, the invasion of Normandy, and the liberation of concentration camps;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(7)(E)","text":"describe the military contributions of leaders during World War II, including Dwight Eisenhower, Douglas MacArthur, and Chester W. Nimitz;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(7)(F)","text":"explain issues affecting the home front, including volunteerism, the purchase of war bonds, and Victory Gardens and opportunities and obstacles for women and ethnic minorities; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(7)(G)","text":"explain how American patriotism inspired high levels of military enlistment and the bravery and contributions of the Tuskegee Airmen, the Flying Tigers, and the Navajo Code Talkers."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(8)","statement":"History. The student understands the impact of significant national and international decisions and conflicts in the Cold War on the United States. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(8)(A)","text":"describe U.S. responses to Soviet aggression after World War II, including the Truman Doctrine, the Marshall Plan, the Berlin Airlift, the North Atlantic Treaty Organization, and John F. Kennedy's role in the Cuban Missile Crisis;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(8)(B)","text":"describe how Cold War tensions were intensified by the House Un-American Activities Committee (HUAC), McCarthyism, the arms race, and the space race;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(8)(C)","text":"explain reasons and outcomes for U.S. involvement in the Korean War and its relationship to the containment policy;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(8)(D)","text":"explain reasons and outcomes for U.S. involvement in foreign countries and their relationship to the Domino Theory, including the Vietnam War;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(8)(E)","text":"analyze the major events of the Vietnam War, including the escalation of forces, the Tet Offensive, Vietnamization, and the fall of Saigon; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(8)(F)","text":"describe the responses to the Vietnam War, including the draft, the 26th Amendment, the role of the media, the credibility gap, the silent majority, and the anti-war movement."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(9)","statement":"History. The student understands the impact of the American civil rights movement. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(9)(A)","text":"trace the historical development of the civil rights movement from the late 1800s through the 21st century, including the 13th, 14th, 15th, and 19th amendments;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(9)(B)","text":"explain how Jim Crow laws and the Ku Klux Klan created obstacles to civil rights for minorities such as the suppression of voting;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(9)(C)","text":"describe the roles of political organizations that promoted African American, Chicano, American Indian, and women's civil rights;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(9)(D)","text":"identify the roles of significant leaders who supported various rights movements, including Martin Luther King Jr., Cesar Chavez, Dolores Huerta, Rosa Parks, and Betty Friedan;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(9)(E)","text":"compare and contrast the approach taken by the Black Panthers with the nonviolent approach of Martin Luther King Jr.;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(9)(F)","text":"discuss the impact of the writings of Martin Luther King Jr., including his \"I Have a Dream\" speech and \"Letter from Birmingham Jail\" on the civil rights movement;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(9)(G)","text":"describe presidential actions and congressional votes to address minority rights in the United States, including desegregation of the armed forces, the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and the Voting Rights Act of 1965;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(9)(H)","text":"explain how George Wallace, Orval Faubus, and the Congressional bloc of southern Democrats sought to maintain the status quo;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(9)(I)","text":"evaluate changes in the United States that have resulted from the civil rights movement, including increased participation of minorities in the political process; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(9)(J)","text":"describe how Sweatt v. Painter and Brown v. Board of Education played a role in protecting the rights of the minority during the civil rights movement."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(10)","statement":"History. The student understands the impact of political, economic, and social factors in the U.S. from the 1970s through 1990. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(10)(A)","text":"describe Richard M. Nixon's leadership in the normalization of relations with China and the policy of détente;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(10)(B)","text":"describe Ronald Reagan's leadership in domestic and international policies, including Reagan's economic policies and Peace Through Strength;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(10)(C)","text":"describe U.S. involvement in the Middle East such as support for Israel, the Camp David Accords, the Iran Hostage Crisis, Marines in Lebanon, and the Iran-Contra Affair;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(10)(D)","text":"describe the causes and key organizations of the conservative resurgence of the 1980s such as the Heritage Foundation and the Moral Majority; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(10)(E)","text":"describe significant societal issues of this time period such as the War on Drugs and the AIDS epidemic."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(11)","statement":"History. The student understands the emerging political, economic, and social issues of the United States from the 1990s into the 21st century. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(11)(A)","text":"describe U.S. involvement in world affairs, including the end of the Cold War, the Persian Gulf War, the events surrounding September 11, 2001, and the global War on Terror;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(11)(B)","text":"identify significant social and political issues such as health care, immigration, and education from different viewpoints across the political spectrum;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(11)(C)","text":"analyze the impact of third parties on the 1992 and 2000 presidential elections; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(11)(D)","text":"identify the impact of international events, multinational corporations, government policies, and individuals on the 21st century economy."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(12)","statement":"Geography. The student understands the impact of geographic factors on major events. The student is expected to analyze the impact of physical and human geographic factors on the Klondike Gold Rush, the Panama Canal, the Dust Bowl, and the levee failure in New Orleans after Hurricane Katrina.","expectations":[]},{"code":"113.41(d)(13)","statement":"Geography. The student understands the causes and effects of migration and immigration on American society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(13)(A)","text":"analyze the causes and effects of changing demographic patterns resulting from migration within the United States, including western expansion, rural to urban, the Great Migration, and the Rust Belt to the Sun Belt; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(13)(B)","text":"analyze the causes and effects of changing demographic patterns resulting from immigration to the United States."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(14)","statement":"Geography. The student understands the relationship between population growth and the physical environment. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(14)(A)","text":"identify the effects of population growth and distribution on the physical environment; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(14)(B)","text":"identify the roles of governmental entities and private citizens in managing the environment such as the establishment of the National Park System, the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), and the Endangered Species Act."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(15)","statement":"Economics. The student understands domestic and foreign issues related to U.S. economic growth from the 1870s to 1920. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(15)(A)","text":"describe how the economic impact of the Transcontinental Railroad and the Homestead Act contributed to the close of the frontier in the late 19th century;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(15)(B)","text":"describe the changing relationship between the federal government and private business, including the growth of free enterprise, costs and benefits of laissez-faire, Sherman Antitrust Act, Interstate Commerce Act, and Pure Food and Drug Act;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(15)(C)","text":"explain how foreign policies affected economic issues such as the Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882, the Open Door Policy, Dollar Diplomacy, and immigration quotas; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(15)(D)","text":"describe the economic effects of international military conflicts, including the Spanish-American War and World War I, on the United States."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(16)","statement":"Economics. The student understands significant economic developments between World War I and World War II. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(16)(A)","text":"analyze causes of economic growth and prosperity in the 1920s, including Warren Harding's Return to Normalcy, reduced taxes, and increased production efficiencies;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(16)(B)","text":"identify the causes of the Great Depression, including the impact of tariffs on world trade, stock market speculation, bank failures, and the monetary policy of the Federal Reserve System;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(16)(C)","text":"analyze the effects of the Great Depression on the U.S. economy and society such as widespread unemployment and deportation and repatriation of people of Mexican heritage;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(16)(D)","text":"compare the New Deal policies and its opponents' approaches to resolving the economic effects of the Great Depression; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(16)(E)","text":"describe how various New Deal agencies and programs, including the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Securities and Exchange Commission, and the Social Security Administration, continue to affect the lives of U.S. citizens."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(17)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the economic effects of government policies from World War II through the present. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(17)(A)","text":"describe the economic effects of World War II on the home front such as mobilization, the end of the Great Depression, rationing, and increased opportunity for women and minority employment;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(17)(B)","text":"identify the causes of prosperity in the 1950s, including the Baby Boom and the impact of the GI Bill (Servicemen's Readjustment Act of 1944), and the effects of prosperity in the 1950s such as increased consumption and the growth of agriculture and business;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(17)(C)","text":"describe the economic impact of defense spending on the business cycle and education priorities from 1945 to the 1990s;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(17)(D)","text":"identify the actions and outcomes of government policies intended to create economic opportunities for citizens such as the Great Society, affirmative action, and Title IX; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(17)(E)","text":"describe the dynamic relationship between U.S. international trade policies and the U.S. free enterprise system such as the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) oil embargo, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT), and the North American Free Trade Agreement (NAFTA)."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(18)","statement":"Government. The student understands changes over time in the role of government. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(18)(A)","text":"evaluate the impact of New Deal legislation on the historical roles of state and federal government;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(18)(B)","text":"explain constitutional issues raised by federal government policy changes during times of significant events, including World War I, the Great Depression, World War II, the 1960s, and September 11, 2001;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(18)(C)","text":"describe the effects of political scandals, including Teapot Dome, Watergate, and Bill Clinton's impeachment, on the views of U.S. citizens concerning trust in the federal government and its leaders; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(18)(D)","text":"describe the role of contemporary government legislation in the private and public sectors such as the Community Reinvestment Act of 1977, USA PATRIOT Act of 2001, and the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(19)","statement":"Government. The student understands the changing relationships among the three branches of the federal government. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(19)(A)","text":"describe the impact of events such as the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution and the War Powers Act on the relationship between the legislative and executive branches of government; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(19)(B)","text":"evaluate the impact of relationships among the legislative, executive, and judicial branches of government, including Franklin D. Roosevelt's attempt to increase the number of U.S. Supreme Court justices and the presidential election of 2000."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(20)","statement":"Government. The student understands the impact of constitutional issues on American society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(20)(A)","text":"analyze the effects of landmark U.S. Supreme Court decisions, including Plessy v. Ferguson, Brown v. Board of Education, Hernandez v. Texas, Tinker v. Des Moines, and Wisconsin v. Yoder; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(20)(B)","text":"explain why landmark constitutional amendments have been proposed and ratified from 1877 to the present."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(21)","statement":"Citizenship. The student understands the concept of American exceptionalism as identified by Alexis de Tocqueville. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(21)(A)","text":"discuss values crucial to America's success as a constitutional republic, including liberty, egalitarianism, individualism, populism, and laissez-faire; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(21)(B)","text":"describe how American values are different and unique from those of other nations."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(22)","statement":"Citizenship. The student understands the promises of the Declaration of Independence and the protections of the U.S. Constitution and the Bill of Rights. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(22)(A)","text":"identify and analyze methods of expanding the right to participate in the democratic process, including lobbying, non-violent protesting, litigation, and amendments to the U.S. Constitution;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(22)(B)","text":"evaluate various means of achieving equality of political rights, including the 19th, 24th, and 26th amendments and congressional acts such as the American Indian Citizenship Act of 1924;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(22)(C)","text":"explain how participation in the democratic process reflects our national identity, patriotism, and civic responsibility; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(22)(D)","text":"summarize the criteria and explain the process for becoming a naturalized citizen of the United States."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(23)","statement":"Citizenship. The student understands the importance of effective leadership in a constitutional republic. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(23)(A)","text":"evaluate the contributions of significant political and social leaders in the United States such as Andrew Carnegie, Thurgood Marshall, Billy Graham, Sandra Day O'Connor, and Hillary Clinton; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(23)(B)","text":"explain the importance of Congressional Medal of Honor recipients such as Army First Lieutenant Vernon J. Baker, Army Corporal Alvin York, and Army Master Sergeant Raul \"Roy\" Perez Benavidez."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(24)","statement":"Culture. The student understands the relationship between the arts and the times during which they were created. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(24)(A)","text":"describe how the characteristics of and issues in U.S. history have been reflected in various genres of art, music, film, and literature;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(24)(B)","text":"describe the impacts of cultural movements in art, music, and literature such as Tin Pan Alley, the Harlem Renaissance, the Beat Generation, rock and roll, the Chicano Mural Movement, and country and western music on American society; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(24)(C)","text":"identify and analyze the global diffusion of American culture through various media."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(25)","statement":"Culture. The student understands how people from various groups contribute to our national identity. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(25)(A)","text":"explain actions taken by people to expand economic opportunities and political rights for racial, ethnic, gender, and religious groups in American society;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(25)(B)","text":"describe the Americanization movement to assimilate immigrants and American Indians into American culture;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(25)(C)","text":"explain how the contributions of people of various racial, ethnic, gender, and religious groups shape American culture; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(25)(D)","text":"identify the contributions of women such as Rosa Parks, Eleanor Roosevelt, and Sonia Sotomayor to American society."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(26)","statement":"Science, technology, and society. The student understands the impact of science, technology, and the free enterprise system on the economic development of the United States. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(26)(A)","text":"explain the effects of scientific discoveries and technological innovations such as electric power, telephone and satellite communications, petroleum-based products, steel production, and computers on the economic development of the United States;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(26)(B)","text":"explain how specific needs result in scientific discoveries and technological innovations in agriculture, the military, and medicine; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(26)(C)","text":"describe the effect of technological innovations in the workplace such as assembly line manufacturing and robotics."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(27)","statement":"Science, technology, and society. The student understands the influence of scientific discoveries, technological innovations, and the free enterprise system on the standard of living in the United States. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(27)(A)","text":"analyze how scientific discoveries, technological innovations, space exploration, and the application of these by the free enterprise system improve the standard of living in the United States, including changes in transportation and communication; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(27)(B)","text":"describe how the free enterprise system drives technological innovation and its application in the marketplace such as cell phones, inexpensive personal computers, and global positioning products."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(28)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student understands how historians use historiography to interpret the past and applies critical-thinking skills to organize and use information acquired from a variety of valid sources, including technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(28)(A)","text":"analyze primary and secondary sources such as maps, graphs, speeches, political cartoons, and artifacts to acquire information to answer historical questions;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(28)(B)","text":"analyze information by applying absolute and relative chronology through sequencing, categorizing, identifying cause-and-effect relationships, comparing and contrasting, finding the main idea, summarizing, making generalizations, making predictions, drawing inferences, and drawing conclusions;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(28)(C)","text":"apply the process of historical inquiry to research, interpret, and use multiple types of sources of evidence;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(28)(D)","text":"evaluate a variety of historical and contemporary sources for validity, credibility, bias, and accuracy;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(28)(E)","text":"identify bias and support with historical evidence a point of view on a social studies issue or event; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(28)(F)","text":"formulate and communicate visually, orally, or in writing a claim supported by evidence and reasoning for an intended audience and purpose."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(29)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student communicates in written, oral, and visual forms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(29)(A)","text":"create written, oral, and visual presentations of social studies information using effective communication skills, including proper citations and avoiding plagiarism;"},{"code":"113.41(d)(29)(B)","text":"use social studies terminology correctly; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(29)(C)","text":"apply foundational language skills to engage in civil discourse about social studies topics, including those with multiple perspectives."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(30)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student uses geographic tools to collect, analyze, and interpret data. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(30)(A)","text":"create a visual representation of historical information such as thematic maps, graphs, and charts; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(30)(B)","text":"pose and answer questions about geographic distributions and patterns shown on maps, graphs, charts, and available databases."}]},{"code":"113.41(d)(31)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student uses problem-solving and decision-making skills, working independently and with others. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.41(d)(31)(A)","text":"explain governmental and democratic processes such as voting, due process, and caucuses using simulations and models; and"},{"code":"113.41(d)(31)(B)","text":"use problem-solving and decision-making processes to identify a problem, gather information, list and consider options, consider advantages and disadvantages, choose and implement a solution, and evaluate the effectiveness of the solution."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Foundations + Celebrate Freedom Week","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(1)","113.41(d)(2)","113.41(d)(21)","113.41(d)(22)","113.41(d)(23)","113.41(d)(28)","113.41(d)(29)"],"items":["Course intro: periodization and traditional historical reference points from 1877 to the present","Celebrate Freedom Week: origins/history of the Declaration of Independence and its founding principles","Text study: promises of the Declaration and protections of the U.S. Constitution and Bill of Rights","American exceptionalism as identified by Alexis de Tocqueville (equality, individualism, populism, laissez-faire)","Skill lab: historiography and evaluating primary vs. secondary sources (spiraled all year)","Writing bootcamp: thesis, evidence, and document-based short response (communicates in written form)","Traits of effective leadership in a constitutional republic — case-study intro"]},{"unit":"Gilded Age: Industry, Politics & Society 1877-1898","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(3)","113.41(d)(13)","113.41(d)(15)","113.41(d)(18)","113.41(d)(26)","113.41(d)(28)","113.41(d)(30)"],"items":["Rise of big business: Carnegie, Rockefeller, vertical/horizontal integration and the free enterprise system","Labor responds: unions, strikes (Homestead, Pullman) and government's changing role in the economy","Political corruption, patronage vs. civil service reform, and Gilded Age politics","Transcontinental railroad and technology driving economic development","New immigration (1870s-1890s): push/pull factors and nativist reaction","Geographic tools lab: reading economic and railroad maps to interpret industrial growth data","Historiography check: comparing conflicting accounts of the 'Robber Barons vs. Captains of Industry' debate"]},{"unit":"Westward Expansion, Immigration & Urban Growth","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(3)","113.41(d)(12)","113.41(d)(13)","113.41(d)(14)","113.41(d)(25)","113.41(d)(30)","113.41(d)(31)"],"items":["Settling the West: Homestead Act, farmers, ranchers, and the Populist movement","American Indian policy: reservations, Dawes Act, and Wounded Knee","Geographic case study: physical/human factors of the Klondike Gold Rush","Population growth and the physical environment — resource use and settlement patterns","Urbanization and how diverse groups (immigrants, migrants) contribute to national identity","Map-skills lab: interpreting migration/settlement data with geographic tools","Decision-making activity: working in groups to solve a Gilded Age urban-reform problem"]},{"unit":"Becoming a World Power 1898-1920","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(4)","113.41(d)(12)","113.41(d)(15)","113.41(d)(29)"],"items":["Imperialism and the Spanish-American War: causes, yellow journalism, and results","Geographic case study: physical/human factors and impact of the Panama Canal","U.S. as world power: Open Door Policy, Roosevelt Corollary, dollar diplomacy","Causes of U.S. entry into World War I and mobilization of the economy","The home front, over there, and Wilson's Fourteen Points","Visual/oral communication project: propaganda-poster analysis and presentation"]},{"unit":"Progressive Era & Reform Movements","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(5)","113.41(d)(18)","113.41(d)(19)","113.41(d)(20)","113.41(d)(23)","113.41(d)(31)"],"items":["Muckrakers, third parties, and the roots of Progressive reform","Effects of reform: 16th, 17th, 18th, and 19th Amendments (constitutional issues)","Women's suffrage and the leadership of reform figures (effective leadership in a republic)","Expanding role of government: regulation, trust-busting, and consumer protection","Changing relationships among the three branches (TR/Wilson expansion of executive power)","Problem-solving simulation: draft a Progressive reform policy and defend it in groups"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Benchmark (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["113.41(d)(2)","113.41(d)(28)","113.41(d)(29)"],"items":["Spiral review: 1877-1920 reference points and cause/effect chains","Timeline-building station rotation to reinforce periodization","Benchmark assessment mirroring STAAR EOC item formats","Data-driven reteach of missed standards from benchmark results","DBQ practice with written and oral defense of a claim"]},{"unit":"The Roaring Twenties","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(6)","113.41(d)(16)","113.41(d)(24)","113.41(d)(25)","113.41(d)(27)"],"items":["Postwar boom: consumer culture, credit, and economic developments of the 1920s","Cultural clashes: Red Scare, Prohibition, Scopes Trial, and immigration quotas","The Harlem Renaissance and the arts as a reflection of their times","Technology and rising standard of living: automobiles, radio, and electricity","Contributions of diverse groups to national identity in the Jazz Age","Social issues and key individuals of the decade (Lindbergh, Garvey, Ford)"]},{"unit":"Great Depression & New Deal","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(12)","113.41(d)(16)","113.41(d)(17)","113.41(d)(18)","113.41(d)(20)"],"items":["Causes of the Great Depression: speculation, bank failures, and the 1929 crash","Geographic case study: physical/human factors of the Dust Bowl and migration","Hoover vs. FDR: economic policy responses and the changing role of government","The New Deal: relief, recovery, reform programs and their economic effects","Constitutional clash: FDR's court-packing plan and Supreme Court challenges (constitutional issues)"]},{"unit":"World War II","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(7)","113.41(d)(17)","113.41(d)(25)","113.41(d)(29)"],"items":["Causes of WWII, U.S. neutrality, and the road to Pearl Harbor","Mobilizing the economy: rationing, war production, and government economic policy","The home front: women, Rosie the Riveter, and diverse groups (Tuskegee Airmen, Navajo Code Talkers, 442nd)","Japanese American internment and civil liberties in wartime","Major turning points in the European and Pacific theaters","The Holocaust, D-Day, and the decision to use the atomic bomb","Research-and-present project: a WWII individual or group's contribution (visual/oral communication)"]},{"unit":"Cold War & 1950s America","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(8)","113.41(d)(17)","113.41(d)(20)","113.41(d)(27)"],"items":["Origins of the Cold War: containment, Truman Doctrine, Marshall Plan, NATO","Hot spots: Korean War, Berlin, and the arms/space race","McCarthyism and Second Red Scare — constitutional issues and civil liberties","Postwar economy: GI Bill, suburbanization, and government policy effects","Scientific and technological advances raising the American standard of living"]},{"unit":"Civil Rights Movement","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(9)","113.41(d)(19)","113.41(d)(20)","113.41(d)(23)","113.41(d)(25)"],"items":["Roots and Brown v. Board of Education — constitutional issues and the courts","Strategies of the movement: Montgomery, sit-ins, Freedom Rides, March on Washington","Leadership case study: MLK Jr., nonviolence, and other civil rights leaders","Landmark legislation: Civil Rights Act of 1964 and Voting Rights Act of 1965","Three branches in action: legislative, executive, and judicial roles in civil rights","Expanding rights: women, Latinos (Chavez/UFW), and other groups' contributions to national identity"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["113.41(d)(28)","113.41(d)(31)"],"items":["Flex days to absorb calendar shifts, snow days, or assemblies","Targeted reteach of low-mastery standards from unit assessments","Historiography seminar: revisiting an interpretive debate with new sources","Group problem-solving/decision-making simulation on a modern civic issue","Cumulative quiz and error-analysis conferencing"]},{"unit":"The 1970s through 1990","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(10)","113.41(d)(24)","113.41(d)(26)"],"items":["Vietnam War, the antiwar movement, and the War Powers Act","Political turmoil: Watergate, the Nixon resignation, and declining trust in government","Economic challenges: stagflation, the oil crisis, and Reaganomics","The arts and popular culture as a reflection of the era","Science, technology, and free enterprise: the personal computer and the tech boom","End of the Cold War: Reagan, Gorbachev, and the fall of the Berlin Wall"]},{"unit":"1990s into the 21st Century","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(11)","113.41(d)(12)","113.41(d)(14)","113.41(d)(26)","113.41(d)(27)"],"items":["Post-Cold War world, globalization, NAFTA, and the tech-driven economy","September 11, 2001, the War on Terror, and homeland security","Geographic case study: levee failure in New Orleans after Hurricane Katrina","Population growth, immigration, and the physical environment in a modern U.S.","Science and technology (internet, biotech) reshaping the standard of living and free enterprise"]},{"unit":"STAAR EOC Review","weeks":3,"kind":"review","teks":["113.41(d)(28)","113.41(d)(29)","113.41(d)(30)","113.41(d)(31)"],"items":["Systematic strand-by-strand review of all tested reporting categories","Stimulus-based practice: political cartoons, maps, charts, and primary-source excerpts","Released STAAR EOC item drills with data-tracking and targeted small-group reteach","Timed full-length practice EOC and error analysis","Vocabulary and key-people/events review stations","Test-taking strategy and stamina building before the exam window"]},{"unit":"STAAR EOC Assessment Window","weeks":1,"kind":"assessment","teks":[],"items":["Administer the U.S. History STAAR EOC (April 19-30 window)","Non-testers: enrichment mini-documentary viewing and discussion","Reflection and self-assessment of learning goals","Light review support for makeup/retest students"]},{"unit":"Post-EOC: Capstone & Enrichment","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.41(d)(24)","113.41(d)(29)","113.41(d)(31)"],"items":["Capstone research project: connecting a historical theme to a current issue","Analyzing modern arts and media as a reflection of contemporary times","Structured academic debate and decision-making on a current civic question","Multimedia presentations (written, oral, and visual communication)","Semester reflection, portfolio review, and course wrap-up"]}]},{"course":"Economics with Emphasis on the Free Enterprise System and Its Benefits","tacSection":"19 TAC §113.31","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter113_sec.113.31","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-113"],"strands":[{"code":"113.31(d)(1)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the concepts of scarcity and opportunity costs. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(1)(A)","text":"explain why scarcity and choice are basic economic problems faced by every society;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(1)(B)","text":"describe how societies answer the basic economic questions: what to produce, how to produce, and for whom to produce;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(1)(C)","text":"describe the economic factors of production: land, labor, capital, and entrepreneurship; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(1)(D)","text":"interpret a production-possibilities curve and apply the concepts of opportunity costs and scarcity."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(2)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the interaction of supply, demand, and price. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(2)(A)","text":"understand the effect of changes in price on the quantity demanded and quantity supplied;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(2)(B)","text":"identify the non-price determinants that create changes in supply and demand, which result in a new equilibrium price; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(2)(C)","text":"interpret a supply-and-demand graph using supply-and-demand schedules."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(3)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the reasons for international trade and its importance to the United States and the global economy. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(3)(A)","text":"apply the concepts of absolute and comparative advantages;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(3)(B)","text":"compare the effects of free trade and trade barriers on economic activities, including the benefits and costs of participating in international trade; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(3)(C)","text":"analyze the effects of changes in exchange rates on imports and exports."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(4)","statement":"Economics. The student understands free enterprise, socialist, and communist economic systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(4)(A)","text":"explain the basic characteristics of economic systems, including property rights, incentives, economic freedom, competition, and the role of government;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(4)(B)","text":"contrast current and historic examples of the free enterprise system, socialism, and communism using the basic characteristics of economic systems; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(4)(C)","text":"analyze the contributions of various economic philosophers, including Friedrich Hayek, Milton Friedman, John Maynard Keynes, and Adam Smith, and their impact on the U.S. free enterprise system."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(5)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the basic characteristics and benefits of the U.S. free enterprise system. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(5)(A)","text":"explain the benefits of the U.S. free enterprise system, including individual freedom of consumers and producers, variety of goods, responsive prices, investment opportunities, and the creation of wealth; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(5)(B)","text":"analyze recent changes in the basic characteristics, including private property, incentives, economic freedom, competition, and the limited role of government, of the U.S. economy."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(6)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the right to own, use, and dispose of private property. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(6)(A)","text":"analyze the costs and benefits of the purchase, use, or disposal of personal and business property; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(6)(B)","text":"identify and evaluate examples of restrictions that the government places on the use of business and individual property."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(7)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the circular-flow model of the economy. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(7)(A)","text":"interpret the roles of resource owners and firms in a circular-flow model of the economy and provide real-world examples to illustrate elements of the model; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(7)(B)","text":"explain how government actions affect the circular-flow model."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(8)","statement":"Economics. The student understands types of market structures. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(8)(A)","text":"describe characteristics and give examples of pure competition, monopolistic competition, oligopoly, and monopoly; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(8)(B)","text":"identify regulations that apply to the establishment and operation of various types of market structures."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(9)","statement":"Economics. The student understands key economic measurements. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(9)(A)","text":"interpret economic data, including unemployment rate, gross domestic product, gross domestic product per capita as a measure of national wealth, and rate of inflation; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(9)(B)","text":"analyze business cycles using key economic indicators."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(10)","statement":"Economics. The student understands key components of economic growth. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(10)(A)","text":"analyze how productivity relates to growth;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(10)(B)","text":"analyze how technology relates to growth; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(10)(C)","text":"analyze how trade relates to growth."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(11)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the role of money in an economy. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(11)(A)","text":"describe the functions of money;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(11)(B)","text":"describe the characteristics of money, including commodity money, fiat money, and representative money; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(11)(C)","text":"analyze the positive and negative aspects of barter, currency, and debit cards."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(12)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the role of the Federal Reserve System in establishing monetary policy. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(12)(A)","text":"explain the structure of the Federal Reserve System;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(12)(B)","text":"analyze the three basic tools used to implement U.S. monetary policy, including reserve requirements, the discount rate and the federal funds rate target, and open-market operations;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(12)(C)","text":"explain how the actions of the Federal Reserve System affect the nation's money supply; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(12)(D)","text":"describe the current role of the U.S. dollar in trade in the world market and analyze how that has changed over time, in particular since departing from the gold standard in 1971."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(13)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the role that the government plays in the U.S. free enterprise system. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(13)(A)","text":"describe the role of government in the U.S. free enterprise system and the changes in that role over time; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(13)(B)","text":"analyze the costs and benefits of U.S. economic policies, rules, and regulations related to the economic goals of economic growth, stability, full employment, freedom, security, equity (equal opportunity versus equal outcome), and efficiency."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(14)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the economic impact of fiscal policy decisions at the local, state, and national levels. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(14)(A)","text":"identify types of taxes at the local, state, and national levels and the economic importance of each;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(14)(B)","text":"explain the categories of revenues and expenditures in the U.S. federal budget; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(14)(C)","text":"analyze the impact of fiscal policy decisions on the economy."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(15)","statement":"Personal financial literacy. The student understands types of business ownership. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(15)(A)","text":"explain the characteristics of sole proprietorships, partnerships, and corporations; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(15)(B)","text":"analyze the advantages and disadvantages of sole proprietorships, partnerships, and corporations."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(16)","statement":"Personal financial literacy. The student understands the role of financial markets/institutions in saving, borrowing, and capital formation. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(16)(A)","text":"explain the functions of financial institutions and how they affect households and businesses;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(16)(B)","text":"explain how the amount of savings in an economy is the basis of capital formation;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(16)(C)","text":"analyze the role of interest and risk in allocating savings to its most productive use; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(16)(D)","text":"examine the types of accounts available to consumers from financial institutions and the risks, monetary costs, and benefits of maintaining these accounts."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(17)","statement":"Personal financial literacy. The student understands the role of individuals in financial markets. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(17)(A)","text":"assess ways to be a wise investor in the stock market and in other personal investment options such as developing a personal retirement plan;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(17)(B)","text":"explain how to begin a savings program;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(17)(C)","text":"demonstrate how to maintain a checking account, including reconciling a bank statement;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(17)(D)","text":"identify the types of loans available to consumers;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(17)(E)","text":"explain the responsibilities and obligations of borrowing money; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(17)(F)","text":"develop strategies to become a low-risk borrower by improving and understanding one's personal credit score."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(18)","statement":"Personal financial literacy. The student applies critical-thinking skills to analyze the costs and benefits of personal financial decisions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(18)(A)","text":"examine ways to avoid and eliminate credit card debt;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(18)(B)","text":"evaluate the costs and benefits of declaring personal bankruptcy;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(18)(C)","text":"evaluate the costs and benefits of buying insurance; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(18)(D)","text":"evaluate the costs and benefits of charitable giving."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(19)","statement":"Personal financial literacy. The student understands how to provide for basic needs while living within a budget. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(19)(A)","text":"evaluate the costs and benefits of renting a home versus buying a home; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(19)(B)","text":"assess the financial aspects of making the transition from renting to home ownership."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(20)","statement":"Personal financial literacy. The student understands the various methods available to pay for college and other postsecondary education and training. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(20)(A)","text":"understand how to complete the Free Application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA) provided by the United States Department of Education;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(20)(B)","text":"research and evaluate various scholarship opportunities such as those from state governments, schools, employers, individuals, private companies, nonprofits, and professional organizations;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(20)(C)","text":"analyze and compare student grant options;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(20)(D)","text":"analyze and compare student loan options, including private and federal loans; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(20)(E)","text":"research and evaluate various work-study program opportunities."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(21)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student applies critical-thinking skills to organize and use information acquired through established research methodologies from a variety of valid sources, including technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(21)(A)","text":"analyze economic information by sequencing, categorizing, identifying cause-and-effect relationships, comparing, contrasting, finding the main idea, summarizing, making generalizations and predictions, and drawing inferences and conclusions;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(21)(B)","text":"create economic models, including production-possibilities curves, circular-flow charts, and supply-and-demand graphs, to analyze economic concepts or issues;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(21)(C)","text":"explain a point of view on an economic issue;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(21)(D)","text":"analyze and evaluate a variety of economic information from primary and secondary sources for validity, credibility, accuracy, bias, propaganda, point of view, and frame of reference;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(21)(E)","text":"evaluate economic data using charts, tables, graphs, and maps; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(21)(F)","text":"formulate and communicate visually, orally, or in writing a claim supported by evidence and reasoning for an intended audience and purpose."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(22)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student communicates in written, oral, and visual forms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.31(d)(22)(A)","text":"use social studies terminology correctly;"},{"code":"113.31(d)(22)(B)","text":"create written, oral, and visual presentations of economic information using effective communication skills, including proper citations and avoiding plagiarism; and"},{"code":"113.31(d)(22)(C)","text":"apply foundational language skills to engage in civil discourse about economics topics, including those with multiple perspectives."}]},{"code":"113.31(d)(23)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student uses problem-solving and decision-making skills, working independently and with others. The student is expected to use problem-solving and decision-making processes to identify a problem, gather information, list and consider options, consider advantages and disadvantages, choose and implement a solution, and evaluate the effectiveness of the solution.","expectations":[]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Course Launch: Scarcity & Opportunity Cost","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(1)","113.31(d)(21)","113.31(d)(22)","113.31(d)(23)"],"items":["The economic problem: scarcity, needs vs. wants, and why choices are unavoidable","Factors of production: land, labor, capital, entrepreneurship","Opportunity cost and trade-offs (guns vs. butter)","Production Possibilities Curve (PPC): efficiency, growth, and cost","Marginal thinking and cost-benefit analysis","Introduce the PACED decision-making process and apply it to a real personal choice","Economics research toolkit: evaluating valid sources and reading data (BLS/BEA intro)"]},{"unit":"Comparing Economic Systems","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(4)"],"items":["The three basic economic questions: what, how, and for whom to produce","Traditional, command, market, and mixed economies","Free enterprise/capitalism vs. socialism vs. communism","Case studies: United States, former Soviet Union, China, Cuba, Sweden","Economic-freedom continuum and the role of incentives","Structured debate: which system best meets human needs?"]},{"unit":"U.S. Free Enterprise & Private Property","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(5)","113.31(d)(6)"],"items":["Characteristics of U.S. free enterprise: private property, competition, profit motive, consumer sovereignty, limited government","Benefits of the free enterprise system for consumers, producers, and society","Entrepreneurship, innovation, and voluntary exchange","Private property rights: the right to own, use, and dispose of property","Intellectual property, patents, and copyrights as incentives","How secure property rights drive investment and economic growth"]},{"unit":"Supply, Demand & Price","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(2)","113.31(d)(22)"],"items":["Law of demand: the demand curve, change in quantity demanded vs. shifts","Determinants of demand (income, tastes, substitutes, complements, expectations)","Law of supply: the supply curve and determinants of supply","Market equilibrium: how price coordinates buyers and sellers","Shortages, surpluses, and price as a signal","Price ceilings and price floors (rent control, minimum wage)","Elasticity of demand and total revenue (introduction)","Supply-and-demand graphing lab: build, label, and explain graphs (visual communication)"]},{"unit":"Market Structures","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(8)"],"items":["Perfect competition and the price-taker firm","Monopoly: barriers to entry and market power","Monopolistic competition and product differentiation","Oligopoly, interdependence, and collusion","Antitrust law and protecting competition"]},{"unit":"Circular Flow & Business Organization","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(7)","113.31(d)(15)"],"items":["Circular-flow model: households and firms, product and factor markets","Adding government, the financial sector, and the rest of the world","Sole proprietorships: advantages and unlimited liability","Partnerships and how they share risk and profit","Corporations, stock, and limited liability","LLCs, cooperatives, and franchises","Choose-a-business-structure decision project"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["113.31(d)(21)","113.31(d)(23)"],"items":["Reteach sticky concepts (PPC, supply/demand shifts) using real data","Unit reassessment and guided test corrections","Absorb any calendar slippage from Labor Day / early-fall holidays","Research-skills mini-lesson: citing and evaluating economic sources","Enrichment: current-events economics stations"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Exam","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["113.31(d)(1)","113.31(d)(2)","113.31(d)(4)","113.31(d)(5)","113.31(d)(6)","113.31(d)(7)","113.31(d)(8)","113.31(d)(15)"],"items":["Cumulative review stations: microeconomics foundations","Semester-exam review game and study guide","Semester exam","Exam error analysis and reflection","Spring-semester goal setting"]},{"unit":"Measuring the Economy","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(9)","113.31(d)(21)"],"items":["Gross Domestic Product (GDP): expenditure components (C + I + G + NX)","Real vs. nominal GDP and using a price index","Unemployment: labor force, the unemployment rate, and its types","Inflation, the Consumer Price Index (CPI), and purchasing power","The business cycle: expansion, peak, recession, trough","Research project: track current GDP, inflation, and unemployment from BLS/BEA data"]},{"unit":"Economic Growth","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(10)"],"items":["Productivity as the engine of economic growth","Physical capital, technology, and investment","Human capital, education, and specialization","Standard of living and per-capita real GDP","Trade-offs of growth: sustainability and distribution"]},{"unit":"Money & Banking","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(11)"],"items":["Functions of money: medium of exchange, store of value, unit of account","Characteristics of money and its history (barter to commodity to fiat)","Types of money and the money supply (M1 and M2)","How banks work: deposits, loans, and interest","Fractional-reserve banking and money creation","Financial technology and the future of money (digital payments)"]},{"unit":"The Federal Reserve & Monetary Policy","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(12)"],"items":["Structure of the Federal Reserve System: Board of Governors, districts, FOMC","The Fed's dual mandate: stable prices and maximum employment","Tools of monetary policy: open-market operations, discount rate, reserve requirement / interest on reserves","Expansionary vs. contractionary monetary policy","How the Fed influences interest rates and the broader economy","Simulation: you are the FOMC — set policy for an inflation/recession scenario"]},{"unit":"Government & Fiscal Policy","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(13)","113.31(d)(14)"],"items":["Role of government in free enterprise: public goods, externalities, and regulation","Protecting consumers, competition, and property rights","Types of taxes: progressive, regressive, proportional (income, sales, property)","Government spending and the federal budget","Fiscal policy tools: taxing and spending to stabilize the economy","Budget deficits, surpluses, and the national debt","Fiscal decisions at local, state, and national levels (school bonds, state budgets)"]},{"unit":"International Trade","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(3)","113.31(d)(23)"],"items":["Why nations trade: absolute and comparative advantage","Imports, exports, and the balance of trade","Exchange rates and how currency values affect trade","Trade barriers: tariffs, quotas, and embargoes","Trade agreements and globalization (USMCA, WTO)","Trade-policy debate applying the decision-making process"]},{"unit":"Financial Markets & Institutions","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(16)","113.31(d)(17)"],"items":["Saving vs. investing and how financial markets drive capital formation","Financial institutions: banks, credit unions, and brokerages","Stocks, bonds, and mutual funds","Risk, return, and diversification","The individual as saver, investor, and borrower","Compound interest and the time value of money (calculations)"]},{"unit":"Smart Financial Decisions & Budgeting","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(18)","113.31(d)(19)","113.31(d)(22)"],"items":["Cost-benefit analysis of personal financial decisions","Credit, credit scores, and managing debt responsibly","Needs vs. wants and living within a budget","Building a monthly budget: income, fixed and variable expenses, savings","Insurance and protecting against financial risk","Gross vs. net pay, deductions, and reading a paystub","Personal budget project: written plan plus oral/visual presentation"]},{"unit":"Paying for College & Postsecondary Paths","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.31(d)(20)","113.31(d)(21)","113.31(d)(23)"],"items":["Costs of college, trade school, and career training","Grants, scholarships, and work-study","FAFSA and the financial-aid process (guided research)","Student loans: subsidized vs. unsubsidized and repayment realities","Compare postsecondary paths using the full decision-making process (identify, gather, weigh, choose, evaluate)"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review, Capstone & Final","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["113.31(d)(3)","113.31(d)(9)","113.31(d)(10)","113.31(d)(11)","113.31(d)(12)","113.31(d)(13)","113.31(d)(14)","113.31(d)(16)","113.31(d)(17)","113.31(d)(18)","113.31(d)(19)","113.31(d)(20)","113.31(d)(22)"],"items":["Cumulative review: macroeconomics and personal finance","Personal finance capstone project (budget + investing + postsecondary plan)","Final-exam review stations and study guide","Final exam","Capstone presentations (oral/visual communication)","Course reflection and economic-citizenship wrap-up"]}]},{"course":"United States Government","tacSection":"19 TAC §113.44","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter113_sec.113.44","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-113"],"strands":[{"code":"113.44(d)(1)","statement":"History. The student understands how constitutional government, as developed in America and expressed in the Declaration of Independence, the Articles of Confederation, and the U.S. Constitution, has been influenced by ideas, people, and historical documents. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(1)(A)","text":"explain major political ideas in history, including the laws of nature and nature's God, unalienable rights, divine right of kings, social contract theory, and the rights of resistance to illegitimate government;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(1)(B)","text":"identify major intellectual, philosophical, political, and religious traditions that informed the American founding, including Judeo-Christian (especially biblical law), English common law and constitutionalism, Enlightenment, and republicanism, as they address issues of liberty, rights, and responsibilities of individuals;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(1)(C)","text":"identify the individuals whose principles of laws and government institutions informed the American founding documents, including those of Moses, William Blackstone, John Locke, and Charles de Montesquieu;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(1)(D)","text":"identify the contributions of the political philosophies of the Founding Fathers, including John Adams, Alexander Hamilton, Thomas Jefferson, James Madison, John Jay, George Mason, Roger Sherman, and James Wilson, on the development of the U.S. government;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(1)(E)","text":"analyze debates and compromises that impacted the creation of the founding documents; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(1)(F)","text":"identify significant individuals in the field of government and politics, including George Washington, Thomas Jefferson, John Marshall, Andrew Jackson, Abraham Lincoln, Theodore Roosevelt, Franklin D. Roosevelt, and Ronald Reagan."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(2)","statement":"History. The student understands the roles played by individuals, political parties, interest groups, and the media in the U.S. political system, past and present. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(2)(A)","text":"describe the processes used by individuals, political parties, interest groups, or the media to affect public policy; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(2)(B)","text":"analyze the impact of political changes brought about by individuals, political parties, interest groups, or the media, past and present."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(3)","statement":"Geography. The student understands how geography can influence U.S. political districts and policies. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(3)(A)","text":"explain how population shifts affect voting patterns;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(3)(B)","text":"examine political boundaries to make inferences regarding the distribution of political power; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(3)(C)","text":"explain how political districts are crafted and how they are affected by Supreme Court decisions such as Baker v. Carr."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(4)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the roles played by local, state, and national governments in both the public and private sectors of the U.S. free enterprise system. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(4)(A)","text":"explain how government fiscal, and regulatory policies influence the economy at the local, state, and national levels;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(4)(B)","text":"compare the role of government in the U.S. free enterprise system and other economic systems; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(4)(C)","text":"explain how government taxation, expenditures, and regulation can influence the U.S. economy and impact private enterprise."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(5)","statement":"Economics. The student understands the relationship between U.S. government policies and the economy. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(5)(A)","text":"analyze how economic and natural resources influence U.S. foreign policy; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(5)(B)","text":"describe the roles of the executive and legislative branches in setting international trade and fiscal policies."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(6)","statement":"Government. The student understands the American beliefs and principles reflected in the U.S. Constitution and why these are significant. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(6)(A)","text":"explain the importance of a written constitution;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(6)(B)","text":"explain how the federal government serves the purposes set forth in the Preamble to the U.S. Constitution;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(6)(C)","text":"analyze how the Federalist Papers such as Number 10 and Number 51 explain the principles of the American constitutional system of government;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(6)(D)","text":"evaluate constitutional provisions for limiting the role of government, including republicanism, checks and balances, federalism, separation of powers, popular sovereignty, and individual rights;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(6)(E)","text":"describe the constitutionally prescribed procedures by which the U.S. Constitution can be changed and analyze the role of the amendment process in a constitutional government; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(6)(F)","text":"identify how the Declaration of Independence and the U.S. Constitution continue to shape American beliefs and principles in the United States today."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(7)","statement":"Government. The student understands the structure and functions of the government created by the U.S. Constitution. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(7)(A)","text":"analyze the structure and functions of the legislative branch of government, including the bicameral structure of Congress, the role of committees, and the procedure for enacting laws;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(7)(B)","text":"analyze the structure and functions of the executive branch of government, including the constitutional powers of the president, the growth of presidential power, and the role of the Cabinet and executive departments;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(7)(C)","text":"analyze the structure and functions of the judicial branch of government, including the federal court system, types of jurisdiction, and judicial review;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(7)(D)","text":"identify the purpose of selected independent executive agencies, including the National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA), and regulatory commissions, including the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA), Food and Drug Administration (FDA), and Federal Communications Commission (FCC);"},{"code":"113.44(d)(7)(E)","text":"explain how provisions of the U.S. Constitution provide for checks and balances among the three branches of government;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(7)(F)","text":"analyze selected issues raised by judicial activism and judicial restraint;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(7)(G)","text":"explain the major responsibilities of the federal government for domestic and foreign policy such as national defense; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(7)(H)","text":"compare the structures, functions, and processes of national, state, and local governments in the U.S. federal system."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(8)","statement":"Government. The student understands the concept of federalism. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(8)(A)","text":"explain why the Founding Fathers created a distinctly new form of federalism and adopted a federal system of government instead of a unitary system;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(8)(B)","text":"categorize government powers as national, state, or shared;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(8)(C)","text":"analyze historical and contemporary conflicts over the respective roles of national and state governments; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(8)(D)","text":"explain how the U.S. Constitution limits the power of national and state governments."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(9)","statement":"Government. The student understands the processes for filling public offices in the U.S. system of government. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(9)(A)","text":"identify different methods of filling public offices, including elected and appointed offices at the local, state, and national levels;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(9)(B)","text":"explain the process of electing the president of the United States and analyze the Electoral College; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(9)(C)","text":"analyze the impact of the passage of the 17th Amendment."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(10)","statement":"Government. The student understands the role of political parties in the U.S. system of government. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(10)(A)","text":"analyze the functions of political parties and their role in the electoral process at local, state, and national levels; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(10)(B)","text":"explain the two-party system and evaluate the role of third parties in the United States."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(11)","statement":"Government. The student understands the similarities and differences that exist among the U.S. system of government and other political systems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(11)(A)","text":"compare the U.S. constitutional republic to historical and contemporary forms of government such as monarchy, a classical republic, authoritarian, socialist, direct democracy, theocracy, tribal, and other republics; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(11)(B)","text":"analyze advantages and disadvantages of presidential and parliamentary systems of government."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(12)","statement":"Citizenship. The student understands the rights that are protected and secured by the U.S. Constitution and Bill of Rights. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(12)(A)","text":"explain the roles of limited government and the rule of law in the protection of individual rights;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(12)(B)","text":"identify and define the unalienable rights;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(12)(C)","text":"identify the freedoms and rights protected and secured by each amendment in the Bill of Rights;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(12)(D)","text":"analyze the reasons the Founding Fathers protected religious freedom in America and guaranteed its free exercise by saying that \"Congress shall make no law respecting an establishment of religion, or prohibiting the free exercise thereof,\" and compare this to the concept of separation of church and state;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(12)(E)","text":"analyze U.S. Supreme Court interpretations of rights guaranteed by the U.S. Constitution in selected cases, including Engel v. Vitale, Schenck v. United States, Texas v. Johnson, Miranda v. Arizona, Gideon v. Wainwright, Mapp v. Ohio, and Roe v. Wade;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(12)(F)","text":"explain the importance of due process rights to the protection of individual rights and in limiting the powers of government; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(12)(G)","text":"recall the conditions that produced the 14th Amendment and describe subsequent efforts to selectively extend some of the Bill of Rights to the states through U.S. Supreme Court rulings and analyze the impact on the scope of fundamental rights and federalism."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(13)","statement":"Citizenship. The student understands the difference between personal and civic responsibilities. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(13)(A)","text":"describe scenarios where good citizenship may require the subordination of personal desire for the sake of the public good;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(13)(B)","text":"explain the responsibilities, duties, and obligations of citizenship such as being well informed about civic affairs, serving in the military, voting, serving on a jury, observing the laws, paying taxes, and serving the public good; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(13)(C)","text":"describe the voter registration process and the criteria for voting in elections."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(14)","statement":"Citizenship. The student understands the importance of voluntary individual participation in the U.S. constitutional republic. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(14)(A)","text":"analyze the effectiveness of various methods of participation in the political process at local, state, and national levels;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(14)(B)","text":"analyze historical and contemporary examples of citizen movements to bring about political change or to maintain continuity; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(14)(C)","text":"describe the factors that influence an individual's political attitudes and actions."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(15)","statement":"Citizenship. The student understands the importance of the expression of different points of view in a constitutional republic. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(15)(A)","text":"analyze different points of view of political parties and interest groups such as the League of United Latin American Citizens (LULAC), the National Rifle Association (NRA), and the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) on important contemporary issues; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(15)(B)","text":"analyze the importance of the First Amendment rights of petition, assembly, speech, and press and the Second Amendment right to keep and bear arms."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(16)","statement":"Culture. The student understands the relationship between government policies and the culture of the United States. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(16)(A)","text":"evaluate a U.S. government policy or court decision that has affected a particular racial, ethnic, or religious group, including the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the U.S. Supreme Court cases of Hernandez v. Texas and Grutter v. Bollinger; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(16)(B)","text":"explain changes in American culture brought about by government policies such as voting rights, the Servicemen's Readjustment Act of 1944 (GI Bill of Rights), the Immigration and Nationality Act of 1965, the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986, affirmative action, and racial integration."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(17)","statement":"Science, technology, and society. The student understands the role the government plays in developing policies and establishing conditions that influence scientific discoveries and technological innovations. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(17)(A)","text":"explain how U.S. constitutional protections such as patents have fostered competition and entrepreneurship; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(17)(B)","text":"identify examples of government-assisted research that, when shared with the private sector, have resulted in improved consumer products such as computer and communication technologies."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(18)","statement":"Science, technology, and society. The student understands the impact of advances in science and technology on government. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(18)(A)","text":"describe the potential impact of recent scientific discoveries and technological innovations on government policy; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(18)(B)","text":"evaluate the impact of the Internet and other electronic information on the political process."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(19)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student applies critical-thinking skills to organize and use information acquired through established research methodologies from a variety of valid sources, including technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(19)(A)","text":"analyze information by sequencing, categorizing, identifying cause-and-effect relationships, comparing, contrasting, finding the main idea, summarizing, making generalizations and predictions, and drawing inferences and conclusions;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(19)(B)","text":"create a product on a contemporary government issue or topic using critical methods of inquiry;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(19)(C)","text":"analyze and defend a point of view on a current political issue;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(19)(D)","text":"analyze and evaluate a variety of historical and contemporary sources for validity of information, arguments, counterarguments, credibility, accuracy, bias, propaganda, point of view, and frame of reference;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(19)(E)","text":"evaluate government data using charts, tables, graphs, and maps; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(19)(F)","text":"formulate and communicate visually, orally, or in writing a claim supported by evidence and reasoning for an intended audience and purpose."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(20)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student communicates in written, oral, and visual forms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(20)(A)","text":"use social studies terminology correctly;"},{"code":"113.44(d)(20)(B)","text":"create written, oral, and visual presentations of social studies information using effective communication skills, including proper citations and avoiding plagiarism; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(20)(C)","text":"apply foundational language skills to engage in civil discourse about social studies topics, including those with multiple perspectives."}]},{"code":"113.44(d)(21)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student uses problem-solving and decision-making skills, working independently and with others. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.44(d)(21)(A)","text":"explain governmental and democratic processes such as voting, due process, and caucuses using simulations and models; and"},{"code":"113.44(d)(21)(B)","text":"use problem-solving and decision-making processes to identify a problem, gather information, list and consider options, consider advantages and disadvantages, choose and implement a solution, and evaluate the effectiveness of the solution."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Foundations & Purposes of Government","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(1)","113.44(d)(6)","113.44(d)(11)","113.44(d)(19)","113.44(d)(20)"],"items":["Purposes and functions of government: order, security, public services, common good","Forms of government: democracy, republic, monarchy, oligarchy, autocracy, theocracy","Enlightenment thinkers: Hobbes, Locke, Montesquieu, Rousseau","Social contract, natural rights, and consent of the governed","Direct vs. representative democracy; unitary, confederal, and federal systems (intro)","Skill: annotating and analyzing founding-era primary sources"]},{"unit":"Roots of American Government","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(1)","113.44(d)(6)","113.44(d)(19)","113.44(d)(20)"],"items":["English heritage: Magna Carta, English Bill of Rights, common law, limited monarchy","Colonial self-government: Mayflower Compact, town meetings, colonial legislatures","Declaration of Independence: grievances, Locke's influence, unalienable rights","Articles of Confederation: structure, strengths, and weaknesses","Shays' Rebellion and the case for a stronger national government","Skill: comparing multiple documents to trace how an idea developed"]},{"unit":"Drafting & Ratifying the Constitution","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(1)","113.44(d)(6)","113.44(d)(15)","113.44(d)(20)"],"items":["Constitutional Convention: delegates, secrecy, Virginia vs. New Jersey plans","The Great (Connecticut) Compromise and the Three-Fifths Compromise","Six principles: popular sovereignty, limited government, separation of powers, checks and balances, judicial review, federalism","Federalists vs. Anti-Federalists and The Federalist Papers","Ratification debate and the promise of a Bill of Rights","Skill: structured academic debate presenting opposing viewpoints"]},{"unit":"Federalism & the Division of Power","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(6)","113.44(d)(8)","113.44(d)(19)"],"items":["Delegated, reserved, concurrent, and denied powers","Supremacy clause and McCulloch v. Maryland","Commerce clause and the growth of national power","Dual vs. cooperative federalism; grants-in-aid and mandates","Roles of state and local governments","Article V: the constitutional amendment process"]},{"unit":"The Legislative Branch","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(7)","113.44(d)(3)","113.44(d)(20)"],"items":["Bicameral structure: House vs. Senate comparison","Enumerated and implied powers; the necessary and proper clause","How a bill becomes a law","Apportionment, congressional districts, and redistricting","Gerrymandering: how geography shapes representation and policy","Committees, leadership, and congressional oversight","Skill: mapping and evaluating a district plan"]},{"unit":"The Executive Branch","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(7)","113.44(d)(20)"],"items":["Roles and powers of the President (chief executive, commander-in-chief, diplomat)","Qualifications, terms, and succession (25th Amendment)","The Cabinet and executive departments","The federal bureaucracy and independent/regulatory agencies","Executive checks: veto, appointments, treaties, executive orders","Skill: writing a persuasive briefing memo"]},{"unit":"The Judicial Branch","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(7)","113.44(d)(19)","113.44(d)(20)"],"items":["Structure of the federal courts: district, appellate, Supreme","Original vs. appellate jurisdiction","Judicial review and Marbury v. Madison","How the Supreme Court selects and decides cases","Selection and confirmation of federal judges","Judicial activism vs. restraint; analyzing a landmark case"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Exam (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["113.44(d)(6)","113.44(d)(7)","113.44(d)(8)","113.44(d)(19)"],"items":["Cumulative review: foundations through the three branches","Primary-source and Supreme Court case-analysis practice","Semester exam","Error analysis and targeted reteach","Timed constitutional-principles essay"]},{"unit":"Civil Liberties & the Bill of Rights","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(12)","113.44(d)(15)","113.44(d)(20)"],"items":["First Amendment freedoms: speech, religion, press, assembly, petition","Landmark cases: Tinker v. Des Moines, Engel v. Vitale, Schenck v. United States","Rights of the accused: 4th-8th Amendments","Due process and selective incorporation (14th Amendment)","Balancing individual liberty against public order and security","Skill: defending a dissenting viewpoint respectfully in writing and discussion"]},{"unit":"Civil Rights & Equality","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(12)","113.44(d)(13)","113.44(d)(16)"],"items":["Equal protection clause and levels of judicial scrutiny","Brown v. Board of Education and the civil rights movement","Expansion of voting rights: 15th, 19th, 24th, and 26th Amendments","Contemporary equality debates (affirmative action, disability, LGBTQ rights)","How government policy both shapes and reflects American culture","Personal responsibility in defending the rights of others"]},{"unit":"Political Parties, Interest Groups & Media","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(2)","113.44(d)(10)","113.44(d)(15)"],"items":["Functions of political parties; the two-party system and third parties","Party platforms, realignment, and polarization","Interest groups, lobbying, and PACs/Super PACs","The media's role: agenda-setting, watchdog, and bias","Public opinion, polling, and propaganda-technique analysis","Skill: evaluating source credibility and detecting bias"]},{"unit":"Voting, Elections & Campaigns","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(9)","113.44(d)(14)","113.44(d)(2)"],"items":["Nomination process: primaries, caucuses, and conventions","The general election and the Electoral College","Campaign finance and the role of money in elections","Voter qualifications, registration, and turnout","Why voluntary participation sustains the constitutional republic","Mock election / campaign simulation"]},{"unit":"Citizenship, Rights & Responsibilities","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(13)","113.44(d)(14)","113.44(d)(15)","113.44(d)(21)"],"items":["Paths to citizenship and the naturalization process","Personal responsibilities vs. civic responsibilities","Civic duties: voting, jury duty, taxes, selective service","Volunteerism and community engagement","Group civic-action project: identify a problem and propose a solution"]},{"unit":"Government & the Economy","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(4)","113.44(d)(5)","113.44(d)(16)"],"items":["Free enterprise and the role of government in the economy","Public vs. private sector; local, state, and national economic roles","Fiscal policy: taxing, spending, and the federal budget","Monetary policy and the Federal Reserve (basics)","How economic policy affects citizens and shapes culture","Skill: interpreting economic data and budget graphics"]},{"unit":"Science, Technology & Government","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(17)","113.44(d)(18)","113.44(d)(19)"],"items":["Government support for research and innovation (NASA, NIH, patents, grants)","Regulation of technology (FDA, FCC, EPA, cybersecurity)","How technology changes governing: e-government, data, digital campaigns","Privacy, surveillance, and civil-liberties trade-offs","Skill: researching and presenting a science-and-technology policy issue"]},{"unit":"Comparing Political & Economic Systems","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.44(d)(11)","113.44(d)(5)","113.44(d)(20)"],"items":["Unitary, confederal, and federal systems","Presidential vs. parliamentary democracies","Authoritarian and totalitarian governments","Economic systems: capitalism, socialism, communism","Comparing the U.S. system to another nation's government","Skill: building a comparison chart and delivering an oral summary"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Flex (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["113.44(d)(19)","113.44(d)(20)","113.44(d)(21)"],"items":["Absorb calendar shifts, assemblies, and testing interruptions","Reteach the lowest-mastery standards using assessment data","Makeup work, retakes, and targeted remediation","Enrichment: current-events / Supreme Court case-of-the-term study","Spiral research, writing, and decision-making skills with a named mini-task"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review, Final Exam & Civic Capstone (wiggle)","weeks":2,"kind":"assessment","teks":["113.44(d)(6)","113.44(d)(7)","113.44(d)(14)","113.44(d)(21)"],"items":["Cumulative course review across all six grading periods","Comprehensive final exam","Civic-action / public-policy capstone project","Capstone presentations and structured peer feedback","Reflection on informed, engaged citizenship"]}]},{"course":"Psychology","tacSection":"19 TAC §113.45","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter113_sec.113.45","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-113"],"strands":[{"code":"113.45(c)(1)","statement":"History. The student understands the development of the field of psychology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(1)(A)","text":"identify characteristics that differentiate the field of psychology from other related social sciences;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(1)(B)","text":"trace the historical development of the contemporary perspectives in psychology, including biological, behavioral, cognitive, sociocultural, humanistic, and psychodynamic; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(1)(C)","text":"explore subfields and career opportunities available in the science of psychology."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(2)","statement":"Science of psychology. The student differentiates the processes of theory development and validation. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(2)(A)","text":"define and differentiate the concepts of theory and principle;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(2)(B)","text":"identify and describe the basic methods of social scientific reasoning;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(2)(C)","text":"apply the standards of the American Psychological Association (APA) for ethical decision making regarding the collection, storage, and use of psychological data; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(2)(D)","text":"define and interpret measures of central tendency (mean, median, and mode) and dispersion (range and standard deviation)."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(3)","statement":"Science of psychology. The student understands the relationship between biology and behavior. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(3)(A)","text":"describe the anatomy of the central and peripheral nervous systems and the endocrine system; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(3)(B)","text":"explain the effects of the endocrine and nervous systems on development and behavior."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(4)","statement":"Science of psychology. The student understands how sensations and perceptions influence cognition and behavior. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(4)(A)","text":"explain the capabilities and limitations of sensory systems and individual perceptions; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(4)(B)","text":"understand the interaction of the individual and the environment in determining sensation and perception."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(5)","statement":"Individual development. The student understands that development is a life-long process. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(5)(A)","text":"critique the various perspectives presented in the nature versus nurture debate;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(5)(B)","text":"trace the influence of physical development on the individual;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(5)(C)","text":"discuss the role of the caregiver on individual development;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(5)(D)","text":"explain factors involved in cognitive development according to Jean Piaget;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(5)(E)","text":"describe Erik Erikson's stages of psychosocial development;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(5)(F)","text":"evaluate the predicted outcomes of given courses of actions in particular situations based on an understanding of the development of morality; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(5)(G)","text":"evaluate the presented theories of human development and specify the strengths and weaknesses of each."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(6)","statement":"Individual development. The student understands behavioral and social learning theories. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(6)(A)","text":"demonstrate an understanding of the principles of operant and classical conditioning and of social learning; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(6)(B)","text":"describe the processes of learning using typical classroom situations."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(7)","statement":"Individual identity. The student understands the principles of motivation and emotion. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(7)(A)","text":"compare predominant theories of motivation and emotion; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(7)(B)","text":"explore the interaction of biological and cultural factors in emotion and motivation."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(8)","statement":"Individual identity. The student understands the nature of intelligence. The student is expected to differentiate the various types of intelligence.","expectations":[]},{"code":"113.45(c)(9)","statement":"Individual identity. The student understands the basic principles of tests and measurements. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(9)(A)","text":"describe statistical concepts used in testing; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(9)(B)","text":"differentiate among aptitude, achievement, and Intelligence Quotient (IQ) tests."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(10)","statement":"Individual identity. The student understands the development and assessment of personality. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(10)(A)","text":"define personality;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(10)(B)","text":"compare and evaluate various theories of personality, including psychodynamic, trait, humanistic, and sociocultural; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(10)(C)","text":"describe personality assessment tools."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(11)","statement":"Individual experience. The student understands basic elements of cognition. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(11)(A)","text":"define and identify the basic elements of thought;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(11)(B)","text":"identify strategies and obstacles associated with problem solving and decision making;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(11)(C)","text":"explore the structural features of language;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(11)(D)","text":"discuss theories of language acquisition and development;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(11)(E)","text":"evaluate the limitations and capabilities of the information processing model; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(11)(F)","text":"understand the states and levels of consciousness."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(12)","statement":"Individual experience. The student understands the multifaceted aspects of mental health. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(12)(A)","text":"explain stress and the individual's physiological, behavioral, and psychological responses to stressors;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(12)(B)","text":"evaluate cognitive and behavioral strategies for dealing with stress;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(12)(C)","text":"analyze the challenges inherent in defining abnormal behavior and acknowledge the sociocultural stigma of labeling behavior as abnormal;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(12)(D)","text":"recognize the biological, social, and cognitive origins of abnormal behavior;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(12)(E)","text":"discuss major categories of abnormal behaviors and identify their respective characteristics as classified in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual (DSM); and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(12)(F)","text":"evaluate the effectiveness of past and present methods of therapy."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(13)","statement":"The individual in society. The student will understand the influence of society and culture on behavior and cognition. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(13)(A)","text":"describe how attributions affect explanations of behavior;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(13)(B)","text":"explore the nature and effects of bias and discrimination;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(13)(C)","text":"describe circumstances in which conformity and obedience are likely to occur;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(13)(D)","text":"describe the effects of the presence of others on individual behavior;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(13)(E)","text":"discuss the nature of altruism;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(13)(F)","text":"discuss the factors influencing attraction; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(13)(G)","text":"identify sources of attitude formation and assess methods used to influence attitudes."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(14)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student applies critical-thinking skills to organize and use information acquired from a variety of valid sources, including electronic technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(14)(A)","text":"create a product on a contemporary psychology-related issue or topic using critical methods of inquiry;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(14)(B)","text":"draw and evaluate conclusions from qualitative information;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(14)(C)","text":"apply evaluation rules to quantitative information; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(14)(D)","text":"analyze information by sequencing, categorizing, identifying cause-and-effect relationships, comparing, contrasting, finding the main idea, summarizing, making generalizations and predictions, and drawing inferences and conclusions."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(15)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student communicates in written, oral, and visual forms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(15)(A)","text":"use psychology-related terminology correctly;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(15)(B)","text":"use standard grammar, spelling, sentence structure, and punctuation;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(15)(C)","text":"transfer information from one medium to another, including written to visual and written or visual to statistical, using computer software as appropriate; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(15)(D)","text":"create written, oral, and visual presentations of social studies information."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(16)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student uses problem-solving and decision-making skills, working independently and with others, in a variety of settings. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(16)(A)","text":"use a problem-solving process to identify a problem, gather information, list and consider options, consider advantages and disadvantages, choose and implement a solution, and evaluate the effectiveness of the solution;"},{"code":"113.45(c)(16)(B)","text":"use a decision-making process to identify a situation that requires a decision, gather information, identify options, predict consequences, and take action to implement a decision; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(16)(C)","text":"participate in conflict resolution using persuasion, compromise, debate, and negotiation."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(17)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student develops long-term and short-term goal-setting skills for individual and community problem solving. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(17)(A)","text":"illustrate the relationship and sequence between intermediate goals and terminal goals; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(17)(B)","text":"monitor and evaluate self-directed inquiry or projects for timelines, accuracy, and goal attainment."}]},{"code":"113.45(c)(18)","statement":"Science and technology. The student understands the relationship of changes in technology to personal growth and development. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.45(c)(18)(A)","text":"analyze examples of attitudes, beliefs, and behaviors related to changes in available technology; and"},{"code":"113.45(c)(18)(B)","text":"evaluate the impact of changes in technology on personal growth and development."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Intro & History of Psychology","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(1)","113.45(c)(14)","113.45(c)(15)"],"items":["What is psychology? Subfields, careers, and the biopsychosocial view","Roots of the field: Wundt & structuralism (Titchener) vs. functionalism (James)","Major schools: psychoanalytic, behaviorist, humanistic, cognitive, biological","Contemporary approaches and how the field keeps evolving","Build a history-of-psychology timeline infographic (visual communication)","Psychology in the media: separate real science from pop-psych myths"]},{"unit":"Research Methods & Science of Psychology","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(2)","113.45(c)(14)","113.45(c)(16)"],"items":["Theory, hypothesis, and how theories are validated, revised, or discarded","Descriptive methods: case study, naturalistic observation, and survey","Correlation vs. causation and the experimental method (IV/DV, control, random assignment)","Descriptive statistics: reading graphs, mean/median/mode, distributions","Research ethics & IRB simulation: design a simple study and defend the decisions","Evaluate sources and spot pseudoscience in real media claims"]},{"unit":"Biological Bases of Behavior","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(3)","113.45(c)(18)"],"items":["The neuron: structure and the action potential","Neurotransmitters and how drugs act at the synapse","Nervous system divisions (CNS/PNS, autonomic) and the endocrine system","Brain structures: brainstem, limbic system, and cortical lobes","Genetics, heredity, and the nature–nurture question in behavior","Neuroimaging technology (EEG, CT, fMRI) and what each reveals"]},{"unit":"Sensation & Perception","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(4)"],"items":["Sensation vs. perception: transduction, absolute & difference thresholds","Vision: eye structure and color theories","Hearing and the other senses (touch, taste, smell, vestibular, kinesthetic)","Perceptual organization: Gestalt principles and depth cues","Perceptual sets and illusions — how expectation shapes cognition and behavior"]},{"unit":"Buffer / reteach / assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["113.45(c)(14)","113.45(c)(15)"],"items":["Data-driven reteach of high-miss concepts from GP1–GP2","Unit-test corrections and small-group targeted reteach","Skills lab: evidence-based writing and citing valid sources","Flex/catch-up day to absorb calendar shifts, assemblies, or drills"]},{"unit":"Lifespan Development","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(5)"],"items":["Prenatal development, teratogens, and newborn abilities","Piaget's stages of cognitive development","Attachment (Harlow, Ainsworth) and parenting styles","Erikson's psychosocial stages across the lifespan","Kohlberg's stages of moral development","Adolescence, adulthood, aging, and death & dying"]},{"unit":"Learning","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(6)"],"items":["Classical conditioning: Pavlov, acquisition, extinction, generalization","Applications: taste aversion and conditioned fear (Watson & Little Albert)","Operant conditioning: reinforcement vs. punishment (Skinner)","Schedules of reinforcement and shaping behavior","Observational and social learning theory (Bandura, Bobo doll)"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Exam","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["113.45(c)(1)","113.45(c)(2)","113.45(c)(3)","113.45(c)(4)","113.45(c)(5)","113.45(c)(6)"],"items":["Cumulative review stations: history through learning","Practice semester exam with item analysis of weak spots","Semester exam","Reflection and goal-setting for semester two"]},{"unit":"Motivation & Emotion","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(7)"],"items":["Theories of motivation: instinct, drive-reduction, arousal, Maslow's hierarchy","Hunger and eating: biology, environment, and eating disorders","Theories of emotion: James-Lange, Cannon-Bard, Schachter-Singer","Stress, the general adaptation syndrome, and coping strategies","Expressing and detecting emotion through nonverbal cues"]},{"unit":"Intelligence","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(8)"],"items":["Defining intelligence: general 'g' vs. Gardner's multiple intelligences","Sternberg's triarchic theory and emotional intelligence","History of IQ testing (Binet, Terman, Wechsler)","Extremes of intelligence and the nature–nurture contribution","Cultural bias, stereotype threat, and fairness in intelligence testing"]},{"unit":"Tests & Measurement","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(9)","113.45(c)(16)"],"items":["Standardization and the normal curve","Reliability and validity of a measure","Types of tests: aptitude, achievement, and personality","Construct and score a short survey instrument (problem-solving)","Interpreting scores ethically: uses and misuses of testing"]},{"unit":"Personality","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(10)"],"items":["Psychoanalytic theory: Freud's structure of mind and defense mechanisms","Neo-Freudians and the humanistic view (Rogers, Maslow)","Trait theories and the Big Five model","Social-cognitive theory and reciprocal determinism (Bandura)","Assessing personality: projective tests vs. self-report inventories"]},{"unit":"Cognition: Memory, Thinking & Language","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(11)","113.45(c)(18)"],"items":["Three-stage memory model: encoding, storage, retrieval","Forgetting, memory construction, and eyewitness reliability","Thinking: concepts, problem-solving, heuristics, and biases","Language development and the language–thought relationship","Technology and cognition: search engines, note apps, and how they change memory"]},{"unit":"Mental Health & Psychological Disorders","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(12)"],"items":["Defining abnormality; the DSM and models of diagnosis","Anxiety, OCD, and trauma- and stressor-related disorders","Mood disorders and suicide awareness / prevention resources","Schizophrenia and personality disorders","Therapies: psychodynamic, humanistic, behavioral, and cognitive (CBT)","Biomedical treatment and reducing mental-health stigma"]},{"unit":"Social & Cultural Psychology","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(13)","113.45(c)(15)"],"items":["Attribution, attitudes, and cognitive dissonance","Conformity and obedience (Asch, Milgram)","Group behavior: bystander effect, groupthink, deindividuation","Prejudice, stereotypes, and how culture shapes behavior and cognition","Present a cross-cultural case study (visual + oral communication)"]},{"unit":"Applied Psychology: Goal-Setting & Technology","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.45(c)(17)","113.45(c)(18)"],"items":["Growth mindset and the psychology of habit formation","Write SMART short- and long-term goals and a personal growth plan","How technology (social media, screen time, AI) shapes development and well-being","Digital well-being: applying psychology to healthy technology use"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Project","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["113.45(c)(14)","113.45(c)(15)","113.45(c)(16)","113.45(c)(17)"],"items":["Cumulative review stations across semester-two units","Capstone: investigate a psychological question using valid sources","Present the final project in written, oral, and visual form","Course reflection and long-term goal check-in","Final exam"]}]},{"course":"Sociology","tacSection":"19 TAC §113.46","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["https://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter113_sec.113.46","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-113"],"strands":[{"code":"113.46(c)(1)","statement":"Foundations of sociology. The student understands the theoretical perspectives of the historical interpretations of human social development. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(1)(A)","text":"describe the development of the field of sociology;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(1)(B)","text":"identify leading sociologists in the field of social science, including Auguste Comte, Emile Durkheim, Herbert Spencer, Max Weber, and Karl Marx, and interpret their contributions to the foundation of sociology; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(1)(C)","text":"identify sociologists such as W. E. B. DuBois, Booker T. Washington, Robert E. Park, Harriet Martineau, Jane Addams, Robert Nisbet, and Julian Samora and interpret their contributions to the field."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(2)","statement":"Foundations of sociology. The student understands how society evolves and cause and effect of social and institutional change. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(2)(A)","text":"differentiate types of societies such as hunting and gathering, agrarian, pastoral, industrial, and post-industrial;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(2)(B)","text":"identify and describe the types of societies that exist in the world today;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(2)(C)","text":"examine changes in U.S. institutions and society resulting from industrialization, urbanization, and immigrant assimilation; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(2)(D)","text":"analyze information about cultural life in the United States and other countries over time."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(3)","statement":"Culture and social structure. The student examines world cultures. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(3)(A)","text":"identify the elements of culture to include language, symbols, norms, and values;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(3)(B)","text":"explain how the elements of culture form a whole culture; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(3)(C)","text":"give examples of subcultures and describe what makes them unique."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(4)","statement":"Culture and social structure. The student understands types of groups and their functions. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(4)(A)","text":"describe models of primary, secondary, formal, informal, and reference groups and e-communities; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(4)(B)","text":"analyze groups in terms of membership roles, status, values, mores, role conflicts, and methods of resolution."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(5)","statement":"Culture and social structure. The student differentiates and recognizes examples of subculture and counterculture. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(5)(A)","text":"compare cultural norms such as ethnicity, national origin, age, socioeconomic status, and gender among various U.S. subculture groups;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(5)(B)","text":"describe stereotypes of various U.S. subcultures;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(5)(C)","text":"analyze social problems in selected U.S. subcultures; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(5)(D)","text":"examine counterculture movements and analyze their impact on society as a whole."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(6)","statement":"Individual and society. The student understands the process of socialization. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(6)(A)","text":"define socialization and describe how the process of socialization is culturally determined;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(6)(B)","text":"differentiate the agents of socialization and evaluate their functions and roles; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(6)(C)","text":"trace socialization as a lifelong process."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(7)","statement":"Individual and society. The student understands the concept of adolescence and its characteristics. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(7)(A)","text":"explain how education, exclusion from the labor force, and the juvenile justice system led to the development of adolescence as a distinct stage of the life cycle;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(7)(B)","text":"identify and interpret the five characteristics of adolescence: biological growth and development, an undefined status, increased decision making, increased pressures, and the search for self;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(7)(C)","text":"identify issues and concerns facing contemporary adolescents such as dating, dating violence, sexuality, teen parenting, drug use, suicide, and eating disorders; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(7)(D)","text":"identify and discuss the skills adolescents need to make responsible life choices."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(8)","statement":"Individual and society. The student understands the life stage of adulthood and its characteristics. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(8)(A)","text":"identify the stages of adult development and compare the differences between male and female development;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(8)(B)","text":"analyze the traditional roles of work and how the composition of the labor force has changed in the United States; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(8)(C)","text":"analyze the characteristics of late adulthood and changes on the individual and society such as retirement, physical and mental functioning, dependency on others, and death."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(9)","statement":"Individual and society. The student will explain the nature and social function of deviance. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(9)(A)","text":"compare theories of deviance such as the functionalist, conflict, and interactionist perspectives;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(9)(B)","text":"interpret differences in crime and arrest rates by social categories such as ethnicity, gender, socioeconomic status, and age, including cross-reference with the National Crime Victimization Survey; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(9)(C)","text":"analyze the criminal justice system in the United States in relation to deviant behavior."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(10)","statement":"Social inequality. The student understands the nature of social stratification in society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(10)(A)","text":"analyze the characteristics and components of caste and class systems and social mobility and how motivation affects each;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(10)(B)","text":"define poverty and its components and analyze poverty's impact on the individual and society;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(10)(C)","text":"contrast theories of social stratification; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(10)(D)","text":"recognize and examine global stratification and inequality."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(11)","statement":"Social inequality. The student understands the impact of race and ethnicity on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(11)(A)","text":"define race and ethnicity and differentiate among the distinguishing characteristics of minority groups;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(11)(B)","text":"contrast the terms discrimination, prejudice, and bias;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(11)(C)","text":"discuss the ramifications of stereotyping;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(11)(D)","text":"analyze the varying treatment patterns of minority groups such as African American, Asian American, Hispanic American, and American Indian; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(11)(E)","text":"explain instances of institutional racism in American society."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(12)","statement":"Social inequality. The student understands changing societal views on gender, age, and health. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(12)(A)","text":"analyze how gender roles affect the opportunities available to men and women in society;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(12)(B)","text":"analyze the effects of an aging society;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(12)(C)","text":"compare the nature of health care in a global society; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(12)(D)","text":"evaluate the nature of health care in different segments of American society."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(13)","statement":"Social institutions. The student identifies the basic social institution of the family and explains its influences on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(13)(A)","text":"define the functions and rituals of the family and how the family has changed over time;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(13)(B)","text":"define family systems and patterns;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(13)(C)","text":"analyze the trends in American society regarding family life and the needs that the institution of family satisfies; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(13)(D)","text":"analyze ways in which family life can be disrupted."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(14)","statement":"Social institutions. The student identifies the basic social institutions of economics and politics and explains their influence on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(14)(A)","text":"define and differentiate between the economic models of free enterprise and socialism and how they impact society;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(14)(B)","text":"define and differentiate among different types of government and discuss the legitimacy of those in power and the impact of each on its citizens; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(14)(C)","text":"trace the changes in ideas about citizenship and participation of different groups through time."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(15)","statement":"Social institutions. The student identifies the basic social institutions of education and religion and explains their influence on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(15)(A)","text":"explain functionalist, conflict, and interactionist theories of education;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(15)(B)","text":"argue and defend some current issues in American education;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(15)(C)","text":"examine religion from the sociological point of view;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(15)(D)","text":"analyze the functions of society and the basic societal needs that religion serves; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(15)(E)","text":"compare and contrast distinctive features of religion in the United States with religion in other societies."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(16)","statement":"Social institutions. The student understands the basic social institutions of science and the mass media and their influence on society. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(16)(A)","text":"identify factors that have contributed to the institutionalization of science, explain the norms of scientific research, and explain how these norms differ from the realities of scientific research;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(16)(B)","text":"trace major developments in the history of mass media and identify the types of mass media in the United States;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(16)(C)","text":"explain the differences between the functionalist and conflict perspectives of mass media; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(16)(D)","text":"examine contemporary mass media issues."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(17)","statement":"Changing world. The student understands how population and urbanization contribute to a changing social world. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(17)(A)","text":"describe the study of demography, the basic demographic concepts, and changes in settlement patterns on society; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(17)(B)","text":"explain and critique various theories of population growth and its impact on society."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(18)","statement":"Changing world. The student understands how collective behavior, social movements, and modernization contribute to a changing social world. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(18)(A)","text":"compare and contrast various types of collective behavior and social movements and how they affect society;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(18)(B)","text":"discuss theories that have been developed to explain collective behavior and social movements; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(18)(C)","text":"illustrate three social processes that contribute to social change and discuss and evaluate how technology, population, natural environment, revolution, and war cause cultures to change."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(19)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student applies critical-thinking skills to organize and use information acquired from a variety of valid sources, including electronic technology. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(19)(A)","text":"create a product on a contemporary sociological issue or topic using critical methods of inquiry;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(19)(B)","text":"analyze information by sequencing, categorizing, identifying cause-and-effect relationships, comparing, contrasting, finding the main idea, summarizing, making generalizations and predictions, and drawing inferences and conclusions; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(19)(C)","text":"use appropriate mathematical skills to interpret sociological information."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(20)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student communicates in written, oral, and visual forms. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(20)(A)","text":"use sociology-related terminology correctly;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(20)(B)","text":"use standard grammar, spelling, sentence structure, and punctuation;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(20)(C)","text":"transfer information from one medium to another, including written to visual and written or visual to statistical, using computer software as appropriate; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(20)(D)","text":"create written, oral, and visual presentations of social studies information."}]},{"code":"113.46(c)(21)","statement":"Social studies skills. The student uses problem-solving and decision-making skills, working independently and with others, in a variety of settings. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"113.46(c)(21)(A)","text":"use a problem-solving process to identify a problem, gather information, list and consider options, consider advantages and disadvantages, choose and implement a solution, and evaluate the effectiveness of the solution;"},{"code":"113.46(c)(21)(B)","text":"use a decision-making process to identify a situation that requires a decision, gather information, identify options, predict consequences, and take action to implement a decision; and"},{"code":"113.46(c)(21)(C)","text":"participate in conflict resolution using persuasion, compromise, debate, and negotiation."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Introduction to Sociology & the Sociological Perspective","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(1)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Define sociology and the sociological imagination (Mills); personal troubles vs. public issues","Compare the founders: Comte, Durkheim, Marx, Weber and their questions about society","Distinguish the three theoretical perspectives: functionalism, conflict theory, symbolic interactionism","Apply each perspective to a familiar institution (e.g., school or sports)","Set up an evidence journal: evaluate a valid source and cite it (intro to skills strand)"]},{"unit":"Doing Sociology: Research Methods & Social Science Skills","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(1)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)","113.46(c)(21)"],"items":["Steps of the scientific method applied to social research; hypothesis and variables","Compare methods: surveys, experiments, field observation, secondary/historical analysis","Read and interpret data: tables, graphs, and correlation vs. causation","Research ethics: informed consent, confidentiality, objectivity and bias","Mini-project: design a small survey, collect data, present findings orally and visually"]},{"unit":"How Societies Evolve: Social & Institutional Change","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(2)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Types of societies: hunter-gatherer, horticultural/pastoral, agrarian, industrial, post-industrial","Causes of social change: technology, environment, population, ideology, conflict","Cause-and-effect case study: the Industrial Revolution and institutional change","Durkheim's mechanical vs. organic solidarity","Debate: is social change progress? Evidence-based written argument"]},{"unit":"Culture & World Cultures","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(3)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Elements of culture: symbols, language, values, beliefs, norms (folkways, mores, taboos)","Material vs. nonmaterial culture; cultural universals","Ethnocentrism vs. cultural relativism; culture shock","Compare two or more world cultures using valid sources","Cultural change, diffusion, and cultural lag","Visual project: build a culture profile comparing traditions, values, and norms"]},{"unit":"Groups, Subcultures & Countercultures","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(4)","113.46(c)(5)","113.46(c)(21)"],"items":["Types of groups: primary vs. secondary, in-groups vs. out-groups, reference groups","Group functions, size effects (dyad/triad), and leadership styles","Formal organizations and bureaucracy (Weber's characteristics)","Define subculture with examples; how subcultures form","Distinguish counterculture from subculture with historical examples","Group task: classify real-world groups and defend classifications collaboratively"]},{"unit":"Socialization & the Formation of Self","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(6)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Nature vs. nurture; cases of isolation and feral children","Theories of self: Cooley's looking-glass self, Mead's I/Me and role-taking","Agents of socialization: family, school, peers, media, religion, workplace","Resocialization and total institutions (Goffman)","Case analysis: how one agent shaped a person's values (written reflection)"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Cumulative Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["113.46(c)(1)","113.46(c)(2)","113.46(c)(3)","113.46(c)(19)"],"items":["Spiral review of perspectives, methods, social change, culture, groups, socialization","Vocabulary and concept synthesis stations","Practice applying perspectives to new scenarios","Cumulative semester assessment","Reteach targeted gaps identified from assessment data"]},{"unit":"The Life Course I: Adolescence","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(7)","113.46(c)(20)","113.46(c)(21)"],"items":["Adolescence as a social construction; historical emergence of the teen years","Characteristics: identity formation, peer influence, autonomy vs. dependence","Rites of passage across cultures","Contemporary issues: social media, education, and adolescent development","Collaborative panel: challenges and supports for adolescents today"]},{"unit":"The Life Course II: Adulthood & Aging","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(8)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Stages of adulthood: early, middle, and late adulthood characteristics","Transitions: work, marriage, parenthood, empty nest, retirement","Theories of aging and the social meaning of growing older","Death and dying as social experiences (Kübler-Ross stages, cross-cultural)","Interview-based project: an adult life transition, presented visually"]},{"unit":"Deviance, Crime & Social Control","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(9)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(21)"],"items":["Define deviance; how norms and context shape what counts as deviant","Explaining deviance: strain (Merton), labeling, differential association, control theory","Functions of deviance (Durkheim) and social control mechanisms","Crime types and the criminal justice system; interpreting crime statistics","Problem-solving task: propose an evidence-based response to a deviance scenario"]},{"unit":"Social Stratification","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(10)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Systems of stratification: slavery, caste, estate, class","Explaining stratification: Davis-Moore functionalist view vs. conflict/Marxist view","Social class in the U.S.; measuring wealth, income, power, prestige","Social mobility: upward, downward, intergenerational; the American Dream examined","Poverty: absolute vs. relative; analyze stratification data and draw conclusions"]},{"unit":"Race & Ethnicity","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(11)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Race as a social construct vs. ethnicity; minority and dominant groups","Prejudice, stereotypes, discrimination, and racism (individual vs. institutional)","Patterns of intergroup relations: assimilation, pluralism, segregation, genocide","Immigration and ethnic diversity in the U.S. and the borderland context","Research brief: an intergroup relations case study using valid sources"]},{"unit":"Gender, Age & Health","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(12)","113.46(c)(20)","113.46(c)(21)"],"items":["Sex vs. gender; gender roles and socialization; changing societal views","Gender stratification in work, family, and politics","Ageism and the changing social status of the elderly","Health and illness as social issues; the sociology of medicine and access","Decision-making task: evaluate a policy addressing inequality of gender, age, or health"]},{"unit":"Social Institutions I: The Family","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(13)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Family functions and forms across cultures (nuclear, extended, blended)","Marriage patterns, kinship, and residence/descent systems","Perspectives on the family: functionalist, conflict, interactionist","Changing family trends: divorce, cohabitation, single-parent, dual-income","Analyze family data and connect trends to broader social change"]},{"unit":"Social Institutions II: Economy & Politics","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(14)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(21)"],"items":["Economic systems: capitalism, socialism, mixed economies","The changing world of work: agricultural to service to gig economy","Political systems and types of authority (Weber: traditional, charismatic, legal-rational)","Power, the state, and civic participation","Connect economy and politics: how they shape and influence society"]},{"unit":"Social Institutions III: Education & Religion","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(15)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Functions of education: manifest and latent; the hidden curriculum","Education and inequality: tracking, funding, and opportunity","Sociological views of religion (Durkheim, Marx, Weber)","Types of religious organizations and secularization trends","Compare how education and religion influence society using valid evidence"]},{"unit":"Social Institutions IV: Science & Mass Media","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(16)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Science as a social institution; norms of science and technology's social role","Mass media forms and their social functions","Media effects, agenda-setting, and representation","Social media, digital culture, and information/misinformation","Media analysis project: critically evaluate a media message and present findings"]},{"unit":"A Changing World: Population & Urbanization","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(17)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)"],"items":["Demography basics: birth/death rates, migration, and population pyramids","Population growth, the demographic transition, and Malthus vs. critics","Urbanization: the growth of cities, suburbanization, and megacities","Interpreting population data and projections","Case study: population and urbanization pressures on a real region"]},{"unit":"A Changing World: Collective Behavior, Social Movements & Modernization","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["113.46(c)(18)","113.46(c)(20)","113.46(c)(21)"],"items":["Forms of collective behavior: crowds, mobs, panics, rumor, fads, public opinion","Types and stages of social movements; theories (relative deprivation, resource mobilization)","Historical and contemporary movements and their social impact","Modernization theory and the effects of globalization","Action project: design an informed campaign for a chosen cause and present it"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)","113.46(c)(21)"],"items":["Flex days to absorb calendar shifts, assemblies, and weather closures","Reteach highest-need concepts from unit assessment data","Catch-up on missed labs, projects, or presentations","Skills clinic: source evaluation, citation, and presentation polishing","Low-stakes checkpoint quiz and feedback"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Capstone Review & Final","weeks":2,"kind":"assessment","teks":["113.46(c)(1)","113.46(c)(9)","113.46(c)(10)","113.46(c)(18)","113.46(c)(19)","113.46(c)(20)","113.46(c)(21)"],"items":["Comprehensive spiral review across all six strands via concept maps","Capstone: apply sociological perspectives to a self-chosen social issue","Present capstone in written, oral, and visual forms","Cumulative final exam","Reflection: how sociology explains your world; course wrap-up"]}]},{"course":"Mathematical Models with Applications","tacSection":"19 TAC §111.43","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["http://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter111_sec.111.43","https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/texas/19-Tex-Admin-Code-SS-111-43","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-111"],"strands":[{"code":"111.43(c)(1)","statement":"Mathematical process standards. The student uses mathematical processes to acquire and demonstrate mathematical understanding. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"apply mathematics to problems arising in everyday life, society, and the workplace;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"use a problem-solving model that incorporates analyzing given information, formulating a plan or strategy, determining a solution, justifying the solution, and evaluating the problem-solving process and the reasonableness of the solution;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"select tools, including real objects, manipulatives, paper and pencil, and technology as appropriate, and techniques, including mental math, estimation, and number sense as appropriate, to solve problems;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"communicate mathematical ideas, reasoning, and their implications using multiple representations, including symbols, diagrams, graphs, and language as appropriate;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"create and use representations to organize, record, and communicate mathematical ideas;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"analyze mathematical relationships to connect and communicate mathematical ideas; and"},{"code":"(G)","text":"display, explain, and justify mathematical ideas and arguments using precise mathematical language in written or oral communication."}]},{"code":"111.43(c)(2)","statement":"Mathematical modeling in personal finance. The student uses mathematical processes with graphical and numerical techniques to study patterns and analyze data related to personal finance. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use rates and linear functions to solve problems involving personal finance and budgeting, including compensations and deductions;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"solve problems involving personal taxes; and"},{"code":"(C)","text":"analyze data to make decisions about banking, including options for online banking, checking accounts, overdraft protection, processing fees, and debit card/ATM fees."}]},{"code":"111.43(c)(3)","statement":"Mathematical modeling in personal finance. The student uses mathematical processes with algebraic formulas, graphs, and amortization modeling to solve problems involving credit. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use formulas to generate tables to display series of payments for loan amortizations resulting from financed purchases;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze personal credit options in retail purchasing and compare relative advantages and disadvantages of each option;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"use technology to create amortization models to investigate home financing and compare buying a home to renting a home; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"use technology to create amortization models to investigate automobile financing and compare buying a vehicle to leasing a vehicle."}]},{"code":"111.43(c)(4)","statement":"Mathematical modeling in personal finance. The student uses mathematical processes with algebraic formulas, numerical techniques, and graphs to solve problems related to financial planning. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"analyze and compare coverage options and rates in insurance;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"investigate and compare investment options, including stocks, bonds, annuities, certificates of deposit, and retirement plans; and"},{"code":"(C)","text":"analyze types of savings options involving simple and compound interest and compare relative advantages of these options."}]},{"code":"111.43(c)(5)","statement":"Mathematical modeling in science and engineering. The student applies mathematical processes with algebraic techniques to study patterns and analyze data as it applies to science. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use proportionality and inverse variation to describe physical laws such as Hook's Law, Newton's Second Law of Motion, and Boyle's Law;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"use exponential models available through technology to model growth and decay in areas, including radioactive decay; and"},{"code":"(C)","text":"use quadratic functions to model motion."}]},{"code":"111.43(c)(6)","statement":"Mathematical modeling in science and engineering. The student applies mathematical processes with algebra and geometry to study patterns and analyze data as it applies to architecture and engineering. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use similarity, geometric transformations, symmetry, and perspective drawings to describe mathematical patterns and structure in architecture;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"use scale factors with two-dimensional and three-dimensional objects to demonstrate proportional and non-proportional changes in surface area and volume as applied to fields;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"use the Pythagorean Theorem and special right-triangle relationships to calculate distances; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"use trigonometric ratios to calculate distances and angle measures as applied to fields."}]},{"code":"111.43(c)(7)","statement":"Mathematical modeling in fine arts. The student uses mathematical processes with algebra and geometry to study patterns and analyze data as it applies to fine arts. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use trigonometric ratios and functions available through technology to model periodic behavior in art and music;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"use similarity, geometric transformations, symmetry, and perspective drawings to describe mathematical patterns and structure in art and photography;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"use geometric transformations, proportions, and periodic motion to describe mathematical patterns and structure in music; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"use scale factors with two-dimensional and three-dimensional objects to demonstrate proportional and non-proportional changes in surface area and volume as applied to fields."}]},{"code":"111.43(c)(8)","statement":"Mathematical modeling in social sciences. The student applies mathematical processes to determine the number of elements in a finite sample space and compute the probability of an event. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"determine the number of ways an event may occur using combinations, permutations, and the Fundamental Counting Principle;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"compare theoretical to empirical probability; and"},{"code":"(C)","text":"use experiments to determine the reasonableness of a theoretical model such as binomial or geometric."}]},{"code":"111.43(c)(9)","statement":"Mathematical modeling in social sciences. The student applies mathematical processes and mathematical models to analyze data as it applies to social sciences. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"interpret information from various graphs, including line graphs, bar graphs, circle graphs, histograms, scatterplots, dot plots, stem-and-leaf plots, and box and whisker plots, to draw conclusions from the data and determine the strengths and weaknesses of conclusions;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"analyze numerical data using measures of central tendency (mean, median, and mode) and variability (range, interquartile range or IQR, and standard deviation) in order to make inferences with normal distributions;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"distinguish the purposes and differences among types of research, including surveys, experiments, and observational studies;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"use data from a sample to estimate population mean or population proportion;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"analyze marketing claims based on graphs and statistics from electronic and print media and justify the validity of stated or implied conclusions; and"},{"code":"(F)","text":"use regression methods available through technology to model linear and exponential functions, interpret correlations, and make predictions."}]},{"code":"111.43(c)(10)","statement":"Mathematical modeling in social sciences. The student applies mathematical processes to design a study and use graphical, numerical, and analytical techniques to communicate the results of the study. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"formulate a meaningful question, determine the data needed to answer the question, gather the appropriate data, analyze the data, and draw reasonable conclusions; and"},{"code":"(B)","text":"communicate methods used, analyses conducted, and conclusions drawn for a data-analysis project through the use of one or more of the following: a written report, a visual display, an oral report, or a multi-media presentation."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Foundations of Mathematical Modeling & Data","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["The modeling cycle: pose a question, formulate a model, compute, interpret in context","Representing relationships with tables, graphs, verbal descriptions, and equations","Choosing units, precision, and appropriate tools (calculator, spreadsheet)","Slope and rate of change as a model parameter to be interpreted","Building spreadsheets and formulas to organize real-world data","Communicating and justifying mathematical conclusions with evidence"]},{"unit":"Personal Finance: Income, Budgeting & Spending Models","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.43(c)(2)","111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Gross vs. net income: wages, salary, commission, and tips","Payroll deductions, federal income-tax brackets, and FICA","Building and balancing a monthly personal budget","Simple vs. compound interest on savings accounts","Modeling account growth with linear and exponential formulas","Comparing checking/savings products using fees and data"]},{"unit":"Credit, Loans & Amortization","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.43(c)(3)","111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Cash vs. credit vs. financing for large purchases","Credit cards: APR, minimum payments, and revolving balances","The monthly payment formula for installment loans","Building an amortization schedule (principal vs. interest split)","Total cost of credit and the effect of term and interest rate","Credit scores, credit reports, and borrowing power"]},{"unit":"Financial Planning: Saving, Investing & Retirement","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.43(c)(4)","111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Comparing investment vehicles: stocks, bonds, mutual funds, CDs","Risk vs. return and the Rule of 72","Future value of periodic investments (annuities)","Retirement accounts: 401(k), IRA, and employer match","Insurance types, deductibles, and net-worth statements","Building a long-term financial-plan spreadsheet"]},{"unit":"Mathematical Modeling in Science","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.43(c)(5)","111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Unit/dimensional analysis and scientific notation","Proportional reasoning and measurement conversions","Fitting linear models to experimental data","Exponential models: growth, decay, and half-life","Direct and inverse variation in physical laws","Interpreting residuals and reporting model error"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Semester Assessment","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["111.43(c)(1)","111.43(c)(2)","111.43(c)(3)","111.43(c)(4)"],"items":["Cumulative review of personal-finance modeling","Mixed practice: interest, credit, and amortization problems","Spiral review of process standards and tool use","Semester exam / performance task","Error analysis and targeted reteach stations"]},{"unit":"Modeling in Architecture & Engineering","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.43(c)(6)","111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Similarity, scale factor, and scale drawings/blueprints","Perimeter, area, and composite figures in design","Surface area and volume for material estimates","Right-triangle trigonometry and indirect measurement","The Pythagorean theorem in structural layout","Design task: scale model with a cost estimate"]},{"unit":"Mathematical Modeling in Fine Arts","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.43(c)(7)","111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["The golden ratio and proportion in art and architecture","Symmetry, transformations, and tessellations","Perspective drawing and vanishing points","Ratios and frequency in musical scales","Fractals and self-similarity patterns","Fine-arts project applying geometric modeling"]},{"unit":"Probability & Finite Sample Spaces","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.43(c)(8)","111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Sample spaces and the fundamental counting principle","Permutations and combinations","Theoretical vs. experimental probability","Independent, dependent, and mutually exclusive events","Conditional probability and two-way tables","Expected value and fair-game / decision analysis"]},{"unit":"Analyzing Data in the Social Sciences","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.43(c)(9)","111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Univariate displays: histograms, box plots, center and spread","Scatterplots and describing bivariate association","Correlation coefficient and correlation vs. causation","Linear regression / line of best fit and prediction","Analyzing data from polls, censuses, and surveys","Interpolation, extrapolation, and model limitations"]},{"unit":"Designing & Communicating a Statistical Study","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.43(c)(10)","111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Formulating a research question and identifying variables","Sampling methods and sources of bias","Observational studies vs. experiments; survey design","Collecting, organizing, and cleaning data","Choosing appropriate graphical and numerical summaries","Communicating findings in a written and oral report"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["111.43(c)(1)"],"items":["Flex days to absorb calendar shifts and holidays","Targeted small-group reteach on flagged standards","Make-up assessments and retakes","Skills spiral: process standards and problem solving","Catch-up on outstanding project work"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Project Presentations","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["111.43(c)(1)","111.43(c)(9)","111.43(c)(10)"],"items":["Cumulative review across all modeling contexts","Capstone modeling project synthesis","Final study: design, collect, analyze, and model","Peer review and presentation of findings","Final exam / portfolio defense","Course reflection and real-world modeling showcase"]}]},{"course":"Advanced Quantitative Reasoning","tacSection":"19 TAC §111.44","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["http://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter111_sec.111.44","https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/texas/19-Tex-Admin-Code-SS-111-44","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-111"],"strands":[{"code":"111.44(c)(1)","statement":"Mathematical process standards. The student uses mathematical processes to acquire and demonstrate mathematical understanding. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"apply mathematics to problems arising in everyday life, society, and the workplace;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"use a problem-solving model that incorporates analyzing given information, formulating a plan or strategy, determining a solution, justifying the solution, and evaluating the problem-solving process and the reasonableness of the solution;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"select tools, including real objects, manipulatives, paper and pencil, and technology as appropriate, and techniques, including mental math, estimation, and number sense as appropriate, to solve problems;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"communicate mathematical ideas, reasoning, and their implications using multiple representations, including symbols, diagrams, graphs, and language as appropriate;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"create and use representations to organize, record, and communicate mathematical ideas;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"analyze mathematical relationships to connect and communicate mathematical ideas; and"},{"code":"(G)","text":"display, explain, and justify mathematical ideas and arguments using precise mathematical language in written or oral communication."}]},{"code":"111.44(c)(2)","statement":"Numeric reasoning. The student applies the process standards in mathematics to generate new understandings by extending existing knowledge. The student generates new mathematical understandings through problems involving numerical data that arise in everyday life, society, and the workplace. The student extends existing knowledge and skills to analyze real-world situations. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use precision and accuracy in real-life situations related to measurement and significant figures;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"apply and analyze published ratings, weighted averages, and indices to make informed decisions;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"solve problems involving quantities that are not easily measured using proportionality;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"solve geometric problems involving indirect measurement, including similar triangles, the Pythagorean Theorem, Law of Sines, Law of Cosines, and the use of dynamic geometry software;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"solve problems involving large quantities using combinatorics;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"use arrays to efficiently manage large collections of data and add, subtract, and multiply matrices to solve applied problems, including geometric transformations;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"analyze various voting and selection processes to compare results in given situations; and"},{"code":"(H)","text":"select and apply an algorithm of interest to solve real-life problems such as problems using recursion or iteration involving population growth or decline, fractals, and compound interest; the validity in recorded and transmitted data using checksums and hashing; sports rankings, weighted class rankings, and search engine rankings; and problems involving scheduling or routing situations using vertex-edge graphs, critical paths, Euler paths, and minimal spanning trees and communicate to peers the application of the algorithm in precise mathematical and nontechnical language."}]},{"code":"111.44(c)(3)","statement":"Algebraic reasoning (expressions, equations, and generalized relationships). The student applies the process standards in mathematics to create and analyze mathematical models of everyday situations to make informed decisions related to earning, investing, spending, and borrowing money by appropriate, proficient, and efficient use of tools, including technology. The student uses mathematical relationships to make connections and predictions. The student judges the validity of a prediction and uses mathematical models to represent, analyze, and solve dynamic real-world problems. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"collect numerical bivariate data to create a scatterplot, select a function to model the data, justify the model selection, and use the model to interpret results and make predictions;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"describe the degree to which uncorrelated variables may or may not be related and analyze situations where correlated variables do or do not indicate a cause-and-effect relationship;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"determine or analyze an appropriate growth or decay model for problem situations, including linear, exponential, and logistic functions;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"determine or analyze an appropriate cyclical model for problem situations that can be modeled with periodic functions;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"determine or analyze an appropriate piecewise model for problem situations;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"create, represent, and analyze mathematical models for various types of income calculations to determine the best option for a given situation;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"create, represent, and analyze mathematical models for expenditures, including those involving credit, to determine the best option for a given situation; and"},{"code":"(H)","text":"create, represent, and analyze mathematical models and appropriate representations, including formulas and amortization tables, for various types of loans and investments to determine the best option for a given situation."}]},{"code":"111.44(c)(4)","statement":"Probabilistic and statistical reasoning. The student uses the process standards in mathematics to generate new understandings of probability and statistics. The student analyzes statistical information and evaluates risk and return to connect mathematical ideas and make informed decisions. The student applies a problem-solving model and statistical methods to design and conduct a study that addresses one or more particular question(s). The student uses multiple representations to communicate effectively the results of student-generated statistical studies and the critical analysis of published statistical studies. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"use a two-way frequency table as a sample space to identify whether two events are independent and to interpret the results;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"use the Addition Rule, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B), in mathematical and real-world problems;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"calculate conditional probabilities and probabilities of compound events using tree diagrams, Venn diagrams, area models, and formulas;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"interpret conditional probabilities and probabilities of compound events by analyzing representations to make decisions in problem situations;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"use probabilities to make and justify decisions about risks in everyday life;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"calculate expected value to analyze mathematical fairness, payoff, and risk;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"determine the validity of logical arguments that include compound conditional statements by constructing truth tables;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"identify limitations and lack of relevant information in studies reporting statistical information, especially when studies are reported in condensed form;"},{"code":"(I)","text":"interpret and compare statistical results using appropriate technology given a margin of error;"},{"code":"(J)","text":"identify potential misuses of statistics to justify particular conclusions, including assertions of a cause-and-effect relationship rather than an association, and missteps or fallacies in logical reasoning;"},{"code":"(K)","text":"describe strengths and weaknesses of sampling techniques, data and graphical displays, and interpretations of summary statistics and other results appearing in a study, including reports published in the media;"},{"code":"(L)","text":"determine the need for and purpose of a statistical investigation and what type of statistical analysis can be used to answer a specific question or set of questions;"},{"code":"(M)","text":"identify the population of interest for a statistical investigation, select an appropriate sampling technique, and collect data;"},{"code":"(N)","text":"identify the variables to be used in a study;"},{"code":"(O)","text":"determine possible sources of statistical bias in a study and how bias may affect the validity of the results;"},{"code":"(P)","text":"create data displays for given data sets to investigate, compare, and estimate center, shape, spread, and unusual features of the data;"},{"code":"(Q)","text":"analyze possible sources of data variability, including those that can be controlled and those that cannot be controlled;"},{"code":"(R)","text":"report results of statistical studies to a particular audience, including selecting an appropriate presentation format, creating graphical data displays, and interpreting results in terms of the question studied;"},{"code":"(S)","text":"justify the design and the conclusion(s) of statistical studies, including the methods used; and"},{"code":"(T)","text":"communicate statistical results in oral and written formats using appropriate statistical and nontechnical language."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Launch: Quantitative Reasoning & the Problem-Solving Toolkit","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.44(c)(1)","111.44(c)(2)"],"items":["Course intro: what quantitative reasoning is and where it shows up in daily life, society, and the workplace","Establish the problem-solving model: analyze, plan, solve, justify, evaluate reasonableness","Precision, units, and significant figures in real measurements","Unit analysis / dimensional analysis to convert and check quantities","Estimation and Fermi problems: order-of-magnitude reasoning","Communicating solutions: labeled work, justification, and appropriate technology (spreadsheets, calculators)"]},{"unit":"Numeric Reasoning I: Ratios, Rates & Proportional Situations","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.44(c)(2)","111.44(c)(1)"],"items":["Ratios, rates, and unit rates in real contexts (pricing, speed, density)","Proportional reasoning and scaling recipes, maps, and models","Percent, percent change, and percent error applications","Scale factors, similar figures, and indirect measurement","Fuel economy, dosage, and workplace rate problems","Process check-in: justifying each proportional model chosen"]},{"unit":"Numeric Reasoning II: Big Numbers, Indices & Data Literacy","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.44(c)(2)","111.44(c)(4)"],"items":["Very large and very small numbers: scientific notation in science and finance","Building and interpreting index numbers (CPI, cost-of-living, price indices)","Weighted averages and composite measures (GPA, ratings)","Reading numeric claims critically in news and advertising","Rates of change from tables: absolute vs relative change","Communicating numeric findings with clear representations"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (GP1-2 wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["111.44(c)(1)","111.44(c)(2)"],"items":["Reteach proportional and percent trouble spots from formative data","Spiral review of unit analysis and estimation","Skills quiz and error analysis on numeric reasoning","Catch-up day for calendar shifts, assemblies, or short weeks","Reflection and goal-setting for the next grading period"]},{"unit":"Algebraic Reasoning I: Modeling with Linear & Piecewise Functions","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.44(c)(3)","111.44(c)(1)"],"items":["Building linear models from verbal, tabular, and graphical situations","Slope as a rate of change and intercepts as real quantities","Piecewise models: tax brackets, shipping tiers, utility pricing","Systems of equations for break-even and comparison decisions","Interpreting and judging validity of linear predictions and extrapolation","Using technology to fit and analyze linear models"]},{"unit":"Algebraic Reasoning II: The Mathematics of Earning, Spending & Borrowing","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.44(c)(3)","111.44(c)(2)"],"items":["Wages, salary, commission, deductions, and take-home pay","Budgeting models and needs-vs-wants spending decisions","Simple interest and comparing loan/credit-card costs","Exponential models: compound interest and account growth","Depreciation and appreciation of assets over time","Comparing financial options and defending a recommendation"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Cumulative Checkpoint","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["111.44(c)(1)","111.44(c)(2)","111.44(c)(3)"],"items":["Spiral review across numeric and algebraic reasoning strands","Mixed real-world problem set with justification writing","Cumulative checkpoint assessment and item analysis","Reteach targeted gaps flagged by the checkpoint","Semester reflection and quantitative-reasoning portfolio update"]},{"unit":"Algebraic Reasoning III: Investing, Annuities & Long-Term Planning","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.44(c)(3)","111.44(c)(4)"],"items":["Saving vs investing: risk, return, and time horizon","Future value of periodic savings and annuity models","Amortization: mortgages, car loans, and payment schedules","Retirement and college-savings planning scenarios","Inflation-adjusted (real) vs nominal dollars in decisions","Building a spreadsheet model to compare long-term plans"]},{"unit":"Statistical Reasoning I: Designing Studies & Summarizing Data","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.44(c)(4)","111.44(c)(1)"],"items":["Populations, samples, and sources of bias in study design","Designing and conducting a student-generated survey or study","Center, spread, and shape: mean, median, IQR, standard deviation","Displays: histograms, boxplots, and choosing an honest graph","Comparing distributions across groups","Communicating study results with multiple representations"]},{"unit":"Statistical Reasoning II: Probability, Risk & Return","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.44(c)(4)","111.44(c)(2)"],"items":["Probability basics, complements, and compound events","Two-way tables, conditional probability, and independence","Expected value in games, insurance, and everyday choices","Evaluating risk and return in financial and health decisions","Simulation to estimate probabilities of real events","Interpreting probabilistic claims responsibly"]},{"unit":"Statistical Reasoning III: Critiquing Published Studies & Correlation","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.44(c)(4)","111.44(c)(3)"],"items":["Scatterplots, correlation, and lines of best fit as models","Correlation vs causation and lurking variables","Interpreting margin of error and confidence in polls","Critical analysis of published statistical studies and media claims","Judging the validity of predictions from statistical models","Presenting a critique with evidence and clear visuals"]},{"unit":"Capstone Project: Data-Driven Decision Report","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.44(c)(1)","111.44(c)(4)","111.44(c)(3)"],"items":["Choose a real question and plan a mixed numeric/algebraic/statistical investigation","Collect or source data and build appropriate models","Analyze results, quantify uncertainty, and evaluate reasonableness","Draft a decision report with multiple representations","Peer review and revision using a rubric","Present findings and defend the recommendation"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Exam","weeks":2,"kind":"review","teks":["111.44(c)(1)","111.44(c)(2)","111.44(c)(3)","111.44(c)(4)"],"items":["Cumulative review across all four reasoning strands","Mixed spiral problem sets emphasizing process standards","Final exam review stations and practice","Final exam administration","Portfolio finalization and course reflection","Buffer day for end-of-year calendar shifts"]}]},{"course":"Statistics","tacSection":"19 TAC §111.47","hasStaarEOC":false,"sources":["http://txrules.elaws.us/rule/title19_chapter111_sec.111.47","https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/texas-administrative-code/19-tac-chapter-111"],"strands":[{"code":"111.47(c)(1)","statement":"Mathematical process standards. The student uses mathematical processes to acquire and demonstrate mathematical understanding. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"apply mathematics to problems arising in everyday life, society, and the workplace;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"use a problem-solving model that incorporates analyzing given information, formulating a plan or strategy, determining a solution, justifying the solution, and evaluating the problem-solving process and the reasonableness of the solution;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"select tools, including real objects, manipulatives, paper and pencil, and technology as appropriate, and techniques, including mental math, estimation, and number sense as appropriate, to solve problems;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"communicate mathematical ideas, reasoning, and their implications using multiple representations, including symbols, diagrams, graphs, and language as appropriate;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"create and use representations to organize, record, and communicate mathematical ideas;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"analyze mathematical relationships to connect and communicate mathematical ideas; and"},{"code":"(G)","text":"display, explain, or justify mathematical ideas and arguments using precise mathematical language in written or oral communication."}]},{"code":"111.47(c)(2)","statement":"Statistical process sampling and experimentation. The student applies mathematical processes to apply understandings about statistical studies, surveys, and experiments to design and conduct a study and use graphical, numerical, and analytical techniques to communicate the results of the study. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"compare and contrast the benefits of different sampling techniques, including random sampling and convenience sampling methods;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"distinguish among observational studies, surveys, and experiments;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"analyze generalizations made from observational studies, surveys, and experiments;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"distinguish between sample statistics and population parameters;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"formulate a meaningful question, determine the data needed to answer the question, gather the appropriate data, analyze the data, and draw reasonable conclusions;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"communicate methods used, analyses conducted, and conclusions drawn for a data-analysis project through the use of one or more of the following: a written report, a visual display, an oral report, or a multi-media presentation; and"},{"code":"(G)","text":"critically analyze published findings for appropriateness of study design implemented, sampling methods used, or the statistics applied."}]},{"code":"111.47(c)(3)","statement":"Variability. The student applies the mathematical process standards when describing and modeling variability. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"distinguish between mathematical models and statistical models;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"construct a statistical model to describe variability around the structure of a mathematical model for a given situation;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"distinguish among different sources of variability, including measurement, natural, induced, and sampling variability; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"describe and model variability using population and sampling distributions."}]},{"code":"111.47(c)(4)","statement":"Categorical and quantitative data. The student applies the mathematical process standards to represent and analyze both categorical and quantitative data. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"distinguish between categorical and quantitative data;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"represent and summarize data and justify the representation;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"analyze the distribution characteristics of quantitative data, including determining the possible existence and impact of outliers;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"compare and contrast different graphical or visual representations given the same data set;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"compare and contrast meaningful information derived from summary statistics given a data set; and"},{"code":"(F)","text":"analyze categorical data, including determining marginal and conditional distributions, using two-way tables."}]},{"code":"111.47(c)(5)","statement":"Probability and random variables. The student applies the mathematical process standards to connect probability and statistics. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"determine probabilities, including the use of a two-way table;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"describe the relationship between theoretical and empirical probabilities using the Law of Large Numbers;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"construct a distribution based on a technology-generated simulation or collected samples for a discrete random variable; and"},{"code":"(D)","text":"compare statistical measures such as sample mean and standard deviation from a technology-simulated sampling distribution to the theoretical sampling distribution."}]},{"code":"111.47(c)(6)","statement":"Inference. The student applies the mathematical process standards to make inferences and justify conclusions from statistical studies. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"explain how a sample statistic and a confidence level are used in the construction of a confidence interval;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"explain how changes in the sample size, confidence level, and standard deviation affect the margin of error of a confidence interval;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"calculate a confidence interval for the mean of a normally distributed population with a known standard deviation;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"calculate a confidence interval for a population proportion;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"interpret confidence intervals for a population parameter, including confidence intervals from media or statistical reports;"},{"code":"(F)","text":"explain how a sample statistic provides evidence against a claim about a population parameter when using a hypothesis test;"},{"code":"(G)","text":"construct null and alternative hypothesis statements about a population parameter;"},{"code":"(H)","text":"explain the meaning of the p-value in relation to the significance level in providing evidence to reject or fail to reject the null hypothesis in the context of the situation;"},{"code":"(I)","text":"interpret the results of a hypothesis test using technology-generated results such as large sample tests for proportion, mean, difference between two proportions, and difference between two independent means; and"},{"code":"(J)","text":"describe the potential impact of Type I and Type II Errors."}]},{"code":"111.47(c)(7)","statement":"Bivariate data. The student applies the mathematical process standards to analyze relationships among bivariate quantitative data. The student is expected to:","expectations":[{"code":"(A)","text":"analyze scatterplots for patterns, linearity, outliers, and influential points;"},{"code":"(B)","text":"transform a linear parent function to determine a line of best fit;"},{"code":"(C)","text":"compare different linear models for the same set of data to determine best fit, including discussions about error;"},{"code":"(D)","text":"compare different methods for determining best fit, including median-median and absolute value;"},{"code":"(E)","text":"describe the relationship between influential points and lines of best fit using dynamic graphing technology; and"},{"code":"(F)","text":"identify and interpret the reasonableness of attributes of lines of best fit within the context, including slope and y-intercept."}]}],"sequence":[{"unit":"Foundations: Statistical Reasoning & the Data Cycle","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(1)","111.47(c)(2)"],"items":["Define statistics; distinguish population vs. sample and parameter vs. statistic","The statistical problem-solving cycle: formulate question, collect, analyze, interpret","Types of data: categorical vs. quantitative; discrete vs. continuous","Levels of measurement (nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio)","Process standards launch: communicate reasoning with tables, symbols, and appropriate math language","Read and critique claims in real news/graphs for statistical accuracy"]},{"unit":"Designing Studies: Sampling & Data Collection","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(2)","111.47(c)(1)"],"items":["Observational studies vs. experiments vs. surveys","Sampling methods: simple random, stratified, cluster, systematic, convenience","Sources of bias: undercoverage, nonresponse, response, wording bias","Simulate random sampling with technology and random-number tables","Write clear survey questions and evaluate sampling plans"]},{"unit":"Designing Experiments","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(2)","111.47(c)(1)"],"items":["Explanatory vs. response variables; confounding and lurking variables","Principles of design: control, randomization, replication, blocking","Completely randomized, randomized block, and matched-pairs designs","Placebo effect, blinding, and double-blind experiments","Design a class experiment and describe how results can be generalized"]},{"unit":"Displaying Categorical & Quantitative Data","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(4)","111.47(c)(1)"],"items":["Frequency and relative-frequency tables; bar graphs and pie charts","Dotplots, stemplots, and histograms; choosing bin widths","Describing distributions: shape, center, spread, outliers, gaps, clusters","Comparing distributions across groups with parallel displays","Identify misleading graphs and construct honest displays with technology"]},{"unit":"Summarizing Quantitative Data: Measures of Center & Spread","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(4)","111.47(c)(3)"],"items":["Mean, median, mode; resistance and effect of outliers/skew","Range, IQR, variance, and standard deviation by hand and technology","Five-number summary and boxplots; identifying outliers with the 1.5*IQR rule","Percentiles and z-scores as measures of relative standing","Choosing appropriate summaries based on distribution shape"]},{"unit":"Modeling Variability & the Normal Distribution","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(3)","111.47(c)(1)"],"items":["Describing and quantifying variability in real data sets","Density curves and the 68-95-99.7 (empirical) rule","Standard normal distribution and z-score calculations","Finding areas, proportions, and percentiles using the normal model","Assessing normality with normal probability plots"]},{"unit":"Mid-Year Review & Cumulative Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"review","teks":["111.47(c)(1)","111.47(c)(2)","111.47(c)(3)","111.47(c)(4)"],"items":["Spiral review: study design, sampling, and bias","Spiral review: graphical displays and distribution description","Spiral review: center, spread, z-scores, and the normal model","Mixed free-response practice with justification of reasoning","Cumulative semester assessment and error analysis"]},{"unit":"Probability Foundations","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(5)","111.47(c)(1)"],"items":["Sample spaces, events, and the Law of Large Numbers via simulation","Basic rules: complement, addition rule, mutually exclusive events","Conditional probability and the general multiplication rule","Independence: testing and interpreting independent events","Two-way tables, tree diagrams, and Venn diagrams for probability"]},{"unit":"Random Variables & Probability Distributions","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(5)","111.47(c)(3)"],"items":["Discrete random variables: probability distributions and expected value","Variance and standard deviation of a discrete random variable","Combining random variables: means and variances of sums/differences","Binomial setting, formula, mean, and standard deviation","Geometric distributions and interpreting probability in context"]},{"unit":"Sampling Distributions","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(5)","111.47(c)(6)"],"items":["Concept of a sampling distribution; statistic vs. parameter revisited","Sampling distribution of a sample proportion; bias and variability","Sampling distribution of a sample mean","The Central Limit Theorem and its conditions","Simulating sampling distributions to connect probability and inference"]},{"unit":"Buffer / Reteach / Assessment (wiggle)","weeks":1,"kind":"buffer","teks":["111.47(c)(5)","111.47(c)(1)"],"items":["Flex time to absorb calendar shifts and holidays","Reteach probability and sampling-distribution trouble spots","Small-group problem clinics and targeted retakes","Technology skills catch-up (calculator/applet fluency)","Checkpoint quiz on random variables and sampling distributions"]},{"unit":"Confidence Intervals: Estimating with Confidence","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(6)","111.47(c)(1)"],"items":["Logic of interval estimation: point estimate plus/minus margin of error","Confidence interval for a population proportion; checking conditions","Confidence interval for a population mean using the t-distribution","Interpreting confidence level and margin of error correctly","Determining sample size for a desired margin of error"]},{"unit":"Significance Testing: Making Inferences","weeks":3,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(6)","111.47(c)(1)"],"items":["Null vs. alternative hypotheses and the logic of significance tests","P-values, significance level, and Type I/Type II errors and power","Significance test for a population proportion (one-sample z-test)","Significance test for a population mean (one-sample t-test)","Tests comparing two proportions and two means","Justifying conclusions in context and connecting tests to intervals"]},{"unit":"Bivariate Data: Scatterplots & Correlation","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(7)","111.47(c)(4)"],"items":["Scatterplots: describing direction, form, strength, and outliers","Correlation coefficient r: calculation, interpretation, and properties","Correlation vs. causation and the danger of lurking variables","Distinguishing association from cause in observational data","Using technology to compute and interpret r"]},{"unit":"Bivariate Data: Least-Squares Regression","weeks":2,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(7)","111.47(c)(1)"],"items":["Least-squares regression line: slope and intercept in context","Making predictions; interpolation vs. extrapolation","Residuals, residual plots, and assessing linear fit","Coefficient of determination r-squared and standard deviation of residuals","Transforming nonlinear data to achieve linearity"]},{"unit":"Categorical Inference: Chi-Square Tests","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(6)","111.47(c)(4)"],"items":["Chi-square goodness-of-fit test for one categorical variable","Chi-square test for homogeneity across groups","Chi-square test for independence with two-way tables","Checking conditions and interpreting expected counts","Drawing and communicating conclusions in context"]},{"unit":"Capstone Statistical Project","weeks":1,"kind":"teach","teks":["111.47(c)(2)","111.47(c)(6)"],"items":["Formulate a research question and design a sampling or experimental plan","Collect, organize, and clean real data","Analyze with appropriate graphical and numerical techniques","Apply an inference procedure to justify a conclusion","Present findings and communicate results to an audience"]},{"unit":"End-of-Year Review & Final Exam (wiggle)","weeks":2,"kind":"assessment","teks":["111.47(c)(1)","111.47(c)(6)","111.47(c)(7)"],"items":["Comprehensive review: study design and data analysis","Comprehensive review: probability and sampling distributions","Comprehensive review: confidence intervals and significance tests","Comprehensive review: bivariate data and regression","Mixed cumulative free-response and multiple-choice practice","Final examination and reflection on statistical thinking"]}]}],"verifyNotes":["MERGE: Algebra I appeared in two source records (base TEKS + STAAR-tagged). They were deduplicated into one strand set. Verbatim text is from the base 19 TAC 111.39 record (cleaner notation); STAAR Readiness/Supporting tags were applied from the TEA STAAR Algebra I Assessed Curriculum (Jan 2014). The two sources' expectation text matched except for cosmetic differences (en-dash vs hyphen in 'y - y1', 'x- or y-axis' vs 'X or Y axis') - base-TEKS wording was kept.","STAAR tag count reconciliation: the STAAR source notes first stated '11 total' Readiness then self-corrected and enumerated 16. The correct count is 16 Readiness standards: A.2A, A.2C, A.2I, A.3B, A.3C, A.3D, A.5A, A.5C, A.6A, A.7A, A.7C, A.8A, A.9C, A.9D, A.10E, A.11B. All other A.2-A.12 expectations are tagged Supporting. A.1A-A.1G (process standards) carry NO staar tag by design - per TEA they are embedded across all reporting categories, not separately designated. STAAR reporting categories (RC1-RC5) are appended to each Algebra I strand statement in brackets for reference.","COMPLETENESS: All five math-course TEKS (Algebra I, Geometry, Algebra II, Precalculus) and all four CTE/elective TEKS (Principles of Applied Engineering, Communication Applications, Career and College Exploration, Principles of Information Technology) are COMPLETE - every strand and lettered expectation present and verbatim. No course has missing/truncated TEKS strands.","PACING GAP - Algebra I: A.4A (correlation coefficient) and A.4B (association vs causation) are not assigned to any pacing unit in the Fort Bend ISD sequence. The TEKS strand A.4 is complete; only the pacing coverage is missing these two SEs. Teacher should slot them in (logically alongside A.4C in Unit 2, scatterplots/line-of-best-fit).","PACING GAP - Precalculus: P.5E (nth term / nth partial sum / infinite sum of a geometric series) is not assigned to any pacing unit. Unit 10 (Sequences and Series) covers P.5A,B,C,D,F but omits P.5E. The TEKS strand P.5 is complete; teacher should add P.5E to Unit 10.","PACING NOTE - Algebra II: Unit 3 lists 2A.7A, but in the TEKS '2A.7A' = 'add, subtract, and multiply complex numbers' (strand 2A.7), whereas Unit 3 is about quadratics. This is likely a district label for 'write the quadratic function given three points' which is the actual 2A.4A. Treat the pacing code as the district's, not a TEKS contradiction; verify intended SE.","COURSES WITHOUT PACING: Principles of Applied Engineering, Communication Applications, Career and College Exploration, and Principles of Information Technology have empty 'sequence' arrays - no pacing/scope-and-sequence was supplied for them. Teacher must source or build a unit sequence for these four.","COURSE-IDENTITY FLAGS: (1) Communication Applications is in 19 TAC Chapter 110 (English Language Arts), NOT a CTE chapter - it is the speech graduation elective. (2) 'Career and College Exploration' (19 TAC 127.2) is a MIDDLE-SCHOOL (Grades 7-8) course, used here as the closest authentic TEKS analog because no standalone HS 'College and Career Readiness'/AVID TEKS course exists (AVID is proprietary; CCRS is a non-TEKS framework). Verify intended course before classroom use. (3) Principles of Applied Engineering uses current 19 TAC 127.781 (2021); the older 130.402 citation is repealed. (4) Principles of Information Technology uses 19 TAC 130.302 (2017), migrating to Ch 127 under the 2025 SBOE relocation.","CODE NORMALIZATION: Math expectation codes use standard TEA shorthand (e.g., A.2B, 2A.4D, P.5E) so they match the pacing TEKS references exactly. CTE/elective courses had bare letter codes (A, B, C...) in source; these were prefixed with a course-strand identifier (PAE.x, CA.x, CCE.x, POIT.x) for embeddability and to avoid collisions. Original TAC subsection numbering ((c)(1), (d)(1), (b)(1)) is preserved at the start of each CTE strand statement.","NOTATION: Math notation was flattened to plain text/Unicode in the sources (e.g., 'x2' = x squared, 'r2' = r squared, 'y - y1 = m(x - x1)' uses subscript 1, sqrt/cbrt rendered as the radical glyphs, '-inf, inf' as interval bounds). Meaning is preserved verbatim; only typographic rendering of exponents/subscripts/radicals is approximate. A scrape artifact in the STAAR source ('the X or Y axis') was normalized to the standard-TEKS 'X or Y axis' wording for A.2G.","DATASET SHAPE for embedding: top-level 'courses' array; each course has course, completeness (human-readable flag string), hasStaarEOC (boolean: true only for Algebra I), strands[] (code, statement, expectations[] with code/text/optional staar), and sequence[] (unit, weeks, teks[]). Empty sequence[] = no pacing supplied. The 'staar' field appears ONLY on Algebra I assessed expectations."]};
